{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh32wXRuV3muC7mP85E1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C2fUK1d4hNajwAxYpPjRyo","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/work/PLAN.md","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7340,"cache_read_input_tokens":15016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7340},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92d1ba00-1a0f-4edd-bbd5-53c74c3ffc62","uuid":"1e97c272-2e2c-4387-b0c9-0d73be28b226","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:40:20.323Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh32wFK69mpVFME72jXe","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01C2fUK1d4hNajwAxYpPjRyo":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C2fUK1d4hNajwAxYpPjRyo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Karate Champon\n2\t\n3\t**Build plan for an original point-sparring karate game inspired by *Karate Champ* (1984).**\n4\t\n5\t> **Title:** *Karate Champon* is a riff on *Karate Champ*. Champon (ちゃんぽん) is Nagasaki's mixed noodle dish, and also Japanese slang for \"a mix of everything\". The title was picked as TASK.md asks: a silent brainstorm of 50 riffs on the source's name, then one pick with the OS's cryptographic RNG (`python3 -I` running `secrets.randbelow(50)`). It returned **#20 of 50: \"Karate Champon\"**. Use this title everywhere: the title screen, `<title>`, file names and the AI Hub catalogue.\n6\t\n7\tThis plan is for the developers (possibly other AI models) who will build the game from **TASK.md + this file alone**. Read the whole plan once. Section 15 (architecture) and section 16 (build order) are the working reference. Section 19 maps every TASK.md requirement to the section that covers it and the test that proves it.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. At a glance\n12\t\n13\t| | |\n14\t|---|---|\n15\t| **One-line pitch** | Bow. Begin. One clean strike. A referee stops the fight after every point. Climb from white belt to Champ with two sticks and no buttons. |\n16\t| **Genre** | 1-on-1 tournament karate fighting (point sparring), with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill |\n17\t| **Perspective** | 2D side view (the engine's `'side'`, pitch 0) on a single-screen arena. An optional \"Tatami 2.5D\" camera uses the engine's `'brawler'` view (pitch 25). |\n18\t| **Players** | 1 (story vs CPU), 2 (local versus), 3–4 (local *Kachinuki*: winner stays on), 2–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n19\t| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (full engine, readable), extended (section 15.4) |\n20\t| **Deliverables (in this folder)** | `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743.html` (the single-file game), `exit-interview.json`, and the dev folder `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743/` |\n21\t| **Demo length** | Full story ≈ 35–40 min (9 matches, 3 bonus stages, 3 drill lessons, cutscenes). Every mode can also be reached directly from the main menu. |\n22\t| **Look** | The engine's native HD rigs drawn as pixel art at 320×240 (`res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`): PS1-era 2D, with white-gi vs red-gi fighters on scenic Japanese stages |\n23\t\n24\tFile-name rule: GameTitle = `KarateChampon` and SOURCE = `KarateChamp1984` (TASK.md's \"SOURCE: Karate Champ 1984\" with the spaces removed). Do not invent other names.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## 1. The source, and what we keep\n29\t\n30\t### 1.1 Research summary (what was confirmed)\n31\t- *Karate Champ* (arcade, 1984; Japanese title *Karate Dō*). Data East published it. Shacknews credits Technōs Japan with the development; Wikipedia credits Data East. It is widely called the first one-on-one fighting game. The **Player vs Player** edition (1984) added head-to-head play. Its Japanese subtitle is *Bishōjo Seishun Hen*: two players compete over a girl across 12 locations.\n32\t- **No health bars.** A landed technique ends the exchange. The hitter earns a **half point or a full point**, and **the first to two points wins**. Each technique has a point value. TASVideos #5529 lists, for example, Round Kick 300–600, Jumping Side Kick 500–1000 and Front Kick 100–200. A value of 500 or less is a half point and 600 or more is a full point. A technique that lands while the opponent is mid-technique **scores double**.\n33\t- **The clock starts at 30**, ticks about once a second, and stops while play is stopped. It resumes when the judge says \"begin\". If time runs out, **the judge decides**. The judge's digitized voice calls \"Begin\", \"Stop\", \"Half point\", \"Full point\" and the side's colour (Japanese in the original release).\n34\t- **Controls: two 4-way joysticks and no buttons.** The left stick sets movement and posture, the right stick the technique. Sources count 17–24 moves: front kick, reverse punch, squatting reverse lunge punch, jumping side kick, low kick, round kick, back round kick, turn back kick, front and back foot sweeps, back kick, middle and upper lunge punches, upper punch, jumping back kick, block (its height set by the incoming technique; it cannot stop the low kick or the sweeps), crouch, jump, and front and back somersaults.\n35\t- **Ranks run from Beginner through 1st–10th Dan to Champ.** Dojo matches come first, then a national championship.\n36\t- **Bonus stages:** *Evade* (thrown bonsai pots and boulders, 200 points per object broken, ending when you are hit), *Trial Chop* (board breaking with a \"Perfect\"), and *Get the Bull* (hit the charging bull: 3,000 points; only certain techniques work). A **practice** stage has a senpai demonstrate techniques that you copy (200 points each).\n37\t- Player vs Player locations included a harbor, a Mount Fuji garden, a bamboo forest, a tree trunk over a chasm, a ship's stern, a dojo and a night path.\n38\t- Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Champ; tasvideos.org/5529S (technique table, timer, bonus stages, ranks); shacknews.com \"Shack's Arcade Corner: Karate Champ\"; arcade-history.com/game/1261. StrategyWiki, Giant Bomb and TV Tropes refused automated fetches. Any fact not listed here is **unknown**.\n39\t\n40\t### 1.2 Design pillars (source trait → our mechanic)\n41\t1. **One clean strike decides.** Point sparring, with a referee who stops the fight after every point. Fighters reset to their marks, and the score is shown as point icons, not a health bar.\n42\t2. **Two sticks, no buttons.** A posture stick and a technique stick give 15 scoring techniques, plus block, jump, crouch, two somersaults and a roll. The DualSense's twin sticks map onto this naturally. The face buttons and IJKL act as a 4-way \"technique stick\" too.\n43\t3. **The referee is a character.** He makes the calls, raises the flags, and the corner judges decide at time-out. His voice is gibberish, with subtitles.\n44\t4. **Form is scored.** A clean technique at full reach, struck early in its active frames, scores more. A counter scores ×2. A beginner who flails earns half points, and a sharp player earns full points.\n45\t5. **The climb.** You go from Beginner (white belt) to Champ. The belt colour on the gi changes as you rise through scenic stages.\n46\t6. **Bonus stages** break up the tension: boards, flying pots, a charging bull.\n47\t7. **The senpai's drill.** You learn by mimicking. It doubles as the tutorial.\n48\t8. **White vs red.** Head-to-head versus works locally and online. Online uses *Kachinuki* (winner stays on), a real karate tournament format.\n49\t9. **The arcade frame.** Numeric score, top-ten initials, a 10-second continue countdown and an attract mode.\n50\t\n51\t### 1.3 Narrative tone\n52\tCopy the source's style: **terse, ALL-CAPS arcade calls, earnest**, with no long monologues. Each beat gets one or two lines, at most about 40 characters per line. The comedy comes only from the cast's champon-ingredient names and from Mei's dry remarks. Keep it warm, never mocking. All text is in Appendix A. The Player vs Player edition's girl-as-prize trope becomes **Mei**, the noodle-shop daughter who heckles and feeds the hero, and who turns out to be the reigning champion and the final opponent. The prize is the **Golden Bowl** trophy, not a person.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 2. Game overview\n57\t\n58\t### 2.1 Core loop (write this sentence as a comment at the top of the game code)\n59\t*Read your opponent from your mark. Commit to one technique with two sticks. Land it clean before they land theirs. The referee stops the fight, scores it and resets. First to 2 points takes the round, best of 3 takes the match. Win to earn a belt and the next stage, with a bonus stage after every second win.*\n60\t\n61\t### 2.2 Modes (main menu)\n62\t1. **CHAMPON ROAD** (story/arcade): the ladder in §7.3. Score, continues and top-ten initials. Autosaves after each node, and CONTINUE appears on the title.\n63\t2. **VERSUS**: P1 (white) vs P2 (red, a human or a CPU at level 1–10). Choose fighters, stage and rules. With 3–4 joined players, the **KACHINUKI** variant starts (winner stays on).\n64\t3. **DOJO** (practice): Lessons I/II/III, All Techniques (21 drills), Free Spar against a dummy (stand, crouch, jump, block, attack), and the Move List.\n65\t4. **BONUS GAMES**: Boards, Pots Ahoy!, Stop the Bull, each with its own high-score table and a difficulty choice.\n66\t5. **RECORDS**: top-ten tables (Story, Boards, Pots, Bull).\n67\t6. **OPTIONS**: the engine's standard settings plus the game's own (§8.10).\n68\t7. **ABOUT**: Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt feedback, and credits (§19).\n69\t- **Online:** when the AI Hub opens the page, the game skips all of the above and goes straight into the session (§12).\n70\t\n71\t### 2.3 Story synopsis\n72\tNagasaki Harbor, spring 1984. The Champon Cup returns, and its prize is the Golden Bowl. Taro Moyashi (\"bean sprout\", slang for a skinny weakling) is a white belt at Senpai Negi's dojo. He trains, beats his classmates and tours the town's fight spots: the harbor, the bamboo forest, the log bridge, a ship's stern, the night market and the Fuji garden. Between stops he breaks boards, smashes flying pots and stops a bull. Mei runs the noodle cart and the shop *Champon-tei*; she heckles him, feeds him and cheers from every crowd. At the National Stadium final, Mei enters in a red gi with a gold belt: she is the reigning champion, \"The Iron Ladle\". Beat her and you become THE KARATE CHAMPON. \"Next year I take it back.\"\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 3. Controls and UX\n77\t\n78\t### 3.1 The twin-stick technique system (the heart of the game)\n79\t- **Left stick = posture** (where your body goes): Neutral, Toward the foe, Away, Up (jump), Down (crouch).\n80\t- **Right stick = technique** (where your strike goes): ↑ high, → (toward the foe) middle, ↓ low, ← (away) the *spinning* family.\n81\t- Directions are **relative to the foe**: \"toward\" is the side the opponent is on, not the screen's right. Fighters face their foe automatically whenever they are free and on the ground.\n82\t- A technique starts on the **press edge** of the technique stick (neutral → a direction), combined with the posture held at that moment. Holding the technique stick does not repeat; it must return to neutral.\n83\t- **Late-posture upgrade window:** if the posture changes from Neutral to a direction within **4 frames (8 steps at 120 Hz)** after a standing technique starts, swap to that posture's variant and keep the elapsed startup frames. This makes \"both sticks at once\" forgiving and adds no latency.\n84\t- **Buffer:** a technique press made while busy (recovery, blockstun) is kept for **0.15 s** (`input.buffered`) and fires on the first free frame.\n85\t- **4-way gates:** both sticks are quantized to 4 directions. Analog sticks use the dominant axis, press at 0.5 and release below 0.35 (hysteresis). For digital posture, vertical beats horizontal (W+D = Up). For the technique keys, the most recent press wins; if two are truly simultaneous, the order is ↑ > ↓ > horizontal.\n86\t\n87\t**The technique matrix** (L posture × R technique). The 15 scoring techniques are in **bold**.\n88\t\n89\t| L posture \\ R technique | R ↑ (high) | R → (toward) | R ↓ (low) | R ← (away / spin) |\n90\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n91\t| **Neutral** (stand) | **Round Kick** | **Front Kick** (close: **Reverse Punch**) | **Low Kick** | **Back Kick** |\n92\t| **Toward** (step in) | **Upper Lunge Punch** (too close: Round Kick) | **Middle Lunge Punch** (too close: Front Kick) | **Front Foot Sweep** | **Turn Back Kick** |\n93\t| **Away** (step back) | **Back Round Kick** | Block (auto height) | **Back Foot Sweep** | Back Somersault |\n94\t| **Up** (jump) | Front Somersault (flips over the foe) | **Jumping Side Kick** | Jump (straight) | **Jumping Back Kick** |\n95\t| **Down** (crouch) | **Upper Punch** (anti-air) | **Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch** | Crouch | Back Roll |\n96\t\n97\tPosture alone: Neutral = kamae idle, Toward = walk in (72 u/s), Away = walk back (56 u/s), Up = straight jump, Down = crouch while held. With walking counted, that makes about two dozen moves, matching the source's \"24\".\n98\t\n99\t### 3.2 Device mappings\n100\tEngine action names are given in `code`. The game defines its own input preset, `KC_KEYS` (§15.4 EC1 adds the right-stick codes).\n101\t\n102\t| Action | Gamepad (standard mapping, DualSense first) | Keyboard (1 player) | Mouse |\n103\t|---|---|---|---|\n104\t| Posture `up/down/left/right` | Left stick, D-pad | W A S D (arrows also work; the engine fixes them as moves) | — |\n105\t| Technique `tUp/tDown/tLeft/tRight` | **Right stick** (`PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, EC1) **or face buttons**: △ (Pad3) = tUp, ✕ (Pad0) = tDown, □ (Pad2) = tLeft, ○ (Pad1) = tRight | **I K J L** (plus Numpad 8 5/2 4 6) | — |\n106\t| `pause` | Options (Pad9); ignored while Create (Pad8) is held, which is the AI Hub's chord | Esc, P | — |\n107\t| `start` | Options | Enter | — |\n108\t| Menus `confirm` / `cancel` | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right-click; hover moves the focus; the wheel scrolls lists |\n109\t| Dev panel hotkey (only after dev mode is enabled once) | L3+R3 | F2 or Backquote | — |\n110\t\n111\t- **Local 2 players on one keyboard** (`game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })`, then `input.use()` with explicit maps; do not rely on `halfKeys`' automatic order): **P1** posture W A S D, technique **T F G H** (T up, G down, F left, H right). **P2** posture arrows, technique **I J K L** and Numpad 8 4 5 6 (5 or 2 = down).\n112\t- Menus accept every joined player's confirm and cancel (the engine's `E.Menu` reads `game._players`).\n113\t- **Face buttons double as a technique stick.** ✕ = confirm in menus, but in a fight there is no confirm; skip prompts use `start` only.\n114\t\n115\t### 3.3 Prompts and glyphs\n116\t- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On a gamepad they show **PlayStation glyphs by default**. Set the engine's standard setting `glyphs` (Controls → Controller → *Button symbols*) to default to `'playstation'` (EC2); players can switch it to **Xbox** (A B X Y). The keyboard shows keycaps and the mouse shows mouse icons.\n117\t- **The 5×7 pixel font has no ✕ ○ □ △** (verified: it holds ASCII plus ♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • © × ▸ ♪). Build an icon renderer, `KC.glyph(g, x, y, code, size)`, that draws:\n118\t  - PS badges: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ad8a0`, each on a dark round badge.\n119\t  - Xbox badges: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow.\n120\t  - Stick icons: L or R with a direction arrow, for twin-stick prompts.\n121\t  - Keycaps: a rounded light rectangle with the key label; Space is wide.\n122\t  - Mouse icons with the button highlighted.\n123\t  - Also add ✕ ○ □ △ glyphs to the font (EC3) as a fallback.\n124\t- A technique prompt is two icons, `[L→] + [R↑]` on a pad and `[D] + [I]` on a keyboard. Build it with `KC.promptFor(techId, device)`.\n125\t\n126\t### 3.4 10-foot rules (all screens)\n127\t- Primary menu text uses `scale: 2`. Use no more than 7 items per menu and one primary action per screen. The focus highlight is big and animated (a pulsing bar plus a ▸ cursor). Hold to repeat.\n128\t- A bottom **prompt bar** always shows confirm and back (for example \"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK\", drawn with glyph icons).\n129\t- Mouse hover moves the focus and a click activates. The pixel menus use `E.Menu`, which already does this; custom grids (select, stage, records) must implement hover and click with `input.pointer()` and `input.clicked()`.\n130\t- **Mouse in the world, where this genre uses it:**\n131\t  - on the Champon Road, hovering a stop names it and clicking the pulsing next stop starts it;\n132\t  - on the select screen, clicking a fighter picks them;\n133\t  - in the Move List, clicking a technique around the R-stick previews it;\n134\t  - on the online card, click READY or MORE TIME;\n135\t  - in the Technique Lab, clicking the dummy cycles its mode.\n136\t  - Fighting itself stays on the keyboard or a pad (no mouse attacks), as in PC fighting games.\n137\t- Avoid dense data screens. The records screen shows 10 big rows. The move list is interactive, one posture row at a time (§8.6). The About screen is paged, at most 8 lines per page.\n138\t- Keep titles and pauses clear of the engine's top-right corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`, about 110×60 CSS px).\n139\t\n140\t### 3.5 Touch-only devices\n141\t- Detection: `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, no gamepad connected, and no key pressed yet. Re-check on every `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`; any keyboard or pad input lifts the gate at once.\n142\t- Set `input.touchEnabled = false` and give every scene `touch: []`, so no touch buttons or stick ever show.\n143\t- The title shows a centred panel: **\"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"**, with the engine's corner **settings/menu button** still visible (it opens the standard settings). The attract mode still starts after 10 s and loops. Taps on the canvas only return to the title panel, where the 10 s idle timer restarts the demo.\n144\t\n145\t---\n146\t\n147\t## 4. Combat system\n148\t\n149\t### 4.1 Fighter body and stage geometry\n150\t- The arena walls are at `x0 = 0` and `x1 = 400`. The marks are at **x = 150 (white, left)** and **x = 250 (red, right)**. `y = 0` is the fight plane and `z` is up (side view).\n151\t- Rigs are `new E.Humanoid({ size: 2.2, build: 'heroic', weapon: null, … })` (each fighter's own size, 2.0–2.6, is in §7.1), about 72 px tall at 240 px. The gi colour always follows the side: white on the left mark, red on the right. The body is an `E.Platformer` on an `E.FightStage(0, 400)` (from the fight module): `w = 8 × size ≈ 18`, `h = 30 × size = 66`. Use the same constructor numbers as `E.Fighter` (`accel 2400, decel 3200, airAccel 0, coyote 0, buffer 0, cut 1`).\n152\t- Movement: walk toward 72 u/s and away 56 u/s. Jump `vz 360` with gravity 1100 (apex ≈ 59 u, ≈ 0.65 s or 39 frames in the air). Front somersault `vz 370`, `vx +140` toward the foe (40 frames in the air, 94 u of travel; it crosses a foe within a gap of ≈ 70). Back somersault `vz 370, vx −126` (85 u). Back roll travels 90 u away in 0.47 s along the ground.\n153\t- **Per-fighter stats and tuning multiply the table:** walk speed × `stats.walk`; reach × `stats.reach` × `TUNE.reachScale`; technique frames ÷ (`stats.speed` × `TUNE.techSpeed`), rounded to whole 120 Hz steps.\n154\t- Push boxes never overlap. Resolve them as `E.Fighter.exchange` does: shared correction, with walls respected.\n155\t- **Hurt boxes come from rig joints** (copy `E.Fighter._boxes()`): head, body and legs. **Hit box:** during active frames, a circle of radius `hitR × size / 2` at the striking joint (`footR`, `handR`, `handL` or `heel`). Crouching lowers the head and body boxes; jumps and flips raise the legs. The **explicit height rules (§4.3) are applied as well**, so results never depend on animation alone.\n156\t\n157\t### 4.2 Technique table (authoritative data; frames are at 60 fps, and the engine steps at 120 Hz, so multiply by 2)\n158\t\"Gap\" means the distance between the two push-box edges at contact. Points go in steps of 100. \"Full\" means the technique can reach a full point (600 or more) without a counter. The source total is the TASVideos figure; ours is about 85% of it, for a slightly snappier modern feel.\n159\t\n160\t| id | Name (JP) | Input | Height | Startup / active / recovery | Total (source) | Travel | Reach (gap) | Points | Full |\n161\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n162\t| `frontKick` | Front Kick (mae-geri) | N + → | mid | 11 / 5 / 28 | 44 (54) | 0 | 6–30 | 100–200 | no |\n163\t| `revPunch` | Reverse Punch (gyaku-zuki) | N + → when gap < 12 | mid | 6 / 4 / 18 | 28 (34) | +4 | 0–14 | 100–200 | no |\n164\t| `roundKick` | Round Kick (mawashi-geri) | N + ↑ | high | 10 / 5 / 22 | 37 (44) | 0 | 6–30 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n165\t| `lowKick` | Low Kick (gedan-geri) | N + ↓ | low, unblockable | 10 / 5 / 28 | 43 (52) | 0 | 4–28 | 100–200 | no |\n166\t| `backKick` | Back Kick (ushiro-geri) | N + ← | mid | 13 / 5 / 23 | 41 (49) | +6 | 10–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n167\t| `upLunge` | Upper Lunge Punch (jōdan oi-zuki) | → + ↑ (gap < 18: `roundKick`) | high | 15 / 5 / 26 | 46 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 500–1000 | yes |\n168\t| `midLunge` | Middle Lunge Punch (chūdan oi-zuki) | → + → (gap < 18: `frontKick`) | mid | 14 / 5 / 26 | 45 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 200–400 | counter only |\n169\t| `frontSweep` | Front Foot Sweep (ashi-barai) | → + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (78) | +10 | 4–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n170\t| `turnBackKick` | Turn Back Kick | → + ← | mid | 17 / 5 / 26 | 48 (57) | +12 | 12–40 | 500–1000 | yes |\n171\t| `backRound` | Back Round Kick (ushiro-mawashi-geri) | ← + ↑ | high | 15 / 5 / 23 | 43 (51) | 0 | 8–34 | 500–1000 | yes |\n172\t| `backSweep` | Back Foot Sweep (spinning sweep) | ← + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (75) | 0 | 6–34 | 100–200 | no |\n173\t| `jumpSide` | Jumping Side Kick (tobi yoko-geri) | ↑ + → | high (air) | 16 / 10 / 26 | 52 (62) | +60 in the air | 30–90 | 500–1000 | yes |\n174\t| `jumpBack` | Jumping Back Kick | ↑ + ← | high (air) | 14 / 8 / 22 | 44 (52) | +20 in the air | 8–48 | 500–1000 | yes |\n175\t| `upperPunch` | Upper Punch (age-zuki) | ↓ + ↑ | high, rising (also hits airborne foes in the first half of their arc) | 8 / 7 / 30 | 45 (53) | 0 | 0–18 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n176\t| `squatPunch` | Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch | ↓ + → | mid-low (also hits crouchers) | 13 / 5 / 30 | 48 (57) | +20 | 10–44 | 400–800 | yes |\n177\t| `block` | Block (uke) | ← + → | auto high or mid | 1 / 14 guard / 4 | 19 (22) | 0 | — | — | — |\n178\t| `frontFlip` | Front Somersault | ↑ + ↑ | evasive | 6 crouch, 40 air, 12 land | 58 (70) | +95 | — | — | — |\n179\t| `backFlip` | Back Somersault | ← + ← | evasive | 6 / 40 / 12 | 58 (70) | −85 | — | — | — |\n180\t| `jump` | Jump | ↑ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 4 / 39 air / 5 | 48 (47) | 0 | — | — | — |\n181\t| `crouch` | Crouch | ↓ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 3 in, hold, 5 out | — | — | — | — | — |\n182\t| `backRoll` | Back Roll | ↓ + ← | evasive (low profile, hurt height 24) | 4 / 28 / 8 | 40 (—) | −90 | — | — | — |\n183\t\n184\t**Close substitutions chain:** a Middle Lunge Punch at gap < 12 becomes a Front Kick, and then a Reverse Punch.\n185\t\n186\tStore this as `KC.DATA.techniques` (schema in §15.3). Each entry also carries:\n187\t- `move`: the rig move spec (§10.2);\n188\t- `limb` and `hitR`;\n189\t- `kiai`: `'light'` or `'heavy'`;\n190\t- `bull: true` for the five techniques that work on the bull (`upperPunch, upLunge, squatPunch, frontKick, backKick`, as in the source);\n191\t- `potHeights`: the bonus-stage heights the technique can break.\n192\t\n193\t**The Technique Lab (§14) must measure each technique's real contact gap against a standing dummy, and the tests assert the table within ±4 u.** Tune the rig `reach`/`r1`/`lunge` values until it matches; never change the table to fit the animation.\n194\t\n195\t### 4.3 Height and state rules\n196\t- **High** techniques whiff against a **crouching** foe or one in a **back roll**.\n197\t- **Low** techniques whiff against an **airborne** foe (jump, flips, jump kicks).\n198\t- **Mid** techniques hit standing foes. `squatPunch` also hits crouching foes.\n199\t- `upperPunch` hits standing foes (the head) and **airborne foes in the first half of their arc** (its hit box rises with the fist; tune it in the Lab against a jumping and a flipping dummy). Against an airborne foe it gets +0.25 quality, capped at 1 (the anti-air reward).\n200\t- **Somersaults:** while airborne, the tucked body can be hit only by high techniques and `upperPunch`; mid and low pass under it.\n201\t- A fighter that is **down**, **rising**, **frozen** or **returning** cannot be hit.\n202\t\n203\t### 4.4 Block\n204\t- The input is ← + → (step back and strike toward). Startup is 1 frame, followed by **14 guard frames** (TUNE `blockWindow`) and 4 recovery frames. Holding does not extend it; you must input it again.\n205\t- It blocks **high and mid** techniques, including jump kicks. It cannot block **low** (`lowKick`, `frontSweep`, `backSweep`): \"jump them\".\n206\t- Animation: high block (age-uke, forearm up) or middle block (soto-uke, forearm across), picked when the attack's active frames begin.\n207\t- **On a block:** a wood \"clack\", blue sparks and 0.05 s of hit-stop. The attacker is pushed back 14 u and gets **+10 recovery frames**, counting as *mid-technique* (so it is open to a ×2 counter). The defender's guard ends early and it is free 6 frames sooner (frame advantage). The crowd goes \"ooh\". No points.\n208\t\n209\t### 4.5 Scoring a hit\n210\t1. **Quality** `q = 0.5 × timingQ + 0.5 × spacingQ`, so q runs from 0.15 to 1:\n211\t   - `timingQ` is 1.0 if contact happens in the first 40% of the active frames, falling linearly to 0.3 at the last active frame.\n212\t   - `spacingQ` is 1.0 if the contact gap is in the top 30% of the reach band (full extension, *kime*), falling linearly to 0 at the band's minimum (\"jammed\").\n213\t2. `points = lo + Math.round((hi − lo) × q / 100) × 100`, clamped to [lo, hi]. Round Kick and Upper Punch therefore reach 600 only when q ≥ 0.84 (clean). The 500–1000 techniques always reach a full point when they land; their risk is in their long startup and recovery.\n214\t3. **Counter:** if the defender is in a technique's startup, active or recovery frames, or in blocked recoil, then `points = min(1000, points × TUNE.counterMul (2.0))`. Tag the hit \"COUNTER!\".\n215\t4. **Full point** if `points ≥ TUNE.fullAt` (600), worth 1.0 toward winning the round. Otherwise a **half point**, worth 0.5.\n216\t5. Add the points to the scorer's arcade score. These rules are deterministic, with no RNG, which matters for tests and netplay.\n217\t- Examples: a clean Round Kick (q = 1) gives 600, a full point; a sloppy one (q = 0.6) gives 500, a half. A clean Front Kick counter gives 200 × 2 = 400, still a half. A clean Back Kick counter gives 400 × 2 = 800, a full point.\n218\t\n219\t### 4.6 Simultaneous hits (*aiuchi*)\n220\tOn the first valid hit, keep simulating for **6 steps (0.05 s)** before freezing. If the other fighter also lands in that window, the call is **\"AIUCHI! NO POINT\"**. Nobody scores, and both fighters reset.\n221\t\n222\t### 4.7 Hit reactions\n223\t- Half point: the defender staggers back 10 u and holds the hurt pose.\n224\t- Full point or any sweep: the defender is knocked down. Pass `down: 0..1` to the rig, slide back 18 u and lie still. The attacker holds its **kime** pose (the technique's end pose, extended by `hold`) until \"STOP!\".\n225\t- The Bout's own hit-stop is `TUNE.hitstop` (0.10 s). **Do not use `game.freeze`**: the engine's hit-stop skips the scene's `update`, which would stall networking (§12.6). Full points also get 0.35 s of 0.35× slow motion on `game.timeScale` (skipped under reduce motion, and never on an online host: use a visual effect only).\n226\t\n227\t### 4.8 Facing and cross-ups\n228\t- A fighter turns to face its foe when it is **free and grounded**, with a 6-frame pivot animation. During its own technique it never turns.\n229\t- If a foe flips over you while you are mid-technique, you are open from behind, and their hit scores the counter ×2.\n230\t- The `←` (spin) family always strikes **toward the foe** by turning through: Back Kick turns 180° and drives the heel; Turn Back Kick and Back Round Kick turn a full 360°. It is a high-risk, high-value family.\n231\t\n232\t---\n233\t\n234\t## 5. Bout rules and the referee\n235\t\n236\t### 5.1 Terms\n237\t- **Exchange:** from \"BEGIN!\" until \"STOP!\".\n238\t- **Round:** a 30 s clock (`TUNE.clock`); the first to `TUNE.pointsToWin` (2.0) points wins it.\n239\t- **Match:** best of 3 rounds (`roundsToWin` 2) in story and versus. Online and local Kachinuki bouts are a single round.\n240\t\n241\t### 5.2 Bout state machine (`KC.Bout`, deterministic; all timings × `TUNE.refPace`)\n242\t\n243\t| Phase | What happens | Duration |\n244\t|---|---|---|\n245\t| `intro` | Match start only: both walk in from the arena edges to their marks and face each other | 1.2 s |\n246\t| `rei` | Referee \"BOW!\"; both bow (the `bow` pose) | 0.9 s |\n247\t| `card` | \"ROUND n\" / \"DECIDING ROUND!\" card with a gong | 0.9 s |\n248\t| `ready` | Both stand in yoi/kamae; the referee is centred behind them | 0.4 s |\n249\t| `hajime` | \"BEGIN!\" with the referee's downward chop; control starts at t = 0.35 s | 0.6 s |\n250\t| `fight` | Clock runs; detect hits, blocks and aiuchi. Soft-lock guard: being in `fight` longer than clock + 5 s raises an error | ≤ clock |\n251\t| `yame` | After a scoring hit (or aiuchi): hit-stop, then the referee steps in, \"STOP!\", both frozen, the defender's reaction plays | 0.9 s |\n252\t| `award` | Referee: \"WHITE!/RED!\" plus \"FULL POINT!/HALF POINT!\" with the flag signal; a points popup (\"ROUND KICK • 600\"); the scoreboard updates. Or \"NO POINT!\" for aiuchi | 1.2 s |\n253\t| `reset` | Both auto-walk to their marks at 2× speed; skippable with `start` after 0.6 s of `award` | ≤ 1.2 s |\n254\t| `roundEnd` | \"WHITE WINS!\" (or red); winner pose (fist up + kiai), loser kneels | 2.0 s |\n255\t| `time` | Clock at 0: \"TIME!\". The leader on points wins; on a tie go to `hantei` | 0.8 s |\n256\t| `hantei` | \"JUDGES!\" whistle. The two corner judges and the referee raise flags one by one, 0.5 s apart; the majority wins | 2.4 s |\n257\t| `matchEnd` | Back to the marks, \"BOW!\", the referee raises the winner's hand; then `onMatchEnd(winnerSide, stats)` | 2.5 s |\n258\t\n259\t- The clock runs only during `fight`. It shows `ceil(clock)`.\n260\t- Time bonus: on a round win, +100 for each whole second left (the source's rule).\n261\t- **Hantei votes** (deterministic, seeded by the bout seed). Each judge scores a side as `techPts + 60 × attempts + 40 × blocks + noise`, where:\n262\t  - `techPts` = raw points from scoring hits this round;\n263\t  - `attempts` = techniques thrown toward the foe with the foe within max reach;\n264\t  - `noise` = ±120 from that judge's `E.rng` stream.\n265\t  - The flags fall for the higher score; a tie goes to the side that scored last, then to white.\n266\t- **Match point** call when a side reaches `pointsToWin − 0.5` or more. **Perfect** (no points conceded in the match): +5,000.\n267\t- Event bus: `bout.on('point' | 'call' | 'block' | 'whiff' | 'roundEnd' | 'matchEnd', fn)`. The HUD, audio, crowd, story and netcode all listen to it.\n268\t\n269\t### 5.3 Referee and judges (`KC.Referee`)\n270\t- **Judge Genkotsu:** an older referee in a navy blazer (`outfit: 'coat'`), grey trousers, white shirt and red bow tie (trim). He is bald with a white stubble colour, and a white beard is drawn as an overlay at the head joint. Size 2.1, standing at `x = mid, y = +14` (behind the fighters, as in the source). In the 2.5D view he stands upstage.\n271\t- **Corner judges:** two smaller officials at `x = 18` and `x = 382`, `y = +34`, holding a red and a white flag (drawn from `rig.hand('L'/'R')` as small poles with cloth that waves).\n272\t- **Signals:**\n273\t\n274\t| Call | Gesture |\n275\t|---|---|\n276\t| hajime | Both hands chop down and apart |\n277\t| yame | One open hand raised between the fighters |\n278\t| waza-ari (half point) | Arm 45° down toward the scorer's side |\n279\t| ippon (full point) | Arm raised high toward the scorer's side |\n280\t| aiuchi | Fists knocked together |\n281\t| hantei | Whistle, then point at each judge |\n282\t| winner | Lift the winner's arm |\n283\t| rei | Bow |\n284\t\n285\t- **Calls:** a voice (gibberish, §11.3) plus a subtitle banner in the title font. The **Referee calls** setting chooses English or the Japanese terms:\n286\t\n287\t| id | English | Japanese |\n288\t|---|---|---|\n289\t| begin | BEGIN! | HAJIME! |\n290\t| stop | STOP! | YAME! |\n291\t| white / red | WHITE! / RED! | SHIRO! / AKA! |\n292\t| half | HALF POINT! | WAZA-ARI! |\n293\t| full | FULL POINT! | IPPON! |\n294\t| nopoint | NO POINT! | TORIMASEN! |\n295\t| aiuchi | AIUCHI! | AIUCHI! |\n296\t| time | TIME! | SOREMADE! |\n297\t| judges | JUDGES! | HANTEI! |\n298\t| whiteWins / redWins | WHITE WINS! / RED WINS! | SHIRO NO KACHI! / AKA NO KACHI! |\n299\t| bow | BOW! | REI! |\n300\t| decide | DECIDING ROUND! | SHOBU! |\n301\t| forfeit | FORFEIT! | KIKEN! |\n302\t| matchPoint | MATCH POINT! | MATCH POINT! |\n303\t\n304\t### 5.4 Fight HUD (drawn on the canvas in `r.overlay`, anchored to `r.W`/`r.H`)\n305\t- **Top centre:** the clock in an `E.ui.box`, title font at scale 2.\n306\t- **Top left (WHITE) and top right (RED):** the name, a belt icon (story) or player-colour tag, and **point slots**: `pointsToWin` circles, where a half point half-fills a circle and a full point fills it. Under them, the round pips.\n307\t- **Bottom left:** \"1P 0123400\" score; **bottom right:** \"HI 0250000\".\n308\t- **Centre:** calls in the title font (`scale: 3`, colours `#fff6d0 #ffd060 #e8782a`).\n309\t- **Over the scorer:** a points popup (`particles.text`), with the technique name when *Technique names* is on.\n310\t- **Hints** (story matches 1–3, *Tutorial hints* on): one line at the bottom, kept clear of `touchArea()`.\n311\t- **Input display** (setting or dev): the last 12 inputs as glyph pairs.\n312\t- The \"DEV\" badge shows while any tuning value is non-default.\n313\t\n314\t---\n315\t\n316\t## 6. CPU AI (`KC.KarateAI`)\n317\t\n318\t### 6.1 Architecture\n319\t- Output: the **same control intent a human produces**, `{ posture: 'n'|'u'|'d'|'l'|'r', techPress: null|'u'|'d'|'l'|'r' }` in **screen** directions. `Karateka.control()` converts it to toward/away, so the AI obeys exactly the same rules as a player. The same class is the **Autopilot** for tests and the attract mode.\n320\t- **Perception with reaction delay:** each step, push an observation of the foe (technique id and phase, airborne, crouching, down, gap, facing). The AI acts on the observation that is `react` seconds old.\n321\t- **Decision tick** every `think` seconds (plus jitter from its `E.rng`), by priority:\n322\t  1. **Punish:** the foe is in recovery or blocked recoil within reach, so pick the best technique that reaches (prefer full-capable ones).\n323\t  2. **React** (probability `readP`) to a seen technique:\n324\t     - a low technique → `jump` or `jumpSide` (if aggressive), or `frontFlip`;\n325\t     - a high one → `crouch`, or `squatPunch` (ducks and strikes);\n326\t     - a mid one → `block` (probability `block`), or `backFlip` if the gap allows;\n327\t     - the foe airborne and approaching → `upperPunch` if within 0–30, else walk back.\n328\t  3. **Proactive:** spacing toward `gap`. When in range of a technique in its personality's `weights`, throw it (weighted pick among the techniques whose reach band contains the current gap) with probability `aggression`. Otherwise feint (a short walk in or out) with probability `patience`.\n329\t  4. **Mistakes:** with probability `mistake`, choose a random technique.\n330\t- **Habit tracker** (level ≥ 8): count the foe's techniques this match. Pre-load the counter to its top two (for example, `jump` vs `frontSweep`, `crouch` vs `roundKick`).\n331\t- **Level curve** (L = 1–10): `react = lerp(0.55, 0.12, (L−1)/9)`, `think = lerp(0.50, 0.14)`, `readP = lerp(0.25, 0.92)`, `mistake = lerp(0.30, 0.02)`. Difficulty Easy = L−2 (min 1) with react ×1.25; Hard = L+1 (max 10) with react ×0.85.\n332\t- **Determinism:** all randomness comes from the AI's own `E.rng(seed)`. Same seed, same inputs, same match: the tests hash it.\n333\t- **Showcase mode** (attract): cycle through the technique list in order (each in its range), use perfect reads and react 0.1 s. The director can force outcomes (§13).\n334\t\n335\t### 6.2 Personalities\n336\t\n337\t| Fighter | L | aggr | patience | jump | block | gap | weights (technique: weight) | notes |\n338\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n339\t| KAMABOKO | 1 | .7 | .2 | .05 | .10 | 30 | frontKick 3, lowKick 2, revPunch 2, roundKick 1 | clumsy |\n340\t| SENPAI NEGI | 2 | .5 | .4 | .10 | .35 | 36 | roundKick 2, frontKick 2, backKick 2, midLunge 1 | textbook |\n341\t| EBI | 3 | .6 | .2 | .35 | .20 | 70 | jumpSide 4, frontFlip 2, lowKick 1, roundKick 1 | jumper |\n342\t| KIKURAGE | 4 | .25 | .8 | .05 | .65 | 40 | revPunch 3, upperPunch 3, squatPunch 2, backSweep 1 | counter-puncher |\n343\t| IKA | 5 | .5 | .3 | .25 | .30 | 44 | backRound 3, turnBackKick 3, backKick 2, frontFlip 2, backFlip 2 | cross-ups |\n344\t| CAPTAIN BUTA | 6 | .8 | .2 | .05 | .20 | 60 | upLunge 4, midLunge 3, frontSweep 2, roundKick 1 | walk 60, reach ×1.1 |\n345\t| ASARI | 7 | .4 | .5 | .05 | .50 | 34 | squatPunch 3, backSweep 3, frontSweep 2, lowKick 2, backRoll 2 | crouches 50% |\n346\t| MASTER KYABETSU | 8 | .4 | .7 | .10 | .60 | 42 | roundKick 2, revPunch 2, upperPunch 2, turnBackKick 1 | readP +.05, habit tracker |\n347\t| MEI | 9 | .65 | .4 | .20 | .50 | 46 | jumpSide 3, roundKick 3, frontSweep 3, turnBackKick 2, backRound 2 | habit tracker, mixes high and low |\n348\t\n349\t### 6.3 Balance targets (asserted by `ai.test`, §18)\n350\t- An Autopilot at L5 beats CPU L1–3 in ≥ 90% of seeded matches, L5 in 40–60%, and L9 in ≤ 25%.\n351\t- In CPU-vs-CPU matches at equal level, the mean round length is 15–45 s.\n352\t- Across 60 seeded CPU-vs-CPU matches, every scoring technique lands at least once, no technique has more than 35% of the scoring hits, and full points are 30–60% of scoring hits.\n353\t\n354\t---\n355\t\n356\t## 7. Content\n357\t\n358\t### 7.1 Roster\n359\tGi colours: **white side** cloth and pants `#f2eee4`; **red side** `#c8302a`. Fighters are barefoot (`boot` = skin) and bare-handed (`glove` = skin). Belts: white `#f4f4ee`, yellow `#f0c830`, green `#3aa050`, blue `#2f5fd0`, brown `#6a4024`, black `#16161c`, Champ gold `#e8c040`. Every rig sets `colors` fully; never leave defaults.\n360\t\n361\t| id | Name / title | Rig (beyond the gi) | Stats (walk, reach, speed) | Voice (f0 Hz, formant ×, syllables/s, wave) | Intro line |\n362\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n363\t| `taro` | TARO MOYASHI, the hero | heroic, size 2.15, `hair: 'spiky'` black, white headband (`hat: { style: 'band' }`), slim (`limbW` −10%) | 1, 1, 1 | 190, 1.05, 15, pulse | \"I WILL BE CHAMP!\" |\n364\t| `kamaboko` | KAMABOKO, dojo classmate | default (chibi) build, size 2.0, round, pink-swirl headband `#ff7aa8` | .95, .92, .95 | 240, 1.15, 17, pulse | \"I ATE THREE BOWLS. I AM READY!\" |\n365\t| `negi` | SENPAI NEGI, dojo senior | heroic, size 2.35, `hair: 'ponytail'`, green headband `#3aa050` | 1, 1.04, 1 | 150, 1.0, 13, saw | \"SHOW ME WHAT I TAUGHT YOU.\" |\n366\t| `ebi` | EBI, harbor fisherman | heroic lean, size 2.15, orange bandana, tanned `#c98a5a` | 1.1, .98, 1.05 | 210, 1.08, 19, pulse | \"THE SEA MADE ME QUICK. CATCH ME!\" |\n367\t| `kikurage` | KIKURAGE, bamboo hermit | `hunch .3`, hood (brown), beard overlay, size 2.1 | .9, 1, .95 | 110, .92, 9, saw + breath | \"THE BAMBOO BENDS. IT DOES NOT BREAK.\" |\n368\t| `ika` | IKA, log-bridge acrobat | heroic, `hair: 'long'` white, long sleeves, pale violet skin tint `#e8d8f0` | 1.05, 1.02, 1.02 | 200, 1.1, 14, triangle + vibrato | \"EIGHT ARMS. TWO LEGS. WATCH THEM ALL.\" |\n369\t| `buta` | CAPTAIN BUTA, ship's stern | `build: 'bulky'`, size 2.6, navy sailor cap (`hat: 'cap'`), stubble | .83, 1.1, .92 | 85, .85, 11, saw | \"WELCOME ABOARD. NOW WALK THE PLANK!\" |\n370\t| `asari` | ASARI, night-market stall keeper | heroic, size 2.1, hair bun (`'ponytail'` short), teal headband | 1, .96, 1.04 | 170, 1.0, 15, square + clicks | \"MY SHELL IS SHUT. GO HOME.\" |\n371\t| `kyabetsu` | MASTER KYABETSU, Fuji garden | `hair: 'bald'`, white beard overlay, `hunch .2`, size 2.15 | .95, 1.02, 1.03 | 120, .9, 10, saw + vibrato 5 Hz | \"MANY LAYERS, YOUNG ONE. PEEL THEM.\" |\n372\t| `mei` | MEI, \"THE IRON LADLE\" (reigning champ) | heroic, size 2.15, `hair: 'ponytail'` black, gold belt; in a fight she wears her side's gi colour (red in the story final); an apron outfit in cutscenes | 1.05, 1, 1.05 | 290, 1.18, 16, pulse12 | \"THE GOLDEN BOWL STAYS IN MY SHOP.\" |\n373\t| `genkotsu` | JUDGE GENKOTSU (referee, not playable) | §5.3 | — | 105, .9, 11, saw, rough .18 | — |\n374\t\n375\tStats are small, visible modifiers (3 bars on the select screen). **Signature technique:** +0.1 quality on one technique each (taro `roundKick`, kamaboko `frontKick`, negi `backKick`, ebi `jumpSide`, kikurage `revPunch`, ika `backRound`, buta `upLunge`, asari `squatPunch`, kyabetsu `turnBackKick`, mei `frontSweep`).\n376\t\n377\t**Unlocks** (versus): taro through asari are available from the start. Kyabetsu unlocks on reaching match 8 in story, and Mei on finishing story. The dev mode has \"Unlock all\".\n378\t\n379\t### 7.2 Stages (8 fight stages and 3 bonus locations)\n380\tEach stage is data (`KC.DATA.stages`):\n381\t- the backdrop (sky gradient and layers, using EC6's custom layers);\n382\t- the floor painter (a material and colours);\n383\t- 3–8 props;\n384\t- lights (ambient and point lights);\n385\t- the crowd;\n386\t- weather and ambient particles;\n387\t- an ambience loop;\n388\t- the music id.\n389\t\n390\tThe arena is always `x 0–400`. Paint the world from −140 to 540, so a 16:9 screen (427 px wide) never shows emptiness.\n391\t\n392\t| id | Stage | Time / light | Floor | Backdrop layers | Props and life | Music |\n393\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n394\t| `dojo` | HON-DŌJŌ (Senpai Negi's dojo) | evening, warm interior, ambient .55 | polished planks (`E.MATERIALS.planks`, light oak) with tatami at the sides | shoji wall with warm backlight, wooden beams, kamidana shelf | hanging paper lanterns (light), calligraphy scrolls (kanji sprites 空 手 道), weapon rack (bo staffs), taiko drum, classmates kneeling in seiza along the back wall (crowd) | `dojo` |\n395\t| `harbor` | NAGASAKI PIER | dawn, ambient .7 warm | weathered deck planks | `sea` layer, lighthouse, moored boat silhouettes, distant hills, sunrise | crates, barrels (`kaykit…barrel-small-stack`), rope coils, rowboat (`kenney-pirate:boat-row-large`), **Mei's noodle cart** (custom) with steam, gull sprites, dock workers behind a rope | `harbor` |\n396\t| `bamboo` | BAMBOO FOREST | midday shade, ambient .6, light shafts | dirt with fallen leaves | 3 parallax depths of bamboo stalks (custom layer), canopy | stone lantern (tōrō, custom), small shrine, `weather: leaves`, birdsong; no crowd | `bamboo` |\n397\t| `log` | LOG BRIDGE (a tree trunk over a gorge) | afternoon, ambient .6, mist | the log's top in bark material, with the gorge visible below the floor line | cliffs (`mountains` in rock tones), a waterfall (animated custom layer), pines, a fog layer | vines, rope rails, mist particles; no crowd | `gorge` |\n398\t| `ship` | SHIP'S STERN | sunset, ambient .55 orange | deck planks | sea with sunset glints, distant coast | mast and rigging (custom), ship's wheel (custom), lanterns (light), cannon (`kenney-pirate:cannon`), barrels, a cheering crew (crowd); a 0.5 px roll sway (off under reduce motion) | `ship` |\n399\t| `market` | CHAMPON STREET (night market) | night, ambient .35 | stone pavement (`flagstone`) | `city-night` preset with hills | red paper lanterns in rows (light), stalls (`kenney-fantasy-town:stall-red`), the **CHAMPON-TEI** shop front with a noren curtain (custom), steam (smoke particles), shoppers (crowd) | `market` |\n400\t| `fuji` | FUJI GARDEN | dusk, ambient .6 pink | raked gravel (custom texture) | Mount Fuji (custom cone with a snow cap), pagoda silhouette, torii gate (custom), cherry trees | stone lanterns, koi pond edge, falling petals (pink particles), garden visitors (small crowd) | `fuji` |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92d1ba00-1a0f-4edd-bbd5-53c74c3ffc62","uuid":"d034b9e4-f4bc-4883-8423-f42e3fd5469a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:40:20.485Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Karate Champon\n\n**Build plan for an original point-sparring karate game inspired by *Karate Champ* (1984).**\n\n> **Title:** *Karate Champon* is a riff on *Karate Champ*. Champon (ちゃんぽん) is Nagasaki's mixed noodle dish, and also Japanese slang for \"a mix of everything\". The title was picked as TASK.md asks: a silent brainstorm of 50 riffs on the source's name, then one pick with the OS's cryptographic RNG (`python3 -I` running `secrets.randbelow(50)`). It returned **#20 of 50: \"Karate Champon\"**. Use this title everywhere: the title screen, `<title>`, file names and the AI Hub catalogue.\n\nThis plan is for the developers (possibly other AI models) who will build the game from **TASK.md + this file alone**. Read the whole plan once. Section 15 (architecture) and section 16 (build order) are the working reference. Section 19 maps every TASK.md requirement to the section that covers it and the test that proves it.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **One-line pitch** | Bow. Begin. One clean strike. A referee stops the fight after every point. Climb from white belt to Champ with two sticks and no buttons. |\n| **Genre** | 1-on-1 tournament karate fighting (point sparring), with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill |\n| **Perspective** | 2D side view (the engine's `'side'`, pitch 0) on a single-screen arena. An optional \"Tatami 2.5D\" camera uses the engine's `'brawler'` view (pitch 25). |\n| **Players** | 1 (story vs CPU), 2 (local versus), 3–4 (local *Kachinuki*: winner stays on), 2–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (full engine, readable), extended (section 15.4) |\n| **Deliverables (in this folder)** | `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743.html` (the single-file game), `exit-interview.json`, and the dev folder `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743/` |\n| **Demo length** | Full story ≈ 35–40 min (9 matches, 3 bonus stages, 3 drill lessons, cutscenes). Every mode can also be reached directly from the main menu. |\n| **Look** | The engine's native HD rigs drawn as pixel art at 320×240 (`res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`): PS1-era 2D, with white-gi vs red-gi fighters on scenic Japanese stages |\n\nFile-name rule: GameTitle = `KarateChampon` and SOURCE = `KarateChamp1984` (TASK.md's \"SOURCE: Karate Champ 1984\" with the spaces removed). Do not invent other names.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The source, and what we keep\n\n### 1.1 Research summary (what was confirmed)\n- *Karate Champ* (arcade, 1984; Japanese title *Karate Dō*). Data East published it. Shacknews credits Technōs Japan with the development; Wikipedia credits Data East. It is widely called the first one-on-one fighting game. The **Player vs Player** edition (1984) added head-to-head play. Its Japanese subtitle is *Bishōjo Seishun Hen*: two players compete over a girl across 12 locations.\n- **No health bars.** A landed technique ends the exchange. The hitter earns a **half point or a full point**, and **the first to two points wins**. Each technique has a point value. TASVideos #5529 lists, for example, Round Kick 300–600, Jumping Side Kick 500–1000 and Front Kick 100–200. A value of 500 or less is a half point and 600 or more is a full point. A technique that lands while the opponent is mid-technique **scores double**.\n- **The clock starts at 30**, ticks about once a second, and stops while play is stopped. It resumes when the judge says \"begin\". If time runs out, **the judge decides**. The judge's digitized voice calls \"Begin\", \"Stop\", \"Half point\", \"Full point\" and the side's colour (Japanese in the original release).\n- **Controls: two 4-way joysticks and no buttons.** The left stick sets movement and posture, the right stick the technique. Sources count 17–24 moves: front kick, reverse punch, squatting reverse lunge punch, jumping side kick, low kick, round kick, back round kick, turn back kick, front and back foot sweeps, back kick, middle and upper lunge punches, upper punch, jumping back kick, block (its height set by the incoming technique; it cannot stop the low kick or the sweeps), crouch, jump, and front and back somersaults.\n- **Ranks run from Beginner through 1st–10th Dan to Champ.** Dojo matches come first, then a national championship.\n- **Bonus stages:** *Evade* (thrown bonsai pots and boulders, 200 points per object broken, ending when you are hit), *Trial Chop* (board breaking with a \"Perfect\"), and *Get the Bull* (hit the charging bull: 3,000 points; only certain techniques work). A **practice** stage has a senpai demonstrate techniques that you copy (200 points each).\n- Player vs Player locations included a harbor, a Mount Fuji garden, a bamboo forest, a tree trunk over a chasm, a ship's stern, a dojo and a night path.\n- Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Champ; tasvideos.org/5529S (technique table, timer, bonus stages, ranks); shacknews.com \"Shack's Arcade Corner: Karate Champ\"; arcade-history.com/game/1261. StrategyWiki, Giant Bomb and TV Tropes refused automated fetches. Any fact not listed here is **unknown**.\n\n### 1.2 Design pillars (source trait → our mechanic)\n1. **One clean strike decides.** Point sparring, with a referee who stops the fight after every point. Fighters reset to their marks, and the score is shown as point icons, not a health bar.\n2. **Two sticks, no buttons.** A posture stick and a technique stick give 15 scoring techniques, plus block, jump, crouch, two somersaults and a roll. The DualSense's twin sticks map onto this naturally. The face buttons and IJKL act as a 4-way \"technique stick\" too.\n3. **The referee is a character.** He makes the calls, raises the flags, and the corner judges decide at time-out. His voice is gibberish, with subtitles.\n4. **Form is scored.** A clean technique at full reach, struck early in its active frames, scores more. A counter scores ×2. A beginner who flails earns half points, and a sharp player earns full points.\n5. **The climb.** You go from Beginner (white belt) to Champ. The belt colour on the gi changes as you rise through scenic stages.\n6. **Bonus stages** break up the tension: boards, flying pots, a charging bull.\n7. **The senpai's drill.** You learn by mimicking. It doubles as the tutorial.\n8. **White vs red.** Head-to-head versus works locally and online. Online uses *Kachinuki* (winner stays on), a real karate tournament format.\n9. **The arcade frame.** Numeric score, top-ten initials, a 10-second continue countdown and an attract mode.\n\n### 1.3 Narrative tone\nCopy the source's style: **terse, ALL-CAPS arcade calls, earnest**, with no long monologues. Each beat gets one or two lines, at most about 40 characters per line. The comedy comes only from the cast's champon-ingredient names and from Mei's dry remarks. Keep it warm, never mocking. All text is in Appendix A. The Player vs Player edition's girl-as-prize trope becomes **Mei**, the noodle-shop daughter who heckles and feeds the hero, and who turns out to be the reigning champion and the final opponent. The prize is the **Golden Bowl** trophy, not a person.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Game overview\n\n### 2.1 Core loop (write this sentence as a comment at the top of the game code)\n*Read your opponent from your mark. Commit to one technique with two sticks. Land it clean before they land theirs. The referee stops the fight, scores it and resets. First to 2 points takes the round, best of 3 takes the match. Win to earn a belt and the next stage, with a bonus stage after every second win.*\n\n### 2.2 Modes (main menu)\n1. **CHAMPON ROAD** (story/arcade): the ladder in §7.3. Score, continues and top-ten initials. Autosaves after each node, and CONTINUE appears on the title.\n2. **VERSUS**: P1 (white) vs P2 (red, a human or a CPU at level 1–10). Choose fighters, stage and rules. With 3–4 joined players, the **KACHINUKI** variant starts (winner stays on).\n3. **DOJO** (practice): Lessons I/II/III, All Techniques (21 drills), Free Spar against a dummy (stand, crouch, jump, block, attack), and the Move List.\n4. **BONUS GAMES**: Boards, Pots Ahoy!, Stop the Bull, each with its own high-score table and a difficulty choice.\n5. **RECORDS**: top-ten tables (Story, Boards, Pots, Bull).\n6. **OPTIONS**: the engine's standard settings plus the game's own (§8.10).\n7. **ABOUT**: Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt feedback, and credits (§19).\n- **Online:** when the AI Hub opens the page, the game skips all of the above and goes straight into the session (§12).\n\n### 2.3 Story synopsis\nNagasaki Harbor, spring 1984. The Champon Cup returns, and its prize is the Golden Bowl. Taro Moyashi (\"bean sprout\", slang for a skinny weakling) is a white belt at Senpai Negi's dojo. He trains, beats his classmates and tours the town's fight spots: the harbor, the bamboo forest, the log bridge, a ship's stern, the night market and the Fuji garden. Between stops he breaks boards, smashes flying pots and stops a bull. Mei runs the noodle cart and the shop *Champon-tei*; she heckles him, feeds him and cheers from every crowd. At the National Stadium final, Mei enters in a red gi with a gold belt: she is the reigning champion, \"The Iron Ladle\". Beat her and you become THE KARATE CHAMPON. \"Next year I take it back.\"\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls and UX\n\n### 3.1 The twin-stick technique system (the heart of the game)\n- **Left stick = posture** (where your body goes): Neutral, Toward the foe, Away, Up (jump), Down (crouch).\n- **Right stick = technique** (where your strike goes): ↑ high, → (toward the foe) middle, ↓ low, ← (away) the *spinning* family.\n- Directions are **relative to the foe**: \"toward\" is the side the opponent is on, not the screen's right. Fighters face their foe automatically whenever they are free and on the ground.\n- A technique starts on the **press edge** of the technique stick (neutral → a direction), combined with the posture held at that moment. Holding the technique stick does not repeat; it must return to neutral.\n- **Late-posture upgrade window:** if the posture changes from Neutral to a direction within **4 frames (8 steps at 120 Hz)** after a standing technique starts, swap to that posture's variant and keep the elapsed startup frames. This makes \"both sticks at once\" forgiving and adds no latency.\n- **Buffer:** a technique press made while busy (recovery, blockstun) is kept for **0.15 s** (`input.buffered`) and fires on the first free frame.\n- **4-way gates:** both sticks are quantized to 4 directions. Analog sticks use the dominant axis, press at 0.5 and release below 0.35 (hysteresis). For digital posture, vertical beats horizontal (W+D = Up). For the technique keys, the most recent press wins; if two are truly simultaneous, the order is ↑ > ↓ > horizontal.\n\n**The technique matrix** (L posture × R technique). The 15 scoring techniques are in **bold**.\n\n| L posture \\ R technique | R ↑ (high) | R → (toward) | R ↓ (low) | R ← (away / spin) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Neutral** (stand) | **Round Kick** | **Front Kick** (close: **Reverse Punch**) | **Low Kick** | **Back Kick** |\n| **Toward** (step in) | **Upper Lunge Punch** (too close: Round Kick) | **Middle Lunge Punch** (too close: Front Kick) | **Front Foot Sweep** | **Turn Back Kick** |\n| **Away** (step back) | **Back Round Kick** | Block (auto height) | **Back Foot Sweep** | Back Somersault |\n| **Up** (jump) | Front Somersault (flips over the foe) | **Jumping Side Kick** | Jump (straight) | **Jumping Back Kick** |\n| **Down** (crouch) | **Upper Punch** (anti-air) | **Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch** | Crouch | Back Roll |\n\nPosture alone: Neutral = kamae idle, Toward = walk in (72 u/s), Away = walk back (56 u/s), Up = straight jump, Down = crouch while held. With walking counted, that makes about two dozen moves, matching the source's \"24\".\n\n### 3.2 Device mappings\nEngine action names are given in `code`. The game defines its own input preset, `KC_KEYS` (§15.4 EC1 adds the right-stick codes).\n\n| Action | Gamepad (standard mapping, DualSense first) | Keyboard (1 player) | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Posture `up/down/left/right` | Left stick, D-pad | W A S D (arrows also work; the engine fixes them as moves) | — |\n| Technique `tUp/tDown/tLeft/tRight` | **Right stick** (`PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, EC1) **or face buttons**: △ (Pad3) = tUp, ✕ (Pad0) = tDown, □ (Pad2) = tLeft, ○ (Pad1) = tRight | **I K J L** (plus Numpad 8 5/2 4 6) | — |\n| `pause` | Options (Pad9); ignored while Create (Pad8) is held, which is the AI Hub's chord | Esc, P | — |\n| `start` | Options | Enter | — |\n| Menus `confirm` / `cancel` | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right-click; hover moves the focus; the wheel scrolls lists |\n| Dev panel hotkey (only after dev mode is enabled once) | L3+R3 | F2 or Backquote | — |\n\n- **Local 2 players on one keyboard** (`game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })`, then `input.use()` with explicit maps; do not rely on `halfKeys`' automatic order): **P1** posture W A S D, technique **T F G H** (T up, G down, F left, H right). **P2** posture arrows, technique **I J K L** and Numpad 8 4 5 6 (5 or 2 = down).\n- Menus accept every joined player's confirm and cancel (the engine's `E.Menu` reads `game._players`).\n- **Face buttons double as a technique stick.** ✕ = confirm in menus, but in a fight there is no confirm; skip prompts use `start` only.\n\n### 3.3 Prompts and glyphs\n- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On a gamepad they show **PlayStation glyphs by default**. Set the engine's standard setting `glyphs` (Controls → Controller → *Button symbols*) to default to `'playstation'` (EC2); players can switch it to **Xbox** (A B X Y). The keyboard shows keycaps and the mouse shows mouse icons.\n- **The 5×7 pixel font has no ✕ ○ □ △** (verified: it holds ASCII plus ♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • © × ▸ ♪). Build an icon renderer, `KC.glyph(g, x, y, code, size)`, that draws:\n  - PS badges: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ad8a0`, each on a dark round badge.\n  - Xbox badges: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow.\n  - Stick icons: L or R with a direction arrow, for twin-stick prompts.\n  - Keycaps: a rounded light rectangle with the key label; Space is wide.\n  - Mouse icons with the button highlighted.\n  - Also add ✕ ○ □ △ glyphs to the font (EC3) as a fallback.\n- A technique prompt is two icons, `[L→] + [R↑]` on a pad and `[D] + [I]` on a keyboard. Build it with `KC.promptFor(techId, device)`.\n\n### 3.4 10-foot rules (all screens)\n- Primary menu text uses `scale: 2`. Use no more than 7 items per menu and one primary action per screen. The focus highlight is big and animated (a pulsing bar plus a ▸ cursor). Hold to repeat.\n- A bottom **prompt bar** always shows confirm and back (for example \"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK\", drawn with glyph icons).\n- Mouse hover moves the focus and a click activates. The pixel menus use `E.Menu`, which already does this; custom grids (select, stage, records) must implement hover and click with `input.pointer()` and `input.clicked()`.\n- **Mouse in the world, where this genre uses it:**\n  - on the Champon Road, hovering a stop names it and clicking the pulsing next stop starts it;\n  - on the select screen, clicking a fighter picks them;\n  - in the Move List, clicking a technique around the R-stick previews it;\n  - on the online card, click READY or MORE TIME;\n  - in the Technique Lab, clicking the dummy cycles its mode.\n  - Fighting itself stays on the keyboard or a pad (no mouse attacks), as in PC fighting games.\n- Avoid dense data screens. The records screen shows 10 big rows. The move list is interactive, one posture row at a time (§8.6). The About screen is paged, at most 8 lines per page.\n- Keep titles and pauses clear of the engine's top-right corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`, about 110×60 CSS px).\n\n### 3.5 Touch-only devices\n- Detection: `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, no gamepad connected, and no key pressed yet. Re-check on every `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`; any keyboard or pad input lifts the gate at once.\n- Set `input.touchEnabled = false` and give every scene `touch: []`, so no touch buttons or stick ever show.\n- The title shows a centred panel: **\"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"**, with the engine's corner **settings/menu button** still visible (it opens the standard settings). The attract mode still starts after 10 s and loops. Taps on the canvas only return to the title panel, where the 10 s idle timer restarts the demo.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Combat system\n\n### 4.1 Fighter body and stage geometry\n- The arena walls are at `x0 = 0` and `x1 = 400`. The marks are at **x = 150 (white, left)** and **x = 250 (red, right)**. `y = 0` is the fight plane and `z` is up (side view).\n- Rigs are `new E.Humanoid({ size: 2.2, build: 'heroic', weapon: null, … })` (each fighter's own size, 2.0–2.6, is in §7.1), about 72 px tall at 240 px. The gi colour always follows the side: white on the left mark, red on the right. The body is an `E.Platformer` on an `E.FightStage(0, 400)` (from the fight module): `w = 8 × size ≈ 18`, `h = 30 × size = 66`. Use the same constructor numbers as `E.Fighter` (`accel 2400, decel 3200, airAccel 0, coyote 0, buffer 0, cut 1`).\n- Movement: walk toward 72 u/s and away 56 u/s. Jump `vz 360` with gravity 1100 (apex ≈ 59 u, ≈ 0.65 s or 39 frames in the air). Front somersault `vz 370`, `vx +140` toward the foe (40 frames in the air, 94 u of travel; it crosses a foe within a gap of ≈ 70). Back somersault `vz 370, vx −126` (85 u). Back roll travels 90 u away in 0.47 s along the ground.\n- **Per-fighter stats and tuning multiply the table:** walk speed × `stats.walk`; reach × `stats.reach` × `TUNE.reachScale`; technique frames ÷ (`stats.speed` × `TUNE.techSpeed`), rounded to whole 120 Hz steps.\n- Push boxes never overlap. Resolve them as `E.Fighter.exchange` does: shared correction, with walls respected.\n- **Hurt boxes come from rig joints** (copy `E.Fighter._boxes()`): head, body and legs. **Hit box:** during active frames, a circle of radius `hitR × size / 2` at the striking joint (`footR`, `handR`, `handL` or `heel`). Crouching lowers the head and body boxes; jumps and flips raise the legs. The **explicit height rules (§4.3) are applied as well**, so results never depend on animation alone.\n\n### 4.2 Technique table (authoritative data; frames are at 60 fps, and the engine steps at 120 Hz, so multiply by 2)\n\"Gap\" means the distance between the two push-box edges at contact. Points go in steps of 100. \"Full\" means the technique can reach a full point (600 or more) without a counter. The source total is the TASVideos figure; ours is about 85% of it, for a slightly snappier modern feel.\n\n| id | Name (JP) | Input | Height | Startup / active / recovery | Total (source) | Travel | Reach (gap) | Points | Full |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `frontKick` | Front Kick (mae-geri) | N + → | mid | 11 / 5 / 28 | 44 (54) | 0 | 6–30 | 100–200 | no |\n| `revPunch` | Reverse Punch (gyaku-zuki) | N + → when gap < 12 | mid | 6 / 4 / 18 | 28 (34) | +4 | 0–14 | 100–200 | no |\n| `roundKick` | Round Kick (mawashi-geri) | N + ↑ | high | 10 / 5 / 22 | 37 (44) | 0 | 6–30 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n| `lowKick` | Low Kick (gedan-geri) | N + ↓ | low, unblockable | 10 / 5 / 28 | 43 (52) | 0 | 4–28 | 100–200 | no |\n| `backKick` | Back Kick (ushiro-geri) | N + ← | mid | 13 / 5 / 23 | 41 (49) | +6 | 10–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n| `upLunge` | Upper Lunge Punch (jōdan oi-zuki) | → + ↑ (gap < 18: `roundKick`) | high | 15 / 5 / 26 | 46 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `midLunge` | Middle Lunge Punch (chūdan oi-zuki) | → + → (gap < 18: `frontKick`) | mid | 14 / 5 / 26 | 45 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 200–400 | counter only |\n| `frontSweep` | Front Foot Sweep (ashi-barai) | → + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (78) | +10 | 4–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n| `turnBackKick` | Turn Back Kick | → + ← | mid | 17 / 5 / 26 | 48 (57) | +12 | 12–40 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `backRound` | Back Round Kick (ushiro-mawashi-geri) | ← + ↑ | high | 15 / 5 / 23 | 43 (51) | 0 | 8–34 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `backSweep` | Back Foot Sweep (spinning sweep) | ← + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (75) | 0 | 6–34 | 100–200 | no |\n| `jumpSide` | Jumping Side Kick (tobi yoko-geri) | ↑ + → | high (air) | 16 / 10 / 26 | 52 (62) | +60 in the air | 30–90 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `jumpBack` | Jumping Back Kick | ↑ + ← | high (air) | 14 / 8 / 22 | 44 (52) | +20 in the air | 8–48 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `upperPunch` | Upper Punch (age-zuki) | ↓ + ↑ | high, rising (also hits airborne foes in the first half of their arc) | 8 / 7 / 30 | 45 (53) | 0 | 0–18 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n| `squatPunch` | Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch | ↓ + → | mid-low (also hits crouchers) | 13 / 5 / 30 | 48 (57) | +20 | 10–44 | 400–800 | yes |\n| `block` | Block (uke) | ← + → | auto high or mid | 1 / 14 guard / 4 | 19 (22) | 0 | — | — | — |\n| `frontFlip` | Front Somersault | ↑ + ↑ | evasive | 6 crouch, 40 air, 12 land | 58 (70) | +95 | — | — | — |\n| `backFlip` | Back Somersault | ← + ← | evasive | 6 / 40 / 12 | 58 (70) | −85 | — | — | — |\n| `jump` | Jump | ↑ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 4 / 39 air / 5 | 48 (47) | 0 | — | — | — |\n| `crouch` | Crouch | ↓ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 3 in, hold, 5 out | — | — | — | — | — |\n| `backRoll` | Back Roll | ↓ + ← | evasive (low profile, hurt height 24) | 4 / 28 / 8 | 40 (—) | −90 | — | — | — |\n\n**Close substitutions chain:** a Middle Lunge Punch at gap < 12 becomes a Front Kick, and then a Reverse Punch.\n\nStore this as `KC.DATA.techniques` (schema in §15.3). Each entry also carries:\n- `move`: the rig move spec (§10.2);\n- `limb` and `hitR`;\n- `kiai`: `'light'` or `'heavy'`;\n- `bull: true` for the five techniques that work on the bull (`upperPunch, upLunge, squatPunch, frontKick, backKick`, as in the source);\n- `potHeights`: the bonus-stage heights the technique can break.\n\n**The Technique Lab (§14) must measure each technique's real contact gap against a standing dummy, and the tests assert the table within ±4 u.** Tune the rig `reach`/`r1`/`lunge` values until it matches; never change the table to fit the animation.\n\n### 4.3 Height and state rules\n- **High** techniques whiff against a **crouching** foe or one in a **back roll**.\n- **Low** techniques whiff against an **airborne** foe (jump, flips, jump kicks).\n- **Mid** techniques hit standing foes. `squatPunch` also hits crouching foes.\n- `upperPunch` hits standing foes (the head) and **airborne foes in the first half of their arc** (its hit box rises with the fist; tune it in the Lab against a jumping and a flipping dummy). Against an airborne foe it gets +0.25 quality, capped at 1 (the anti-air reward).\n- **Somersaults:** while airborne, the tucked body can be hit only by high techniques and `upperPunch`; mid and low pass under it.\n- A fighter that is **down**, **rising**, **frozen** or **returning** cannot be hit.\n\n### 4.4 Block\n- The input is ← + → (step back and strike toward). Startup is 1 frame, followed by **14 guard frames** (TUNE `blockWindow`) and 4 recovery frames. Holding does not extend it; you must input it again.\n- It blocks **high and mid** techniques, including jump kicks. It cannot block **low** (`lowKick`, `frontSweep`, `backSweep`): \"jump them\".\n- Animation: high block (age-uke, forearm up) or middle block (soto-uke, forearm across), picked when the attack's active frames begin.\n- **On a block:** a wood \"clack\", blue sparks and 0.05 s of hit-stop. The attacker is pushed back 14 u and gets **+10 recovery frames**, counting as *mid-technique* (so it is open to a ×2 counter). The defender's guard ends early and it is free 6 frames sooner (frame advantage). The crowd goes \"ooh\". No points.\n\n### 4.5 Scoring a hit\n1. **Quality** `q = 0.5 × timingQ + 0.5 × spacingQ`, so q runs from 0.15 to 1:\n   - `timingQ` is 1.0 if contact happens in the first 40% of the active frames, falling linearly to 0.3 at the last active frame.\n   - `spacingQ` is 1.0 if the contact gap is in the top 30% of the reach band (full extension, *kime*), falling linearly to 0 at the band's minimum (\"jammed\").\n2. `points = lo + Math.round((hi − lo) × q / 100) × 100`, clamped to [lo, hi]. Round Kick and Upper Punch therefore reach 600 only when q ≥ 0.84 (clean). The 500–1000 techniques always reach a full point when they land; their risk is in their long startup and recovery.\n3. **Counter:** if the defender is in a technique's startup, active or recovery frames, or in blocked recoil, then `points = min(1000, points × TUNE.counterMul (2.0))`. Tag the hit \"COUNTER!\".\n4. **Full point** if `points ≥ TUNE.fullAt` (600), worth 1.0 toward winning the round. Otherwise a **half point**, worth 0.5.\n5. Add the points to the scorer's arcade score. These rules are deterministic, with no RNG, which matters for tests and netplay.\n- Examples: a clean Round Kick (q = 1) gives 600, a full point; a sloppy one (q = 0.6) gives 500, a half. A clean Front Kick counter gives 200 × 2 = 400, still a half. A clean Back Kick counter gives 400 × 2 = 800, a full point.\n\n### 4.6 Simultaneous hits (*aiuchi*)\nOn the first valid hit, keep simulating for **6 steps (0.05 s)** before freezing. If the other fighter also lands in that window, the call is **\"AIUCHI! NO POINT\"**. Nobody scores, and both fighters reset.\n\n### 4.7 Hit reactions\n- Half point: the defender staggers back 10 u and holds the hurt pose.\n- Full point or any sweep: the defender is knocked down. Pass `down: 0..1` to the rig, slide back 18 u and lie still. The attacker holds its **kime** pose (the technique's end pose, extended by `hold`) until \"STOP!\".\n- The Bout's own hit-stop is `TUNE.hitstop` (0.10 s). **Do not use `game.freeze`**: the engine's hit-stop skips the scene's `update`, which would stall networking (§12.6). Full points also get 0.35 s of 0.35× slow motion on `game.timeScale` (skipped under reduce motion, and never on an online host: use a visual effect only).\n\n### 4.8 Facing and cross-ups\n- A fighter turns to face its foe when it is **free and grounded**, with a 6-frame pivot animation. During its own technique it never turns.\n- If a foe flips over you while you are mid-technique, you are open from behind, and their hit scores the counter ×2.\n- The `←` (spin) family always strikes **toward the foe** by turning through: Back Kick turns 180° and drives the heel; Turn Back Kick and Back Round Kick turn a full 360°. It is a high-risk, high-value family.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Bout rules and the referee\n\n### 5.1 Terms\n- **Exchange:** from \"BEGIN!\" until \"STOP!\".\n- **Round:** a 30 s clock (`TUNE.clock`); the first to `TUNE.pointsToWin` (2.0) points wins it.\n- **Match:** best of 3 rounds (`roundsToWin` 2) in story and versus. Online and local Kachinuki bouts are a single round.\n\n### 5.2 Bout state machine (`KC.Bout`, deterministic; all timings × `TUNE.refPace`)\n\n| Phase | What happens | Duration |\n|---|---|---|\n| `intro` | Match start only: both walk in from the arena edges to their marks and face each other | 1.2 s |\n| `rei` | Referee \"BOW!\"; both bow (the `bow` pose) | 0.9 s |\n| `card` | \"ROUND n\" / \"DECIDING ROUND!\" card with a gong | 0.9 s |\n| `ready` | Both stand in yoi/kamae; the referee is centred behind them | 0.4 s |\n| `hajime` | \"BEGIN!\" with the referee's downward chop; control starts at t = 0.35 s | 0.6 s |\n| `fight` | Clock runs; detect hits, blocks and aiuchi. Soft-lock guard: being in `fight` longer than clock + 5 s raises an error | ≤ clock |\n| `yame` | After a scoring hit (or aiuchi): hit-stop, then the referee steps in, \"STOP!\", both frozen, the defender's reaction plays | 0.9 s |\n| `award` | Referee: \"WHITE!/RED!\" plus \"FULL POINT!/HALF POINT!\" with the flag signal; a points popup (\"ROUND KICK • 600\"); the scoreboard updates. Or \"NO POINT!\" for aiuchi | 1.2 s |\n| `reset` | Both auto-walk to their marks at 2× speed; skippable with `start` after 0.6 s of `award` | ≤ 1.2 s |\n| `roundEnd` | \"WHITE WINS!\" (or red); winner pose (fist up + kiai), loser kneels | 2.0 s |\n| `time` | Clock at 0: \"TIME!\". The leader on points wins; on a tie go to `hantei` | 0.8 s |\n| `hantei` | \"JUDGES!\" whistle. The two corner judges and the referee raise flags one by one, 0.5 s apart; the majority wins | 2.4 s |\n| `matchEnd` | Back to the marks, \"BOW!\", the referee raises the winner's hand; then `onMatchEnd(winnerSide, stats)` | 2.5 s |\n\n- The clock runs only during `fight`. It shows `ceil(clock)`.\n- Time bonus: on a round win, +100 for each whole second left (the source's rule).\n- **Hantei votes** (deterministic, seeded by the bout seed). Each judge scores a side as `techPts + 60 × attempts + 40 × blocks + noise`, where:\n  - `techPts` = raw points from scoring hits this round;\n  - `attempts` = techniques thrown toward the foe with the foe within max reach;\n  - `noise` = ±120 from that judge's `E.rng` stream.\n  - The flags fall for the higher score; a tie goes to the side that scored last, then to white.\n- **Match point** call when a side reaches `pointsToWin − 0.5` or more. **Perfect** (no points conceded in the match): +5,000.\n- Event bus: `bout.on('point' | 'call' | 'block' | 'whiff' | 'roundEnd' | 'matchEnd', fn)`. The HUD, audio, crowd, story and netcode all listen to it.\n\n### 5.3 Referee and judges (`KC.Referee`)\n- **Judge Genkotsu:** an older referee in a navy blazer (`outfit: 'coat'`), grey trousers, white shirt and red bow tie (trim). He is bald with a white stubble colour, and a white beard is drawn as an overlay at the head joint. Size 2.1, standing at `x = mid, y = +14` (behind the fighters, as in the source). In the 2.5D view he stands upstage.\n- **Corner judges:** two smaller officials at `x = 18` and `x = 382`, `y = +34`, holding a red and a white flag (drawn from `rig.hand('L'/'R')` as small poles with cloth that waves).\n- **Signals:**\n\n| Call | Gesture |\n|---|---|\n| hajime | Both hands chop down and apart |\n| yame | One open hand raised between the fighters |\n| waza-ari (half point) | Arm 45° down toward the scorer's side |\n| ippon (full point) | Arm raised high toward the scorer's side |\n| aiuchi | Fists knocked together |\n| hantei | Whistle, then point at each judge |\n| winner | Lift the winner's arm |\n| rei | Bow |\n\n- **Calls:** a voice (gibberish, §11.3) plus a subtitle banner in the title font. The **Referee calls** setting chooses English or the Japanese terms:\n\n| id | English | Japanese |\n|---|---|---|\n| begin | BEGIN! | HAJIME! |\n| stop | STOP! | YAME! |\n| white / red | WHITE! / RED! | SHIRO! / AKA! |\n| half | HALF POINT! | WAZA-ARI! |\n| full | FULL POINT! | IPPON! |\n| nopoint | NO POINT! | TORIMASEN! |\n| aiuchi | AIUCHI! | AIUCHI! |\n| time | TIME! | SOREMADE! |\n| judges | JUDGES! | HANTEI! |\n| whiteWins / redWins | WHITE WINS! / RED WINS! | SHIRO NO KACHI! / AKA NO KACHI! |\n| bow | BOW! | REI! |\n| decide | DECIDING ROUND! | SHOBU! |\n| forfeit | FORFEIT! | KIKEN! |\n| matchPoint | MATCH POINT! | MATCH POINT! |\n\n### 5.4 Fight HUD (drawn on the canvas in `r.overlay`, anchored to `r.W`/`r.H`)\n- **Top centre:** the clock in an `E.ui.box`, title font at scale 2.\n- **Top left (WHITE) and top right (RED):** the name, a belt icon (story) or player-colour tag, and **point slots**: `pointsToWin` circles, where a half point half-fills a circle and a full point fills it. Under them, the round pips.\n- **Bottom left:** \"1P 0123400\" score; **bottom right:** \"HI 0250000\".\n- **Centre:** calls in the title font (`scale: 3`, colours `#fff6d0 #ffd060 #e8782a`).\n- **Over the scorer:** a points popup (`particles.text`), with the technique name when *Technique names* is on.\n- **Hints** (story matches 1–3, *Tutorial hints* on): one line at the bottom, kept clear of `touchArea()`.\n- **Input display** (setting or dev): the last 12 inputs as glyph pairs.\n- The \"DEV\" badge shows while any tuning value is non-default.\n\n---\n\n## 6. CPU AI (`KC.KarateAI`)\n\n### 6.1 Architecture\n- Output: the **same control intent a human produces**, `{ posture: 'n'|'u'|'d'|'l'|'r', techPress: null|'u'|'d'|'l'|'r' }` in **screen** directions. `Karateka.control()` converts it to toward/away, so the AI obeys exactly the same rules as a player. The same class is the **Autopilot** for tests and the attract mode.\n- **Perception with reaction delay:** each step, push an observation of the foe (technique id and phase, airborne, crouching, down, gap, facing). The AI acts on the observation that is `react` seconds old.\n- **Decision tick** every `think` seconds (plus jitter from its `E.rng`), by priority:\n  1. **Punish:** the foe is in recovery or blocked recoil within reach, so pick the best technique that reaches (prefer full-capable ones).\n  2. **React** (probability `readP`) to a seen technique:\n     - a low technique → `jump` or `jumpSide` (if aggressive), or `frontFlip`;\n     - a high one → `crouch`, or `squatPunch` (ducks and strikes);\n     - a mid one → `block` (probability `block`), or `backFlip` if the gap allows;\n     - the foe airborne and approaching → `upperPunch` if within 0–30, else walk back.\n  3. **Proactive:** spacing toward `gap`. When in range of a technique in its personality's `weights`, throw it (weighted pick among the techniques whose reach band contains the current gap) with probability `aggression`. Otherwise feint (a short walk in or out) with probability `patience`.\n  4. **Mistakes:** with probability `mistake`, choose a random technique.\n- **Habit tracker** (level ≥ 8): count the foe's techniques this match. Pre-load the counter to its top two (for example, `jump` vs `frontSweep`, `crouch` vs `roundKick`).\n- **Level curve** (L = 1–10): `react = lerp(0.55, 0.12, (L−1)/9)`, `think = lerp(0.50, 0.14)`, `readP = lerp(0.25, 0.92)`, `mistake = lerp(0.30, 0.02)`. Difficulty Easy = L−2 (min 1) with react ×1.25; Hard = L+1 (max 10) with react ×0.85.\n- **Determinism:** all randomness comes from the AI's own `E.rng(seed)`. Same seed, same inputs, same match: the tests hash it.\n- **Showcase mode** (attract): cycle through the technique list in order (each in its range), use perfect reads and react 0.1 s. The director can force outcomes (§13).\n\n### 6.2 Personalities\n\n| Fighter | L | aggr | patience | jump | block | gap | weights (technique: weight) | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| KAMABOKO | 1 | .7 | .2 | .05 | .10 | 30 | frontKick 3, lowKick 2, revPunch 2, roundKick 1 | clumsy |\n| SENPAI NEGI | 2 | .5 | .4 | .10 | .35 | 36 | roundKick 2, frontKick 2, backKick 2, midLunge 1 | textbook |\n| EBI | 3 | .6 | .2 | .35 | .20 | 70 | jumpSide 4, frontFlip 2, lowKick 1, roundKick 1 | jumper |\n| KIKURAGE | 4 | .25 | .8 | .05 | .65 | 40 | revPunch 3, upperPunch 3, squatPunch 2, backSweep 1 | counter-puncher |\n| IKA | 5 | .5 | .3 | .25 | .30 | 44 | backRound 3, turnBackKick 3, backKick 2, frontFlip 2, backFlip 2 | cross-ups |\n| CAPTAIN BUTA | 6 | .8 | .2 | .05 | .20 | 60 | upLunge 4, midLunge 3, frontSweep 2, roundKick 1 | walk 60, reach ×1.1 |\n| ASARI | 7 | .4 | .5 | .05 | .50 | 34 | squatPunch 3, backSweep 3, frontSweep 2, lowKick 2, backRoll 2 | crouches 50% |\n| MASTER KYABETSU | 8 | .4 | .7 | .10 | .60 | 42 | roundKick 2, revPunch 2, upperPunch 2, turnBackKick 1 | readP +.05, habit tracker |\n| MEI | 9 | .65 | .4 | .20 | .50 | 46 | jumpSide 3, roundKick 3, frontSweep 3, turnBackKick 2, backRound 2 | habit tracker, mixes high and low |\n\n### 6.3 Balance targets (asserted by `ai.test`, §18)\n- An Autopilot at L5 beats CPU L1–3 in ≥ 90% of seeded matches, L5 in 40–60%, and L9 in ≤ 25%.\n- In CPU-vs-CPU matches at equal level, the mean round length is 15–45 s.\n- Across 60 seeded CPU-vs-CPU matches, every scoring technique lands at least once, no technique has more than 35% of the scoring hits, and full points are 30–60% of scoring hits.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Content\n\n### 7.1 Roster\nGi colours: **white side** cloth and pants `#f2eee4`; **red side** `#c8302a`. Fighters are barefoot (`boot` = skin) and bare-handed (`glove` = skin). Belts: white `#f4f4ee`, yellow `#f0c830`, green `#3aa050`, blue `#2f5fd0`, brown `#6a4024`, black `#16161c`, Champ gold `#e8c040`. Every rig sets `colors` fully; never leave defaults.\n\n| id | Name / title | Rig (beyond the gi) | Stats (walk, reach, speed) | Voice (f0 Hz, formant ×, syllables/s, wave) | Intro line |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `taro` | TARO MOYASHI, the hero | heroic, size 2.15, `hair: 'spiky'` black, white headband (`hat: { style: 'band' }`), slim (`limbW` −10%) | 1, 1, 1 | 190, 1.05, 15, pulse | \"I WILL BE CHAMP!\" |\n| `kamaboko` | KAMABOKO, dojo classmate | default (chibi) build, size 2.0, round, pink-swirl headband `#ff7aa8` | .95, .92, .95 | 240, 1.15, 17, pulse | \"I ATE THREE BOWLS. I AM READY!\" |\n| `negi` | SENPAI NEGI, dojo senior | heroic, size 2.35, `hair: 'ponytail'`, green headband `#3aa050` | 1, 1.04, 1 | 150, 1.0, 13, saw | \"SHOW ME WHAT I TAUGHT YOU.\" |\n| `ebi` | EBI, harbor fisherman | heroic lean, size 2.15, orange bandana, tanned `#c98a5a` | 1.1, .98, 1.05 | 210, 1.08, 19, pulse | \"THE SEA MADE ME QUICK. CATCH ME!\" |\n| `kikurage` | KIKURAGE, bamboo hermit | `hunch .3`, hood (brown), beard overlay, size 2.1 | .9, 1, .95 | 110, .92, 9, saw + breath | \"THE BAMBOO BENDS. IT DOES NOT BREAK.\" |\n| `ika` | IKA, log-bridge acrobat | heroic, `hair: 'long'` white, long sleeves, pale violet skin tint `#e8d8f0` | 1.05, 1.02, 1.02 | 200, 1.1, 14, triangle + vibrato | \"EIGHT ARMS. TWO LEGS. WATCH THEM ALL.\" |\n| `buta` | CAPTAIN BUTA, ship's stern | `build: 'bulky'`, size 2.6, navy sailor cap (`hat: 'cap'`), stubble | .83, 1.1, .92 | 85, .85, 11, saw | \"WELCOME ABOARD. NOW WALK THE PLANK!\" |\n| `asari` | ASARI, night-market stall keeper | heroic, size 2.1, hair bun (`'ponytail'` short), teal headband | 1, .96, 1.04 | 170, 1.0, 15, square + clicks | \"MY SHELL IS SHUT. GO HOME.\" |\n| `kyabetsu` | MASTER KYABETSU, Fuji garden | `hair: 'bald'`, white beard overlay, `hunch .2`, size 2.15 | .95, 1.02, 1.03 | 120, .9, 10, saw + vibrato 5 Hz | \"MANY LAYERS, YOUNG ONE. PEEL THEM.\" |\n| `mei` | MEI, \"THE IRON LADLE\" (reigning champ) | heroic, size 2.15, `hair: 'ponytail'` black, gold belt; in a fight she wears her side's gi colour (red in the story final); an apron outfit in cutscenes | 1.05, 1, 1.05 | 290, 1.18, 16, pulse12 | \"THE GOLDEN BOWL STAYS IN MY SHOP.\" |\n| `genkotsu` | JUDGE GENKOTSU (referee, not playable) | §5.3 | — | 105, .9, 11, saw, rough .18 | — |\n\nStats are small, visible modifiers (3 bars on the select screen). **Signature technique:** +0.1 quality on one technique each (taro `roundKick`, kamaboko `frontKick`, negi `backKick`, ebi `jumpSide`, kikurage `revPunch`, ika `backRound`, buta `upLunge`, asari `squatPunch`, kyabetsu `turnBackKick`, mei `frontSweep`).\n\n**Unlocks** (versus): taro through asari are available from the start. Kyabetsu unlocks on reaching match 8 in story, and Mei on finishing story. The dev mode has \"Unlock all\".\n\n### 7.2 Stages (8 fight stages and 3 bonus locations)\nEach stage is data (`KC.DATA.stages`):\n- the backdrop (sky gradient and layers, using EC6's custom layers);\n- the floor painter (a material and colours);\n- 3–8 props;\n- lights (ambient and point lights);\n- the crowd;\n- weather and ambient particles;\n- an ambience loop;\n- the music id.\n\nThe arena is always `x 0–400`. Paint the world from −140 to 540, so a 16:9 screen (427 px wide) never shows emptiness.\n\n| id | Stage | Time / light | Floor | Backdrop layers | Props and life | Music |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `dojo` | HON-DŌJŌ (Senpai Negi's dojo) | evening, warm interior, ambient .55 | polished planks (`E.MATERIALS.planks`, light oak) with tatami at the sides | shoji wall with warm backlight, wooden beams, kamidana shelf | hanging paper lanterns (light), calligraphy scrolls (kanji sprites 空 手 道), weapon rack (bo staffs), taiko drum, classmates kneeling in seiza along the back wall (crowd) | `dojo` |\n| `harbor` | NAGASAKI PIER | dawn, ambient .7 warm | weathered deck planks | `sea` layer, lighthouse, moored boat silhouettes, distant hills, sunrise | crates, barrels (`kaykit…barrel-small-stack`), rope coils, rowboat (`kenney-pirate:boat-row-large`), **Mei's noodle cart** (custom) with steam, gull sprites, dock workers behind a rope | `harbor` |\n| `bamboo` | BAMBOO FOREST | midday shade, ambient .6, light shafts | dirt with fallen leaves | 3 parallax depths of bamboo stalks (custom layer), canopy | stone lantern (tōrō, custom), small shrine, `weather: leaves`, birdsong; no crowd | `bamboo` |\n| `log` | LOG BRIDGE (a tree trunk over a gorge) | afternoon, ambient .6, mist | the log's top in bark material, with the gorge visible below the floor line | cliffs (`mountains` in rock tones), a waterfall (animated custom layer), pines, a fog layer | vines, rope rails, mist particles; no crowd | `gorge` |\n| `ship` | SHIP'S STERN | sunset, ambient .55 orange | deck planks | sea with sunset glints, distant coast | mast and rigging (custom), ship's wheel (custom), lanterns (light), cannon (`kenney-pirate:cannon`), barrels, a cheering crew (crowd); a 0.5 px roll sway (off under reduce motion) | `ship` |\n| `market` | CHAMPON STREET (night market) | night, ambient .35 | stone pavement (`flagstone`) | `city-night` preset with hills | red paper lanterns in rows (light), stalls (`kenney-fantasy-town:stall-red`), the **CHAMPON-TEI** shop front with a noren curtain (custom), steam (smoke particles), shoppers (crowd) | `market` |\n| `fuji` | FUJI GARDEN | dusk, ambient .6 pink | raked gravel (custom texture) | Mount Fuji (custom cone with a snow cap), pagoda silhouette, torii gate (custom), cherry trees | stone lanterns, koi pond edge, falling petals (pink particles), garden visitors (small crowd) | `fuji` |","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1258}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01C2fUK1d4hNajwAxYpPjRyo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
