{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014P79Crabix8GzamdjQj1NZ","type":"tool_result","content":"400\t| `fuji` | FUJI GARDEN | dusk, ambient .6 pink | raked gravel (custom texture) | Mount Fuji (custom cone with a snow cap), pagoda silhouette, torii gate (custom), cherry trees | stone lanterns, koi pond edge, falling petals (pink particles), garden visitors (small crowd) | `fuji` |\n401\t| `stadium` | NATIONAL STADIUM (final, and the default online stage) | indoor night, ambient .45 | blue competition mat with red and white start lines | tiered crowd (dense), banners \"CHAMPON CUP '84\", big scoreboard (custom, glowing) | spotlight cones on the mat, camera flashes in the crowd, judges' chairs (core `chair`), a trophy table holding the **Golden Bowl** | `stadium` |\n402\t| `courtyard` (bonus) | DOJO COURTYARD | morning | packed earth | dojo wall, sky, pine | cinder blocks and board stacks (custom), a water barrel | `bonus` |\n403\t| `fishmarket` (bonus) | FISH-MARKET ALLEY | morning | wet stone | market roofs, harbor beyond | fish crates, ice boxes, hanging signs | `bonus` |\n404\t| `pasture` (bonus) | FUJI PASTURE | noon | grass | Fuji, hills, clouds | fences (`fence`), hay bales (`kenney-graveyard:hay-bale-bundled`), a farmer (crowd of 1) | `bonus` |\n405\t\n406\tCrowds: back rows are cheap kit-style silhouettes (rectangle plus disc, 2–3 parallax rows, bobbing harder on `cheer`, as in the FIGHTING kit's `stage()`). The front row is 4–8 `E.Humanoid({ style: 'classic', size: 1.0 })` drawn `every: 2`, in wave and cheer poses. **Mei stands in the front row of every story stage** after the harbor (an HD rig that cheers on the player's points). Crowd reactions come from the bout bus: \"ooh\" on blocks, a cheer swell on points, a big swell on full points.\n407\t\n408\t### 7.3 Champon Road (the story ladder, `KC.DATA.ladder`)\n409\t\n410\t| # | Node | Content | On success |\n411\t|---|---|---|---|\n412\t| 1 | cutscene `prologue` | Harbor title cards plus Taro and Negi lines | — |\n413\t| 2 | practice `lessonI` (dojo) | 7 drills: frontKick, roundKick, lowKick, backKick, block, jump, crouch | — |\n414\t| 3 | match vs **KAMABOKO** (dojo) | hints on | **YELLOW BELT** |\n415\t| 4 | match vs **SENPAI NEGI** (dojo) | | **GREEN BELT** |\n416\t| 5 | bonus **BREAK THE BOARDS** (courtyard) | 5 pine boards | — |\n417\t| 6 | cutscene `harborMei` and match vs **EBI** (harbor) | Mei introduced | **BLUE BELT** |\n418\t| 7 | practice `lessonII` (harbor pier) | 6 drills: midLunge, upLunge, revPunch (get close), frontSweep, upperPunch, squatPunch | — |\n419\t| 8 | match vs **KIKURAGE** (bamboo) | | **BROWN BELT** |\n420\t| 9 | bonus **POTS AHOY!** (fish market) | 16 objects | — |\n421\t| 10 | match vs **IKA** (log bridge) | | **BLACK BELT, 1ST DAN** |\n422\t| 11 | practice `lessonIII` (log bridge) | 8 drills: backRound, turnBackKick, backSweep, jumpSide, jumpBack, frontFlip, backFlip, backRoll | — |\n423\t| 12 | match vs **CAPTAIN BUTA** (ship) | | **3RD DAN** |\n424\t| 13 | bonus **STOP THE BULL** (pasture) | 3 charges | — |\n425\t| 14 | match vs **ASARI** (market) and a Mei cutscene | | **5TH DAN** |\n426\t| 15 | match vs **MASTER KYABETSU** (fuji) | | **8TH DAN** |\n427\t| 16 | cutscene `finalEntrance` and match vs **MEI** (stadium) | | **CHAMP** |\n428\t| 17 | cutscene `ending`, credits parade, initials | | back to the title |\n429\t\n430\t- Before each match: the **Champon Road** screen (§8.3), then the **VS card**. After each win: the **promotion ceremony**, then the road again.\n431\t- On a match loss: **CONTINUE?** (10 s countdown). Continuing retries the match with the score reset to 0 and the rank kept (arcade rules). Declining or timing out goes to **GAME OVER**, then initials if the score qualifies, then the title. After 2 lost rounds against the same opponent, a **tip card** names its habit and the counter technique (from the roster `tip` field), and after a lost match it offers \"LOWER DIFFICULTY FOR THIS MATCH?\" (an assist).\n432\t- **Scoring:** technique points, the time bonus, bonus-stage points, drill points (200/400), and a rank-up bonus of +1,000 × the new rank index. A match with no points conceded is PERFECT: +5,000.\n433\t- Autosave (`game.saves.autosave()`) after each completed node: `{ node, rank, score, difficulty, unlocks, stats }`.\n434\t\n435\t### 7.4 Bonus stages\n436\tAll three run in the `bonus` scene with the hero's `Karateka` and the same twin-stick input. Their numbers live in `KC.DATA.bonus` and the dev sliders.\n437\t- **BREAK THE BOARDS (Tameshiwari)**\n438\t  - A stack of N boards on two cinder blocks (story: 5 pine boards; Bonus Games: choose 3, 5, 7 or 10, of pine, roof tile ×1.5 or ice ×2).\n439\t  - **Hold R↓ (or ✕, or K)** to breathe in. The *KI* gauge beside the fighter rises at 55%/s with a ±6% sine wobble at 2.2 Hz. Holding above 100% for 0.3 s overcharges it (\"TOO MUCH!\") and drops it to 60%.\n440\t  - **Release to chop** (a downward hammer-fist). Power is the gauge value. Boards broken = `min(N, floor(power/100 × (N + 0.99)))`. Power ≥ 95 is **PERFECT**: all boards plus 2,000.\n441\t  - Each board is worth 200 × the material multiplier.\n442\t  - Presentation: 0.3× slow motion for 0.4 s, the boards split top to bottom with halves flying (`particles.bits` in wood tones), dust and a shake. Judge Genkotsu calls \"PERFECT!\" or \"WEAK!\".\n443\t- **POTS AHOY! (Evade)**\n444\t  - 16 objects thrown from the left and right edges. The interval falls from 1.4 s to 0.8 s and the speed rises from 150 to 230 u/s.\n445\t  - Lanes: HIGH at z ≈ 52, MID ≈ 36, LOW ≈ 10 (tune to the rig: head, chest, shins).\n446\t  - Telegraph 0.7 s ahead: an edge arrow plus a height marker. The fighter turns to face the incoming side.\n447\t  - Objects:\n448\t    - **clay pot**: break it with any technique of its height (R↑ high, R→ mid, R↓ low);\n449\t    - **bonsai pot**: kicks only;\n450\t    - **boulder**: cannot be broken; dodge it (jump the LOW one, crouch the HIGH one, back somersault the MID one);\n451\t    - **steaming champon bowl**: **catch it with Block** for +1,000 (\"NICE CATCH!\"); breaking it costs −500 (\"MOTTAINAI!\"); letting it pass splashes for 0.\n452\t  - **The stage ends on the first hit** (as in the source). 200 per break; handling all 16 is PERFECT +3,000. The 16-object sequence is fixed per visit, seeded by visit number.\n453\t- **STOP THE BULL**\n454\t  - A custom quadruped bull (§9.4). Three charges at 150, 190 and 240 u/s.\n455\t  - Telegraph: paws the ground twice and snorts (1.2 s), lowers its head, then charges from the right.\n456\t  - **Strike window:** the bull's head is 8–40 u from your front edge during the active frames of a **valid technique** (`upperPunch, upLunge, squatPunch, frontKick, backKick`). Success is \"THUD\": the bull stops dizzy (stars, 1.5 s) for **3,000**, plus 1,000 for PERFECT within ±6 u of the window's centre. It then walks off and charges again.\n457\t  - Any other technique, or bad timing, gets you **trampled** (a comic tumble arc) and ends the stage.\n458\t\n459\t### 7.5 Practice (Senpai's drill, `KC.Drill`)\n460\t- Senpai stands on the right facing the player. The drill card at the top reads \"LESSON I-3: LOW KICK\" with the prompt icons for the current device.\n461\t- Loop: \"WATCH.\" Senpai performs the technique into empty air. \"NOW YOU.\" The player has 4 s:\n462\t  - the **matching technique** scores \"GOOD!\" (+200), or \"SHARP!\" (+400) if it starts within 50 frames;\n463\t  - a **wrong technique** gets \"NO. WATCH AGAIN.\" and the demo repeats with the prompt blinking;\n464\t  - after **3 misses**, a hint appears (\"HOLD L ↓, THEN PUSH R ↑\" with animated stick icons), and the drill waits for success.\n465\t- `start` skips a drill (no points). At the end: \"LESSON COMPLETE 6/7\", Senpai nods, and the points are added.\n466\t- Lesson sets: I (7), II (6), III (8); the All Techniques set has 21 drills (§7.3).\n467\t- **Free Spar** (DOJO menu): the hero vs a dummy fighter (modes stand, crouch, jump, block, attack L3). Hit and hurt boxes, frame data and the input display are on, and the bout's referee is off (hits just flash and show points).\n468\t\n469\t### 7.6 Items and objects\n470\tBelts (the rank shown on the rig), the **Golden Bowl** trophy (a stadium prop and the ending), bonus objects (boards, roof tiles, ice blocks, clay pots, bonsai pots, boulders, champon bowls), the bull, and unlockables (2 versus characters plus \"Classic palette\": a white/red NES-like recolour of the fighters, unlocked by finishing the story).\n471\t\n472\t### 7.7 Story beats\n473\tCutscenes are scripted with `E.Dialog` and portraits (`rig.drawPortrait`), with gibberish voices. Every line is in Appendix A. A **VS card** precedes each match: split panels, white on the left and red on the right, names, the belt or rank, the stage name, and the opponent's taunt typed with its voice, for 2.5 s. **Promotions:** Judge Genkotsu presents the belt; the belt icon flies to the waist with glints; \"PROMOTED! BLUE BELT\"; a fanfare; the rank road (one row of 10 icons, from white belt to Champ) highlights the new one. **Ending:** Mei hands over the Golden Bowl. Then the cast parade, each fighter bowing with their name; \"YOU ARE THE KARATE CHAMPON\"; credits; initials.\n474\t\n475\t---\n476\t\n477\t## 8. Modes and screens (flow)\n478\t\n479\t```\n480\tboot → title ─(10 s idle)→ attract ─(any input)→ title\n481\ttitle ─start→ mainMenu → { road → [vsCard → fight → promotion | continue → gameOver → initials] / bonus / practice / cutscene … → ending → credits → initials → title,\n482\t                           versusSetup → select → stageSelect → fight → results,\n483\t                           dojoMenu → practice / freeSpar / moveList,\n484\t                           bonusMenu → bonus → initials,\n485\t                           records, options (engine settings), about }\n486\tAI Hub session (AIHub.active): sessionCard → sessionFight (kachinuki) → sessionOver   (no title, menus or attract)\n487\t```\n488\t\n489\t### 8.1 Title\n490\t- The dojo at night under lanterns. Taro (white) and Mei (red) face each other in kamae and bow every few seconds.\n491\t- Logo: `E.font.title('KARATE', …)` over `'CHAMPON'` at scale 4, colours `#fffbe8 #ffe08a #ffb347 #e0662a`, depth 3. The **O** of CHAMPON is drawn as a noodle bowl with chopsticks and rising steam.\n492\t- \"PRESS START\" or \"PRESS ✕\" (glyph icon by device) blinks. The version line comes from `E.versionLabel()` plus the game version.\n493\t- Main menu items: CONTINUE (only with an autosave, showing \"5TH DAN · NIGHT MARKET\"), CHAMPON ROAD, VERSUS, DOJO, BONUS GAMES, RECORDS, OPTIONS, ABOUT. A live vignette on the right side of the screen changes with the focused item.\n494\t\n495\t### 8.2 Character select (versus)\n496\t- A 5×2 portrait grid (`rig.drawPortrait`). Locked fighters are dark silhouettes marked \"?\".\n497\t- The P1 cursor is white and the P2 cursor red; a CPU cursor shows \"CPU\".\n498\t- A big portrait on each side shows the name, the style line (from the personality) and 3 stat bars. Hover or click picks with the mouse.\n499\t- Engine `E.FightSelect` is a reference, but write `KC.Select` for the grid, locks, CPU level choice and the mouse.\n500\t\n501\t### 8.3 Champon Road screen\n502\t- A horizontal painted scroll: the road from the dojo to the stadium with 8 location stops (the dojo holds 2 matches) and the 3 bonus icons between them. Visited stops are stamped with a belt icon, and the next stop pulses.\n503\t- It shows \"NEXT: BAMBOO FOREST — KIKURAGE\", the current belt, the score, and `✕ GO`. The camera pans along the road. Bonus stops appear as small icons (boards, pot, bull).\n504\t\n505\t### 8.4 Results (versus)\n506\tThe winner's pose, then \"WHITE WINS 2–1\" and 4 big stat lines: full points, half points, blocks, best technique. Menu: REMATCH / CHARACTER SELECT / STAGE SELECT / MAIN MENU.\n507\t\n508\t### 8.5 Stage select\n509\tThe real stage renders behind the selector. Left and right change it, and it shows the name and a \"← n/8 →\" label. RANDOM is an option.\n510\t\n511\t### 8.6 Move List (pause item and DOJO)\n512\t- One posture row at a time: the left side shows the **L-stick icon** for the selected posture (cycled with up/down or by holding the left stick). Around a big **R-stick icon**, the 4 techniques appear in their directions.\n513\t- Pushing R previews the technique: a live rig in a box plays it, with its name, points range and a \"FULL\" star. This replaces a dense 20-cell table.\n514\t\n515\t### 8.7 Continue, game over and initials\n516\t- \"CONTINUE?\" with a big 9…0 countdown and the `confirm` glyph; Mei says \"GET UP, MOYASHI!\".\n517\t- Game over: Taro kneels in the rain; Genkotsu says \"TRAIN. RETURN.\"\n518\t- Initials use `E.Arcade` (the arcade module) letter wheel: `cab.enter(score, 'story', done)`, with tables per mode: `'story'`, `'boards'`, `'pots'`, `'bull'`.\n519\t\n520\t### 8.8 Records\n521\tOne table per page (L1/R1, Q/E or click the tabs). 10 rows of `NAME SCORE` plus the rank reached (story) or the bonus best.\n522\t\n523\t### 8.9 Pause menu\n524\tThe engine standard menu, plus `menu.items` given **as a function** (the engine evaluates it; falsy entries are skipped — verified in `E.Shell`):\n525\t```js\n526\titems: () => [['Move list', openMoveList], canRetry() && ['Retry match', retryMatch], ['Dev mode', openDev]]\n527\t```\n528\tQuit to title goes to `'title'`. In an AI Hub session there is no Quit (`quit: false`) and no Retry.\n529\t\n530\t### 8.10 Options (game settings beside the engine's standard ones)\n531\t| key | label | values (default first) |\n532\t|---|---|---|\n533\t| `difficulty` | Difficulty | Normal, Easy, Hard |\n534\t| `calls` | Referee calls | English, Japanese |\n535\t| `hints` | Tutorial hints | on, off |\n536\t| `camera` | Fight camera | Arcade side, Tatami 2.5D |\n537\t| `techNames` | Technique names | on, off |\n538\t| `inputDisplay` | Input display | off, on |\n539\t| `palette` | Fighter palette | Modern, Classic (once unlocked) |\n540\t| (engine) | Button symbols | **PlayStation** (via EC2), Xbox, Auto, Nintendo, Positions |\n541\t| (EC5) | Voices volume | 0–100%, default 80 |\n542\t\n543\t### 8.11 About / Credits\n544\tPaged, 10-foot readable, reached from the main menu and the credits:\n545\t1. **SOURCE INFORMATION** (the 15 fields TASK.md lists);\n546\t2. **TESTING FEEDBACK** (4 answers);\n547\t3. **ENGINE FEEDBACK** (6);\n548\t4. **PROMPT FEEDBACK** (4);\n549\t5. **CREDITS**.\n550\t\n551\tThe text comes from one data file shared with `exit-interview.json` (§19).\n552\t\n553\t---\n554\t\n555\t## 9. Art direction and assets\n556\t\n557\t### 9.1 Look\n558\t- Use the engine's native HD-rig pixel style. Apply every step of the API card's **Look target**:\n559\t  - `res 'ps1'` with `expand`;\n560\t  - dressed characters with full palettes;\n561\t  - a backdrop behind every scene;\n562\t  - textured floors;\n563\t  - 3–8 props per screen with `propSize: 1.4`;\n564\t  - effects on every hit;\n565\t  - `E.tones` shading for custom art;\n566\t  - title-font logos;\n567\t  - lights in every scene;\n568\t  - portraits in dialogue.\n569\t- Compare screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug quality, not NES.\n570\t- Karate Champ flavour comes from big side-on fighters, white vs red, the referee centred behind them, scenic Japanese settings, and a minimal HUD of point icons.\n571\t\n572\t### 9.2 Effects\n573\t- **Scoring hit:** an impact star (`particles.impact`) at the contact point and sparks (yellow `#ffe070` for full, white for half), a shake (2 for half, 4 for full), `game.flash('#ffffff', .08)` on full only (the engine respects comfort settings), a gamepad rumble, and a gi-snap dust puff.\n574\t- Block: blue sparks. Whiff: a faint `rig.drawSmear` trail on kicks.\n575\t- Knockdown: `particles.dust` on landing.\n576\t- Sweeps: a dust arc. Flips: an afterimage `ghost` trail.\n577\t- Crowd camera flashes in the stadium.\n578\t- Full-point slow motion as in §4.7.\n579\t\n580\t### 9.3 New art to make (by code)\n581\t**(a) Shapes**: `E.SHAPES` entries, checked with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs kc-shapes.js#id`:\n582\t- `toro` (stone lantern: lathe, box and roof prism; a flame light);\n583\t- `torii` (2 cylinders, 2 beams, vermilion `#d8402a`);\n584\t- `noodleCart` (core `cart`, a `kenney-fantasy-town:stall-red` canopy, a lantern, a pot with steam);\n585\t- `shipWheel`;\n586\t- `mast`;\n587\t- `cinderBlock`;\n588\t- `boardStack` (params `n`, `mat`);\n589\t- `taiko` (lathe);\n590\t- `weaponRack`;\n591\t- `scoreboard` (glowing panel);\n592\t- `bonsaiPot`;\n593\t- `champonBowl` (`lathe` with a broth disc; `kenney-food:bowl-soup` is the reference);\n594\t- `goldenBowl` (gold material, on a plinth);\n595\t- `norenShopfront`.\n596\t\n597\t**(b) Custom backdrop layers** (EC6): bamboo stalks, pagoda, Mount Fuji, waterfall, boat silhouettes, lighthouse, ship rigging silhouette, stadium tiers.\n598\t\n599\t**(c) Sprites** (`E.sprite` strings): kanji 空 手 道 and 麺 (12–16 px) for scrolls and the shop sign; gulls (2 frames); koi; cherry petals; pots and boulders (with `rot` while flying); point icons (empty, half, full); belt icons; flags; the stars for a dizzy bull; the glyph badges (§3.3).\n600\t\n601\t**(d) Asset store:** cut a set with `node tools/asset-set.mjs` holding only:\n602\t- `kenney-pirate:boat-row-large`\n603\t- `kenney-pirate:cannon`\n604\t- `kaykit-dungeon-remastered:barrel-small-stack`\n605\t- `kenney-fantasy-town:stall-red`\n606\t- `kenney-food:bowl-soup`\n607\t- `kenney-food:steamer`\n608\t- `kenney-graveyard:hay-bale-bundled`\n609\t- `kenney-platformer:fence-rope`\n610\t- `kenney-nature:pot-small`\n611\t- `kenney-nature:crops-bamboo-stage-a`\n612\t\n613\tName the set `kc` and save it to `assets/kc-set.js`, then load it with `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS.kc)`. Credit Kenney and KayKit (CC0) in the About screen.\n614\t\n615\t### 9.4 The bull (custom creature, `KC.Bull`)\n616\t- Pose the parts in 3D and project them with `r.w(x, y, z)`, as Emberdeep's creatures do:\n617\t  - a torso of stacked shaded ellipses (`E.tones('#4a2e22')`);\n618\t  - neck and head (a box-like skull, a snout, a ring in the nose);\n619\t  - curved horns (`#e8dcc0`);\n620\t  - 4 legs as two-segment IK with hooves, on a gallop cycle (phase offsets 0, .5, .25, .75) and a walk cycle;\n621\t  - a tail pendulum;\n622\t  - eyes that turn red while charging;\n623\t  - nostril steam (smoke particles).\n624\t- Size: about 70 u long and 45 u at the shoulder.\n625\t- States: `idle`, `paw`, `charge`, `stopped` (dizzy stars), `trample`, `walkBack`.\n626\t- Draw with `r.queue` at its depth.\n627\t\n628\t### 9.5 Palette and lighting\n629\tEach stage defines `lights.ambient` and 2–10 lights per frame (lanterns, lamps, the spotlight cones in the stadium drawn as additive dithered polygons). Fight HUD colours are white `#fff6e8`, red `#ff5a4a` and gold `#ffd060`. Information is never carried by colour alone: the sides are always labelled WHITE and RED, and the point icons differ in shape (half vs full fill).\n630\t\n631\t---\n632\t\n633\t## 10. Animation\n634\t\n635\t### 10.1 Base system\n636\tThe engine's procedural `E.Humanoid` rig is the base: `rig.update(dt, state)` every step with continuous values, `E.Attack` timing, `E.MOVES` specs, poses and expressions. No sprite sheets. **Rule:** `Karateka.pose()` builds the rig input **only from serializable state** (state id, technique id, phase, `u`, facing, air, crouch, down, flags). Online guests, replays and the attract mode then pose identically (§12.6).\n637\t\n638\t### 10.2 New karate animations (EC7, or game-side rig post-processing)\n639\t- **Stances:**\n640\t  - `kamae` (a fighting stance: front foot forward, fists up; it replaces `stance: 'guard'`);\n641\t  - `yoi` (ready: fists down in front of the belt);\n642\t  - `bow` (rei: the torso bends about 30° for 0.5 s);\n643\t  - `seiza` (kneeling sitting on the heels: Kachinuki queues, the dojo crowd);\n644\t  - `kime` (a held technique end pose).\n645\t- **Techniques** (move specs added to `E.MOVES` as `kc_*`, or a game registry):\n646\t  - Front Kick: base `kick`, `z0 .6 z1 .9`, chambered.\n647\t  - Round Kick: base `roundhouse`.\n648\t  - Low Kick: `kick` with `z0 .1 z1 .25`.\n649\t  - Reverse Punch: base `cross`.\n650\t  - Lunge punches: `jab`/`cross` with `lunge` 5 plus body travel, `z` high or mid.\n651\t  - Upper Punch: base `uppercut`.\n652\t  - Squat Punch: `cross` plus `crouch .8`.\n653\t  - Sweeps: base `sweep` (the back sweep spins).\n654\t  - Jumping Side Kick: `flyingkick`, with the body sideways (yaw the rig facing by 0.4 rad) and the leg out.\n655\t  - Jumping Back Kick: in the air, the facing turns 180° and the heel drives back.\n656\t  - **Spins:** animate the rig's **facing angle continuously** (the rig is 3D; facing is an angle). Back Kick turns 0 → π during startup and strikes with the heel toward the foe (a back thrust spec, `a0 ≈ π` or a post-processed leg). Turn Back Kick and Back Round Kick turn a full 2π, hitting in the last quarter.\n657\t  - **Blocks:** high (forearm rises above the head), middle (forearm sweeps across the chest), low for drills only (sweeps down).\n658\t  - **Somersaults:** in the air, pass `flip: u` with a tucked pose. If `flip` does not read as a full airborne rotation, rotate every joint around the hip centre after `rig.solvePose()` (Emberdeep's dodge-roll technique).\n659\t  - **Back Roll:** `flip` on the ground.\n660\t- **Reactions:** stagger (half point), knocked flat with a slide (full point), swept (the legs fly up, then a fall on the back), get up (`kneel`, then stand), the 6-frame pivot, the walk back to the mark, win (fist pump plus the `cheer` pose and `expr: 'shout'`), lose (kneel, head down).\n661\t- **Referee and judges:** each signal in §5.3, plus flag raises and waves (the flag cloth on a two-segment pendulum).\n662\t- **Story and other:**\n663\t  - the hammer-fist chop (tameshiwari);\n664\t  - Senpai's demonstrations (the same technique data);\n665\t  - the belt tie (a hands-at-waist loop);\n666\t  - cutscene gestures (`hips`, `wave`, nod, point, crossed arms);\n667\t  - Mei's cheer with a heart pop;\n668\t  - crowd bobbing and arms up;\n669\t  - the bull's gallop, paw, dizzy and trample cycles.\n670\t- **Technique Lab:** check every animation with `tools/filmstrip.mjs` (`--seed 1`) at the Lab's deep link (§14). Each must wind up, strike and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps (the engine's own checklist).\n671\t- **Fallback:** if a spin or flip cannot be made to read well procedurally, cut a motion-capture clip from the engine repository's `src/mocap` (not in the handoff; `tools/anim-set.mjs`, `Mocap.drive`) for that one move. Record that in the engine-feedback answers.\n672\t\n673\t---\n674\t\n675\t## 11. Audio (all generated in code, no files)\n676\t\n677\t### 11.1 Music\n678\tSongs use the engine sequencer `{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes }] }`, with drums `k s h o c t`. Use Japanese pentatonic colour: the *in* scale D E♭ G A B♭ and the *yo* scale D E G A B. Each song runs 16–32 bars, loops, and has its own melody.\n679\t\n680\t| id | Mood | bpm | Lead / bass / percussion |\n681\t|---|---|---|---|\n682\t| `title` | upbeat arcade theme, the \"Champon!\" hook | 132 | square lead, triangle bass, taiko and hats |\n683\t| `dojo` | koto-like plucks | 96 | `pulse12` short notes, taiko |\n684\t| `harbor` | brisk, bright | 120 | square, bouncy bass |\n685\t| `bamboo` | sparse shakuhachi | 84 | triangle with vibrato, shaker |\n686\t| `gorge` | tense ostinato | 140 | pulse bass ostinato, toms |\n687\t| `ship` | 6/8 shanty | 110, `steps: 3` | square, accordion-like `pulse` |\n688\t| `market` | matsuri festival | 124 | sine-flute with vibrato, taiko, chappa (hats) |\n689\t| `fuji` | stately | 100 | arpeggiated square chords, long triangle |\n690\t| `stadium` | heroic final | 150 | double lead, driving drums |\n691\t| `bonus` | comic, fast | 160 | staccato square |\n692\t| `practice` | calm loop | 90 | soft pulse |\n693\t| `select` | versus select | 128 | |\n694\t| `session` | online card and lobby | 110 | |\n695\t| `continue` | ticking tension | 120 | |\n696\t| `gameover` | sad, plays once | 80 | |\n697\t| `ending` | warm reprise of `title` | 104 | |\n698\t| stings | `promotion` fanfare, `victory`, `defeat`, `perfect` | — | `audio.sting(…)` |\n699\t\n700\t### 11.2 Sound effects (`audio.define`)\n701\t- **Fighting:** `whooshL` and `whooshH` (filtered noise, pitched by technique weight), `thwackHalf`, `thwackFull` (a noise crack plus a low sine thump), `clack` (block: a short wood click), `thudFall`, `land`, `stepBare` (per floor: wood, sand, stone), `pivot`.\n702\t- **Referee:** `flagSnap`, `whistle` (2.5 kHz sine with vibrato), `gong`, `taikoHit`.\n703\t- **Crowd:** `crowdOoh`, `crowdCheer`, `crowdBoo` (noise beds plus voice blips, §11.3).\n704\t- **Bonus:** `boardCrack`, `potShatter`, `bowlDing`, `bowlSplash`, `bullSnort`, `bullStomp`, `bullBellow`, `bullThud`, `dizzy`.\n705\t- **Ambience:** `gull`, `waves`, `creak`, `steamHiss`, `cameraFlash`.\n706\t- **UI:** `beltTie`, `scoreTick`, `menuMove`, `menuOk`, `menuBack`, `countBeep`. Engine presets are fine for UI.\n707\t\n708\t### 11.3 Gibberish voices (`KC.Voice`, an Animalese/Simlish-style synth on Web Audio)\n709\t- **Text to syllables:** deterministic (a hash of the line, so a line always sounds the same). One syllable per 1–2 letters, CV-shaped. Vowels a/e/i/o/u come from the text's vowels, and consonant classes from its letters:\n710\t  - plosive: an 8 ms noise burst;\n711\t  - fricative: 25 ms high-pass noise;\n712\t  - nasal: a 20 ms low sine;\n713\t  - none.\n714\t- **Synthesis per syllable:**\n715\t  - source: pulse, saw or triangle at the speaker's f0, with ±3% jitter, through two band-pass formant filters;\n716\t  - formants (F1/F2, scaled by the speaker's formant ×): a 800/1200, e 400/2000, i 300/2500, o 450/800, u 325/700;\n717\t  - envelope: 5 ms attack, 60–90 ms hold, 30 ms release;\n718\t  - optional vibrato and roughness (a noise mix).\n719\t- **Prosody:** declination over the line. `!` rises then falls hard; `?` rises; `…` is slow and low. The stressed syllable is louder and +15% pitch. Speed is the speaker's syllables per second (Animalese is fast: 13–19).\n720\t- **Referee calls:** slower (8–10 syllables/s), louder, with a fixed syllable count and stress per call so each call has a recognizable rhythm (BEGIN = 3 syllables, stress on 2; STOP = 2; FULL POINT = 3, stress on 1). Add reverb in the stadium (a short feedback delay).\n721\t- **Kiai:** 1–2 loud syllables with a pitch drop and a soft waveshaper, chosen by the technique's `kiai` weight, played when the active phase begins.\n722\t- **Crowd:** many tiny random-f0 (120–300 Hz) syllable blips over a band-passed noise bed, swelling on events.\n723\t- **Routing:** through a **voice bus** with its own volume setting (EC5), under master and mute. Headless, the voice is silent, but `speak()` still returns the duration, which drives `E.Dialog` mouths and the referee timings in tests.\n724\t\n725\t---\n726\t\n727\t## 12. Multiplayer: local and AI Hub online\n728\t\n729\t### 12.1 Local\n730\t- **Versus:** P1 is the keyboard or the first pad. P2 joins with Start (Options) on another pad or **2** on the keyboard (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` in the versus setup). P2 can also be a CPU (choose L1–10).\n731\t- **Kachinuki (local 3–4 players):** a queue in join order. White (queue[0]) fights red (queue[1]) in one-round bouts. The winner stays, the loser goes to the back, and the others kneel in seiza at the mat edge. A streak counter is shown, and the first to 5 bout wins is champion. The same `KC.Kachinuki` class runs online (§12.4).\n732\t\n733\t### 12.2 AI Hub session protocol v1 (read `lib/hub/README.md` whole before M10)\n734\t- **Mode:** `shared-world`. **Seats:** `[2, 4]`. The hub's own test runs shared-world games with 4 players, latency, jitter and loss, a host-leave run, and a 2-player server run, so 4 seats are required.\n735\t- **Head:** the AI Hub client `session.js` (1.4.0) is pasted **unmodified as the first script, right after `<meta charset>`**. Then:\n736\t```html\n737\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n738\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"2-4\">\n739\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n740\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n741\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Win bouts: first to 2 points. Winner stays on.\">\n742\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move: left stick/WASD · Strike: right stick, face buttons or IJKL\">\n743\t```\n744\t- **Session flow:**\n745\t  1. `AIHub.active` is true from the first line, so boot straight into `sessionCard` (no title, menus or attract).\n746\t  2. Call `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [2, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, run the normal game.\n747\t  3. Build the stadium, then call **`AIHub.ready()` with no key press**.\n748\t- **The game's own card** (`card: true`) shows:\n749\t  - the stadium, with the players kneeling in seiza in their hub colours (`#e8443a #3b82f6 #22c55e #f5b700`);\n750\t  - names (drawn with the pixel font, uppercased, unsupported characters mapped to `?`, 10 characters max; never as HTML);\n751\t  - ready marks from the `card` event's `ready`;\n752\t  - how to win and this player's controls (device-specific icons);\n753\t  - the countdown `left`.\n754\t  - **Ready** is ✕, Enter or a tap/click on READY: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **More time** is △, T or a click: `AIHub.answer('more')`.\n755\t  - The card uses no `Math.random` (use `AIHub.rng('card')` if needed).\n756\t- **Start:** the `start` event. Show a 3-2-1 countdown with drum hits while `AIHub.time() < 0`; \"BEGIN!\" at 0.\n757\t\n758\t### 12.3 Roles\n759\t- **Host** (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the authoritative `KC.Kachinuki` and `KC.Bout` at 120 Hz. If the host is one of the two fighters, its local input drives that fighter **through a fairness delay**: its inputs are delayed by the current opponent's one-way latency, an EMA of `AIHub.time()` at receipt minus the message's `a`, clamped 0–150 ms.\n760\t- **Server** (`AIHub.server`, the host with no player): the same host code with no local input and no camera control. It still renders.\n761\t- **Guests** send their own input and render the world from snapshots. Spectators (queued players) send heartbeats so traffic flows both ways, which the harness checks.\n762\t\n763\t### 12.4 Kachinuki rules online\n764\t- The queue is the seat order. Bouts are one round to 2 points with a 30 s clock and hantei at time-out. Winner stays; the loser goes to the back of the queue.\n765\t- **The round ends** when a player reaches **5 bout wins** (`TUNE.sessionTarget`) or the hub calls time.\n766\t- **Standings:** by bout wins (descending), then points scored (descending), then points conceded (ascending); ties are grouped together. `scores = { id: boutWins }`.\n767\t- `stats = { id: { 'Bouts won', 'Full points', 'Half points', 'Best streak' } }`.\n768\t- `awards`: IPPON HUNTER (most full points), IRON WALL (most blocks), KACHINUKI (best streak ≥ 3).\n769\t\n770\t### 12.5 Messages (JSON, compact; ≤ 16 KB, ≤ 20 per second per copy)\n771\t\n772\t| t | Direction | Reliable | Content | Rate |\n773\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n774\t| `in` | guest → host | yes | `{ t, s: seq, a: AIHub.time(), p: posture 0–4 (n,u,d,l,r), h: tech held 0–4, e: [[techDir, aMs], …] }`: technique press edges since the last message | on change, ≤ 15/s; coalesce edges |\n775\t| `hb` | every non-host → host | no | `{ t, a }` | 2/s |\n776\t| `s` | host → all | **no** | `{ t, a, k: step, ph: phase, c: clock, sc: [w, r], rw: [w, r], q: [ids], f: [[id, x, z, facing, state, tech, phase, u×1000, flags], …], ref: [state, u×1000] }` | 12/s (every 83 ms of **real** time) |\n777\t| `ev` | host → all | yes | `{ t, list: [{ k: 'call'|'point'|'block'|'bout'|'boutEnd'|'stand'|'over', … }, …] }`: all of one step's events batched into one message | on event (typically ≤ 3/s) |\n778\t\n779\tUse real time (`performance.now()` or `AIHub.time()`) for network cadence, never game time. Slow motion and hit-stop must not change it. Budget: a guest sends ≤ 17/s (`in` + `hb`) and the host ≤ 15/s, under the client's 20/s limit with headroom for its 40-message burst.\n780\t\n781\t### 12.6 Netcode details\n782\t- **Host:** applies a guest's `in` on arrival. Press edges are applied in order; a late edge still fires as soon as it arrives, never dropped. The host takes the posture from the latest message.\n783\t- **Guests:** keep a snapshot buffer and render at `now − 100 ms`, interpolating positions. Each fighter is a **puppet** `Karateka`: `applySnapshot()` sets the serializable state; `u` advances by `dt` between snapshots; then `pose()` runs.\n784\t- **Events** play calls, sounds and popups locally. The guest's own technique gets **cosmetic prediction**: on a local technique press while its puppet is free, start that technique's wind-up locally at once (whoosh plus kiai). If a snapshot confirms the same technique within 250 ms, blend to the authoritative phase over 60 ms; otherwise blend back to the snapshot state.\n785\t- **Leave:**\n786\t  - a fighter in the current bout leaves → the other wins by forfeit (\"KIKEN\"), and the next challenger enters;\n787\t  - fewer than 2 players remain → finish at once;\n788\t  - the host leaves → the hub voids the round; on `end` (`host-left`), show \"HOST LEFT\" and stop.\n789\t- **Time-up:** the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` at once (well inside `graceMs`). A bout in progress counts only if a side leads on points.\n790\t- **End:** take no input, send nothing, and stop music, voices and sfx (`audio.music(null)`, mute the voice bus). Show \"ROUND OVER\" with the hub's standings.\n791\t- **Live pause (EC4):** in a session the world never pauses. Options (when Create is not held) opens the standard menu over the running game, and the local fighter's input is neutral while the menu is open. Set `autoPause: false`; scenes are not `pausable`.\n792\t- **No other network.** No WebSocket, WebRTC or fetch; no `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open` or navigation; storage is the client's memory storage. Never insert names as HTML.\n793\t\n794\t---\n795\t\n796\t## 13. Attract mode (`KC.Attract`)\n797\t- **Trigger:** **10 s with no input** on the title, the main menu or any sub-menu screen (not in gameplay). Every key, button, stick past the dead zone, mouse move or click, or tap counts as input. **Any input exits** to the title and resets the timer.\n798\t- **Loop** (a \"DEMO — PRESS START\" banner throughout; music at half volume):\n799\t  1. **Exhibition bout**, about 45 s: two random roster fighters at a random stage, both CPUs in **showcase mode** (cheating allowed: perfect reads, forced techniques). The director makes the bout show a half point, a full point, an aiuchi, a block, a sweep, a somersault cross-up and a **time-out hantei** (it sets the clock to 6 s for the last exchange).\n800\t  2. **Bonus reel**, about 35 s: an auto-played PERFECT board break, 15 s of Pots Ahoy! with perfect reads (including a bowl catch), and one Stop the Bull charge.\n801\t  3. **How to fight**, about 30 s: Senpai demonstrates techniques while the Move List's stick icons light up.\n802\t  4. **High scores** (6 s), then the **title** (5 s), then repeat.\n803\t- **Touch-only devices:** it runs as usual under the \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\" panel (§3.5).\n804\t- Deterministic per loop index (seeded), so tests can assert the cycle.\n805\t\n806\t---\n807\t\n808\t## 14. Dev mode (`KC.Dev`)\n809\t- **Access:** the pause menu's **Dev mode** item (required). After the first use, F2, Backquote or L3+R3 toggles the panel. `?dev=1` in the URL also enables it. Hide the item in AI Hub sessions, where it would break fairness.\n810\t- **Panel:** a canvas pixel UI, 10-foot readable, 8 rows per page, tabs switched with L1/R1, Q/E or a click.\n811\t  - D-pad or stick up/down selects. Left/right adjusts (hold to accelerate). △ or R resets the focused value. Mouse drag on a slider adjusts it, and a click toggles.\n812\t  - **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits at the top of the Tuning tab.\n813\t  - **Live sliders:** systems read `KC.TUNE` every step, never cached, so changes apply at once.\n814\t- **TUNING** (`KC.TUNE_DEFAULTS`):\n815\t\n816\t| key | range | default |\n817\t|---|---|---|\n818\t| `gameSpeed` (`game.timeScale`) | 0.25–2 | 1 |\n819\t| `techSpeed` (scales all technique frames) | 0.5–1.5 | 1 |\n820\t| `walk` | 40–120 | 72 |\n821\t| `jump` | 250–450 | 360 |\n822\t| `gravity` | 700–1400 | 1100 |\n823\t| `hitstop` | 0–0.3 s | 0.10 |\n824\t| `fullAt` | 300–1000 | 600 |\n825\t| `counterMul` | 1–3 | 2 |\n826\t| `reachScale` | 0.7–1.4 | 1 |\n827\t| `blockWindow` (frames) | 6–30 | 14 |\n828\t| `clock` (s) | 10–99 | 30 |\n829\t| `pointsToWin` | 1–5 (step .5) | 2 |\n830\t| `roundsToWin` | 1–3 | 2 |\n831\t| `cpuLevel` (0 = per opponent) | 0–10 | 0 |\n832\t| `cpuReact` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n833\t| `cpuAggression` (−1 = per opponent) | −1–1 | −1 |\n834\t| `refPace` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n835\t| `potSpeed` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n836\t| `bullSpeed` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n837\t| `kiSpeed` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n838\t| `sessionTarget` (bout wins) | 1–10 | 5 |\n839\t\n840\t- **TOGGLES:** hit, hurt and push boxes; frame data (startup, active, recovery, advantage of the last technique); input display; AI thoughts (state, chosen technique, read result); **P1 invincible** (the CPU cannot score); P2 control (CPU, pad 2, dummy: stand, crouch, jump, block, attack); infinite time; freeze CPU; slow motion 0.25×; **frame step** (while frozen, ▸ advances one step); FPS and stats; mute voices; skip cutscenes; unlock all.\n841\t- **WARP:** any ladder node, the Technique Lab, any stage (versus quick bout), any bonus, any lesson, the ending, credits and the attract mode; set the rank; +10,000 score; win or lose the current round or match; fire any referee call; reset the save.\n842\t- **TECHNIQUE LAB** (scene `lab`, deep link `#lab/<techId>`): the hero vs a standing dummy. Cycle techniques (L1/R1), play (✕), slow motion (□), boxes (△), step. It shows the frame data and the **measured contact gap**, with live sliders for that technique's frames and reach, and \"COPY JSON\" puts the technique entry on the clipboard. It doubles as the filmstrip target.\n843\t- **INFO:** version, build, seed, scene, bout phase, FPS, AI state, the last hit's quality breakdown (timingQ, spacingQ, counter).\n844\t- **Persistence:** stored under `E.store('kc:dev')`. While any tuning value is non-default, the HUD shows **DEV** and **no high scores are recorded**. \"Copy tuning JSON\" exports `KC.TUNE`, so designers tune live and paste the result into data.\n845\t\n846\t---\n847\t\n848\t## 15. Architecture and modules\n849\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92d1ba00-1a0f-4edd-bbd5-53c74c3ffc62","uuid":"3d21c661-e657-4290-ac76-0aa8b6db2571","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:40:23.546Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/work/PLAN.md","content":"| `fuji` | FUJI GARDEN | dusk, ambient .6 pink | raked gravel (custom texture) | Mount Fuji (custom cone with a snow cap), pagoda silhouette, torii gate (custom), cherry trees | stone lanterns, koi pond edge, falling petals (pink particles), garden visitors (small crowd) | `fuji` |\n| `stadium` | NATIONAL STADIUM (final, and the default online stage) | indoor night, ambient .45 | blue competition mat with red and white start lines | tiered crowd (dense), banners \"CHAMPON CUP '84\", big scoreboard (custom, glowing) | spotlight cones on the mat, camera flashes in the crowd, judges' chairs (core `chair`), a trophy table holding the **Golden Bowl** | `stadium` |\n| `courtyard` (bonus) | DOJO COURTYARD | morning | packed earth | dojo wall, sky, pine | cinder blocks and board stacks (custom), a water barrel | `bonus` |\n| `fishmarket` (bonus) | FISH-MARKET ALLEY | morning | wet stone | market roofs, harbor beyond | fish crates, ice boxes, hanging signs | `bonus` |\n| `pasture` (bonus) | FUJI PASTURE | noon | grass | Fuji, hills, clouds | fences (`fence`), hay bales (`kenney-graveyard:hay-bale-bundled`), a farmer (crowd of 1) | `bonus` |\n\nCrowds: back rows are cheap kit-style silhouettes (rectangle plus disc, 2–3 parallax rows, bobbing harder on `cheer`, as in the FIGHTING kit's `stage()`). The front row is 4–8 `E.Humanoid({ style: 'classic', size: 1.0 })` drawn `every: 2`, in wave and cheer poses. **Mei stands in the front row of every story stage** after the harbor (an HD rig that cheers on the player's points). Crowd reactions come from the bout bus: \"ooh\" on blocks, a cheer swell on points, a big swell on full points.\n\n### 7.3 Champon Road (the story ladder, `KC.DATA.ladder`)\n\n| # | Node | Content | On success |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | cutscene `prologue` | Harbor title cards plus Taro and Negi lines | — |\n| 2 | practice `lessonI` (dojo) | 7 drills: frontKick, roundKick, lowKick, backKick, block, jump, crouch | — |\n| 3 | match vs **KAMABOKO** (dojo) | hints on | **YELLOW BELT** |\n| 4 | match vs **SENPAI NEGI** (dojo) | | **GREEN BELT** |\n| 5 | bonus **BREAK THE BOARDS** (courtyard) | 5 pine boards | — |\n| 6 | cutscene `harborMei` and match vs **EBI** (harbor) | Mei introduced | **BLUE BELT** |\n| 7 | practice `lessonII` (harbor pier) | 6 drills: midLunge, upLunge, revPunch (get close), frontSweep, upperPunch, squatPunch | — |\n| 8 | match vs **KIKURAGE** (bamboo) | | **BROWN BELT** |\n| 9 | bonus **POTS AHOY!** (fish market) | 16 objects | — |\n| 10 | match vs **IKA** (log bridge) | | **BLACK BELT, 1ST DAN** |\n| 11 | practice `lessonIII` (log bridge) | 8 drills: backRound, turnBackKick, backSweep, jumpSide, jumpBack, frontFlip, backFlip, backRoll | — |\n| 12 | match vs **CAPTAIN BUTA** (ship) | | **3RD DAN** |\n| 13 | bonus **STOP THE BULL** (pasture) | 3 charges | — |\n| 14 | match vs **ASARI** (market) and a Mei cutscene | | **5TH DAN** |\n| 15 | match vs **MASTER KYABETSU** (fuji) | | **8TH DAN** |\n| 16 | cutscene `finalEntrance` and match vs **MEI** (stadium) | | **CHAMP** |\n| 17 | cutscene `ending`, credits parade, initials | | back to the title |\n\n- Before each match: the **Champon Road** screen (§8.3), then the **VS card**. After each win: the **promotion ceremony**, then the road again.\n- On a match loss: **CONTINUE?** (10 s countdown). Continuing retries the match with the score reset to 0 and the rank kept (arcade rules). Declining or timing out goes to **GAME OVER**, then initials if the score qualifies, then the title. After 2 lost rounds against the same opponent, a **tip card** names its habit and the counter technique (from the roster `tip` field), and after a lost match it offers \"LOWER DIFFICULTY FOR THIS MATCH?\" (an assist).\n- **Scoring:** technique points, the time bonus, bonus-stage points, drill points (200/400), and a rank-up bonus of +1,000 × the new rank index. A match with no points conceded is PERFECT: +5,000.\n- Autosave (`game.saves.autosave()`) after each completed node: `{ node, rank, score, difficulty, unlocks, stats }`.\n\n### 7.4 Bonus stages\nAll three run in the `bonus` scene with the hero's `Karateka` and the same twin-stick input. Their numbers live in `KC.DATA.bonus` and the dev sliders.\n- **BREAK THE BOARDS (Tameshiwari)**\n  - A stack of N boards on two cinder blocks (story: 5 pine boards; Bonus Games: choose 3, 5, 7 or 10, of pine, roof tile ×1.5 or ice ×2).\n  - **Hold R↓ (or ✕, or K)** to breathe in. The *KI* gauge beside the fighter rises at 55%/s with a ±6% sine wobble at 2.2 Hz. Holding above 100% for 0.3 s overcharges it (\"TOO MUCH!\") and drops it to 60%.\n  - **Release to chop** (a downward hammer-fist). Power is the gauge value. Boards broken = `min(N, floor(power/100 × (N + 0.99)))`. Power ≥ 95 is **PERFECT**: all boards plus 2,000.\n  - Each board is worth 200 × the material multiplier.\n  - Presentation: 0.3× slow motion for 0.4 s, the boards split top to bottom with halves flying (`particles.bits` in wood tones), dust and a shake. Judge Genkotsu calls \"PERFECT!\" or \"WEAK!\".\n- **POTS AHOY! (Evade)**\n  - 16 objects thrown from the left and right edges. The interval falls from 1.4 s to 0.8 s and the speed rises from 150 to 230 u/s.\n  - Lanes: HIGH at z ≈ 52, MID ≈ 36, LOW ≈ 10 (tune to the rig: head, chest, shins).\n  - Telegraph 0.7 s ahead: an edge arrow plus a height marker. The fighter turns to face the incoming side.\n  - Objects:\n    - **clay pot**: break it with any technique of its height (R↑ high, R→ mid, R↓ low);\n    - **bonsai pot**: kicks only;\n    - **boulder**: cannot be broken; dodge it (jump the LOW one, crouch the HIGH one, back somersault the MID one);\n    - **steaming champon bowl**: **catch it with Block** for +1,000 (\"NICE CATCH!\"); breaking it costs −500 (\"MOTTAINAI!\"); letting it pass splashes for 0.\n  - **The stage ends on the first hit** (as in the source). 200 per break; handling all 16 is PERFECT +3,000. The 16-object sequence is fixed per visit, seeded by visit number.\n- **STOP THE BULL**\n  - A custom quadruped bull (§9.4). Three charges at 150, 190 and 240 u/s.\n  - Telegraph: paws the ground twice and snorts (1.2 s), lowers its head, then charges from the right.\n  - **Strike window:** the bull's head is 8–40 u from your front edge during the active frames of a **valid technique** (`upperPunch, upLunge, squatPunch, frontKick, backKick`). Success is \"THUD\": the bull stops dizzy (stars, 1.5 s) for **3,000**, plus 1,000 for PERFECT within ±6 u of the window's centre. It then walks off and charges again.\n  - Any other technique, or bad timing, gets you **trampled** (a comic tumble arc) and ends the stage.\n\n### 7.5 Practice (Senpai's drill, `KC.Drill`)\n- Senpai stands on the right facing the player. The drill card at the top reads \"LESSON I-3: LOW KICK\" with the prompt icons for the current device.\n- Loop: \"WATCH.\" Senpai performs the technique into empty air. \"NOW YOU.\" The player has 4 s:\n  - the **matching technique** scores \"GOOD!\" (+200), or \"SHARP!\" (+400) if it starts within 50 frames;\n  - a **wrong technique** gets \"NO. WATCH AGAIN.\" and the demo repeats with the prompt blinking;\n  - after **3 misses**, a hint appears (\"HOLD L ↓, THEN PUSH R ↑\" with animated stick icons), and the drill waits for success.\n- `start` skips a drill (no points). At the end: \"LESSON COMPLETE 6/7\", Senpai nods, and the points are added.\n- Lesson sets: I (7), II (6), III (8); the All Techniques set has 21 drills (§7.3).\n- **Free Spar** (DOJO menu): the hero vs a dummy fighter (modes stand, crouch, jump, block, attack L3). Hit and hurt boxes, frame data and the input display are on, and the bout's referee is off (hits just flash and show points).\n\n### 7.6 Items and objects\nBelts (the rank shown on the rig), the **Golden Bowl** trophy (a stadium prop and the ending), bonus objects (boards, roof tiles, ice blocks, clay pots, bonsai pots, boulders, champon bowls), the bull, and unlockables (2 versus characters plus \"Classic palette\": a white/red NES-like recolour of the fighters, unlocked by finishing the story).\n\n### 7.7 Story beats\nCutscenes are scripted with `E.Dialog` and portraits (`rig.drawPortrait`), with gibberish voices. Every line is in Appendix A. A **VS card** precedes each match: split panels, white on the left and red on the right, names, the belt or rank, the stage name, and the opponent's taunt typed with its voice, for 2.5 s. **Promotions:** Judge Genkotsu presents the belt; the belt icon flies to the waist with glints; \"PROMOTED! BLUE BELT\"; a fanfare; the rank road (one row of 10 icons, from white belt to Champ) highlights the new one. **Ending:** Mei hands over the Golden Bowl. Then the cast parade, each fighter bowing with their name; \"YOU ARE THE KARATE CHAMPON\"; credits; initials.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Modes and screens (flow)\n\n```\nboot → title ─(10 s idle)→ attract ─(any input)→ title\ntitle ─start→ mainMenu → { road → [vsCard → fight → promotion | continue → gameOver → initials] / bonus / practice / cutscene … → ending → credits → initials → title,\n                           versusSetup → select → stageSelect → fight → results,\n                           dojoMenu → practice / freeSpar / moveList,\n                           bonusMenu → bonus → initials,\n                           records, options (engine settings), about }\nAI Hub session (AIHub.active): sessionCard → sessionFight (kachinuki) → sessionOver   (no title, menus or attract)\n```\n\n### 8.1 Title\n- The dojo at night under lanterns. Taro (white) and Mei (red) face each other in kamae and bow every few seconds.\n- Logo: `E.font.title('KARATE', …)` over `'CHAMPON'` at scale 4, colours `#fffbe8 #ffe08a #ffb347 #e0662a`, depth 3. The **O** of CHAMPON is drawn as a noodle bowl with chopsticks and rising steam.\n- \"PRESS START\" or \"PRESS ✕\" (glyph icon by device) blinks. The version line comes from `E.versionLabel()` plus the game version.\n- Main menu items: CONTINUE (only with an autosave, showing \"5TH DAN · NIGHT MARKET\"), CHAMPON ROAD, VERSUS, DOJO, BONUS GAMES, RECORDS, OPTIONS, ABOUT. A live vignette on the right side of the screen changes with the focused item.\n\n### 8.2 Character select (versus)\n- A 5×2 portrait grid (`rig.drawPortrait`). Locked fighters are dark silhouettes marked \"?\".\n- The P1 cursor is white and the P2 cursor red; a CPU cursor shows \"CPU\".\n- A big portrait on each side shows the name, the style line (from the personality) and 3 stat bars. Hover or click picks with the mouse.\n- Engine `E.FightSelect` is a reference, but write `KC.Select` for the grid, locks, CPU level choice and the mouse.\n\n### 8.3 Champon Road screen\n- A horizontal painted scroll: the road from the dojo to the stadium with 8 location stops (the dojo holds 2 matches) and the 3 bonus icons between them. Visited stops are stamped with a belt icon, and the next stop pulses.\n- It shows \"NEXT: BAMBOO FOREST — KIKURAGE\", the current belt, the score, and `✕ GO`. The camera pans along the road. Bonus stops appear as small icons (boards, pot, bull).\n\n### 8.4 Results (versus)\nThe winner's pose, then \"WHITE WINS 2–1\" and 4 big stat lines: full points, half points, blocks, best technique. Menu: REMATCH / CHARACTER SELECT / STAGE SELECT / MAIN MENU.\n\n### 8.5 Stage select\nThe real stage renders behind the selector. Left and right change it, and it shows the name and a \"← n/8 →\" label. RANDOM is an option.\n\n### 8.6 Move List (pause item and DOJO)\n- One posture row at a time: the left side shows the **L-stick icon** for the selected posture (cycled with up/down or by holding the left stick). Around a big **R-stick icon**, the 4 techniques appear in their directions.\n- Pushing R previews the technique: a live rig in a box plays it, with its name, points range and a \"FULL\" star. This replaces a dense 20-cell table.\n\n### 8.7 Continue, game over and initials\n- \"CONTINUE?\" with a big 9…0 countdown and the `confirm` glyph; Mei says \"GET UP, MOYASHI!\".\n- Game over: Taro kneels in the rain; Genkotsu says \"TRAIN. RETURN.\"\n- Initials use `E.Arcade` (the arcade module) letter wheel: `cab.enter(score, 'story', done)`, with tables per mode: `'story'`, `'boards'`, `'pots'`, `'bull'`.\n\n### 8.8 Records\nOne table per page (L1/R1, Q/E or click the tabs). 10 rows of `NAME SCORE` plus the rank reached (story) or the bonus best.\n\n### 8.9 Pause menu\nThe engine standard menu, plus `menu.items` given **as a function** (the engine evaluates it; falsy entries are skipped — verified in `E.Shell`):\n```js\nitems: () => [['Move list', openMoveList], canRetry() && ['Retry match', retryMatch], ['Dev mode', openDev]]\n```\nQuit to title goes to `'title'`. In an AI Hub session there is no Quit (`quit: false`) and no Retry.\n\n### 8.10 Options (game settings beside the engine's standard ones)\n| key | label | values (default first) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `difficulty` | Difficulty | Normal, Easy, Hard |\n| `calls` | Referee calls | English, Japanese |\n| `hints` | Tutorial hints | on, off |\n| `camera` | Fight camera | Arcade side, Tatami 2.5D |\n| `techNames` | Technique names | on, off |\n| `inputDisplay` | Input display | off, on |\n| `palette` | Fighter palette | Modern, Classic (once unlocked) |\n| (engine) | Button symbols | **PlayStation** (via EC2), Xbox, Auto, Nintendo, Positions |\n| (EC5) | Voices volume | 0–100%, default 80 |\n\n### 8.11 About / Credits\nPaged, 10-foot readable, reached from the main menu and the credits:\n1. **SOURCE INFORMATION** (the 15 fields TASK.md lists);\n2. **TESTING FEEDBACK** (4 answers);\n3. **ENGINE FEEDBACK** (6);\n4. **PROMPT FEEDBACK** (4);\n5. **CREDITS**.\n\nThe text comes from one data file shared with `exit-interview.json` (§19).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Art direction and assets\n\n### 9.1 Look\n- Use the engine's native HD-rig pixel style. Apply every step of the API card's **Look target**:\n  - `res 'ps1'` with `expand`;\n  - dressed characters with full palettes;\n  - a backdrop behind every scene;\n  - textured floors;\n  - 3–8 props per screen with `propSize: 1.4`;\n  - effects on every hit;\n  - `E.tones` shading for custom art;\n  - title-font logos;\n  - lights in every scene;\n  - portraits in dialogue.\n- Compare screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug quality, not NES.\n- Karate Champ flavour comes from big side-on fighters, white vs red, the referee centred behind them, scenic Japanese settings, and a minimal HUD of point icons.\n\n### 9.2 Effects\n- **Scoring hit:** an impact star (`particles.impact`) at the contact point and sparks (yellow `#ffe070` for full, white for half), a shake (2 for half, 4 for full), `game.flash('#ffffff', .08)` on full only (the engine respects comfort settings), a gamepad rumble, and a gi-snap dust puff.\n- Block: blue sparks. Whiff: a faint `rig.drawSmear` trail on kicks.\n- Knockdown: `particles.dust` on landing.\n- Sweeps: a dust arc. Flips: an afterimage `ghost` trail.\n- Crowd camera flashes in the stadium.\n- Full-point slow motion as in §4.7.\n\n### 9.3 New art to make (by code)\n**(a) Shapes**: `E.SHAPES` entries, checked with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs kc-shapes.js#id`:\n- `toro` (stone lantern: lathe, box and roof prism; a flame light);\n- `torii` (2 cylinders, 2 beams, vermilion `#d8402a`);\n- `noodleCart` (core `cart`, a `kenney-fantasy-town:stall-red` canopy, a lantern, a pot with steam);\n- `shipWheel`;\n- `mast`;\n- `cinderBlock`;\n- `boardStack` (params `n`, `mat`);\n- `taiko` (lathe);\n- `weaponRack`;\n- `scoreboard` (glowing panel);\n- `bonsaiPot`;\n- `champonBowl` (`lathe` with a broth disc; `kenney-food:bowl-soup` is the reference);\n- `goldenBowl` (gold material, on a plinth);\n- `norenShopfront`.\n\n**(b) Custom backdrop layers** (EC6): bamboo stalks, pagoda, Mount Fuji, waterfall, boat silhouettes, lighthouse, ship rigging silhouette, stadium tiers.\n\n**(c) Sprites** (`E.sprite` strings): kanji 空 手 道 and 麺 (12–16 px) for scrolls and the shop sign; gulls (2 frames); koi; cherry petals; pots and boulders (with `rot` while flying); point icons (empty, half, full); belt icons; flags; the stars for a dizzy bull; the glyph badges (§3.3).\n\n**(d) Asset store:** cut a set with `node tools/asset-set.mjs` holding only:\n- `kenney-pirate:boat-row-large`\n- `kenney-pirate:cannon`\n- `kaykit-dungeon-remastered:barrel-small-stack`\n- `kenney-fantasy-town:stall-red`\n- `kenney-food:bowl-soup`\n- `kenney-food:steamer`\n- `kenney-graveyard:hay-bale-bundled`\n- `kenney-platformer:fence-rope`\n- `kenney-nature:pot-small`\n- `kenney-nature:crops-bamboo-stage-a`\n\nName the set `kc` and save it to `assets/kc-set.js`, then load it with `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS.kc)`. Credit Kenney and KayKit (CC0) in the About screen.\n\n### 9.4 The bull (custom creature, `KC.Bull`)\n- Pose the parts in 3D and project them with `r.w(x, y, z)`, as Emberdeep's creatures do:\n  - a torso of stacked shaded ellipses (`E.tones('#4a2e22')`);\n  - neck and head (a box-like skull, a snout, a ring in the nose);\n  - curved horns (`#e8dcc0`);\n  - 4 legs as two-segment IK with hooves, on a gallop cycle (phase offsets 0, .5, .25, .75) and a walk cycle;\n  - a tail pendulum;\n  - eyes that turn red while charging;\n  - nostril steam (smoke particles).\n- Size: about 70 u long and 45 u at the shoulder.\n- States: `idle`, `paw`, `charge`, `stopped` (dizzy stars), `trample`, `walkBack`.\n- Draw with `r.queue` at its depth.\n\n### 9.5 Palette and lighting\nEach stage defines `lights.ambient` and 2–10 lights per frame (lanterns, lamps, the spotlight cones in the stadium drawn as additive dithered polygons). Fight HUD colours are white `#fff6e8`, red `#ff5a4a` and gold `#ffd060`. Information is never carried by colour alone: the sides are always labelled WHITE and RED, and the point icons differ in shape (half vs full fill).\n\n---\n\n## 10. Animation\n\n### 10.1 Base system\nThe engine's procedural `E.Humanoid` rig is the base: `rig.update(dt, state)` every step with continuous values, `E.Attack` timing, `E.MOVES` specs, poses and expressions. No sprite sheets. **Rule:** `Karateka.pose()` builds the rig input **only from serializable state** (state id, technique id, phase, `u`, facing, air, crouch, down, flags). Online guests, replays and the attract mode then pose identically (§12.6).\n\n### 10.2 New karate animations (EC7, or game-side rig post-processing)\n- **Stances:**\n  - `kamae` (a fighting stance: front foot forward, fists up; it replaces `stance: 'guard'`);\n  - `yoi` (ready: fists down in front of the belt);\n  - `bow` (rei: the torso bends about 30° for 0.5 s);\n  - `seiza` (kneeling sitting on the heels: Kachinuki queues, the dojo crowd);\n  - `kime` (a held technique end pose).\n- **Techniques** (move specs added to `E.MOVES` as `kc_*`, or a game registry):\n  - Front Kick: base `kick`, `z0 .6 z1 .9`, chambered.\n  - Round Kick: base `roundhouse`.\n  - Low Kick: `kick` with `z0 .1 z1 .25`.\n  - Reverse Punch: base `cross`.\n  - Lunge punches: `jab`/`cross` with `lunge` 5 plus body travel, `z` high or mid.\n  - Upper Punch: base `uppercut`.\n  - Squat Punch: `cross` plus `crouch .8`.\n  - Sweeps: base `sweep` (the back sweep spins).\n  - Jumping Side Kick: `flyingkick`, with the body sideways (yaw the rig facing by 0.4 rad) and the leg out.\n  - Jumping Back Kick: in the air, the facing turns 180° and the heel drives back.\n  - **Spins:** animate the rig's **facing angle continuously** (the rig is 3D; facing is an angle). Back Kick turns 0 → π during startup and strikes with the heel toward the foe (a back thrust spec, `a0 ≈ π` or a post-processed leg). Turn Back Kick and Back Round Kick turn a full 2π, hitting in the last quarter.\n  - **Blocks:** high (forearm rises above the head), middle (forearm sweeps across the chest), low for drills only (sweeps down).\n  - **Somersaults:** in the air, pass `flip: u` with a tucked pose. If `flip` does not read as a full airborne rotation, rotate every joint around the hip centre after `rig.solvePose()` (Emberdeep's dodge-roll technique).\n  - **Back Roll:** `flip` on the ground.\n- **Reactions:** stagger (half point), knocked flat with a slide (full point), swept (the legs fly up, then a fall on the back), get up (`kneel`, then stand), the 6-frame pivot, the walk back to the mark, win (fist pump plus the `cheer` pose and `expr: 'shout'`), lose (kneel, head down).\n- **Referee and judges:** each signal in §5.3, plus flag raises and waves (the flag cloth on a two-segment pendulum).\n- **Story and other:**\n  - the hammer-fist chop (tameshiwari);\n  - Senpai's demonstrations (the same technique data);\n  - the belt tie (a hands-at-waist loop);\n  - cutscene gestures (`hips`, `wave`, nod, point, crossed arms);\n  - Mei's cheer with a heart pop;\n  - crowd bobbing and arms up;\n  - the bull's gallop, paw, dizzy and trample cycles.\n- **Technique Lab:** check every animation with `tools/filmstrip.mjs` (`--seed 1`) at the Lab's deep link (§14). Each must wind up, strike and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps (the engine's own checklist).\n- **Fallback:** if a spin or flip cannot be made to read well procedurally, cut a motion-capture clip from the engine repository's `src/mocap` (not in the handoff; `tools/anim-set.mjs`, `Mocap.drive`) for that one move. Record that in the engine-feedback answers.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Audio (all generated in code, no files)\n\n### 11.1 Music\nSongs use the engine sequencer `{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes }] }`, with drums `k s h o c t`. Use Japanese pentatonic colour: the *in* scale D E♭ G A B♭ and the *yo* scale D E G A B. Each song runs 16–32 bars, loops, and has its own melody.\n\n| id | Mood | bpm | Lead / bass / percussion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `title` | upbeat arcade theme, the \"Champon!\" hook | 132 | square lead, triangle bass, taiko and hats |\n| `dojo` | koto-like plucks | 96 | `pulse12` short notes, taiko |\n| `harbor` | brisk, bright | 120 | square, bouncy bass |\n| `bamboo` | sparse shakuhachi | 84 | triangle with vibrato, shaker |\n| `gorge` | tense ostinato | 140 | pulse bass ostinato, toms |\n| `ship` | 6/8 shanty | 110, `steps: 3` | square, accordion-like `pulse` |\n| `market` | matsuri festival | 124 | sine-flute with vibrato, taiko, chappa (hats) |\n| `fuji` | stately | 100 | arpeggiated square chords, long triangle |\n| `stadium` | heroic final | 150 | double lead, driving drums |\n| `bonus` | comic, fast | 160 | staccato square |\n| `practice` | calm loop | 90 | soft pulse |\n| `select` | versus select | 128 | |\n| `session` | online card and lobby | 110 | |\n| `continue` | ticking tension | 120 | |\n| `gameover` | sad, plays once | 80 | |\n| `ending` | warm reprise of `title` | 104 | |\n| stings | `promotion` fanfare, `victory`, `defeat`, `perfect` | — | `audio.sting(…)` |\n\n### 11.2 Sound effects (`audio.define`)\n- **Fighting:** `whooshL` and `whooshH` (filtered noise, pitched by technique weight), `thwackHalf`, `thwackFull` (a noise crack plus a low sine thump), `clack` (block: a short wood click), `thudFall`, `land`, `stepBare` (per floor: wood, sand, stone), `pivot`.\n- **Referee:** `flagSnap`, `whistle` (2.5 kHz sine with vibrato), `gong`, `taikoHit`.\n- **Crowd:** `crowdOoh`, `crowdCheer`, `crowdBoo` (noise beds plus voice blips, §11.3).\n- **Bonus:** `boardCrack`, `potShatter`, `bowlDing`, `bowlSplash`, `bullSnort`, `bullStomp`, `bullBellow`, `bullThud`, `dizzy`.\n- **Ambience:** `gull`, `waves`, `creak`, `steamHiss`, `cameraFlash`.\n- **UI:** `beltTie`, `scoreTick`, `menuMove`, `menuOk`, `menuBack`, `countBeep`. Engine presets are fine for UI.\n\n### 11.3 Gibberish voices (`KC.Voice`, an Animalese/Simlish-style synth on Web Audio)\n- **Text to syllables:** deterministic (a hash of the line, so a line always sounds the same). One syllable per 1–2 letters, CV-shaped. Vowels a/e/i/o/u come from the text's vowels, and consonant classes from its letters:\n  - plosive: an 8 ms noise burst;\n  - fricative: 25 ms high-pass noise;\n  - nasal: a 20 ms low sine;\n  - none.\n- **Synthesis per syllable:**\n  - source: pulse, saw or triangle at the speaker's f0, with ±3% jitter, through two band-pass formant filters;\n  - formants (F1/F2, scaled by the speaker's formant ×): a 800/1200, e 400/2000, i 300/2500, o 450/800, u 325/700;\n  - envelope: 5 ms attack, 60–90 ms hold, 30 ms release;\n  - optional vibrato and roughness (a noise mix).\n- **Prosody:** declination over the line. `!` rises then falls hard; `?` rises; `…` is slow and low. The stressed syllable is louder and +15% pitch. Speed is the speaker's syllables per second (Animalese is fast: 13–19).\n- **Referee calls:** slower (8–10 syllables/s), louder, with a fixed syllable count and stress per call so each call has a recognizable rhythm (BEGIN = 3 syllables, stress on 2; STOP = 2; FULL POINT = 3, stress on 1). Add reverb in the stadium (a short feedback delay).\n- **Kiai:** 1–2 loud syllables with a pitch drop and a soft waveshaper, chosen by the technique's `kiai` weight, played when the active phase begins.\n- **Crowd:** many tiny random-f0 (120–300 Hz) syllable blips over a band-passed noise bed, swelling on events.\n- **Routing:** through a **voice bus** with its own volume setting (EC5), under master and mute. Headless, the voice is silent, but `speak()` still returns the duration, which drives `E.Dialog` mouths and the referee timings in tests.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Multiplayer: local and AI Hub online\n\n### 12.1 Local\n- **Versus:** P1 is the keyboard or the first pad. P2 joins with Start (Options) on another pad or **2** on the keyboard (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` in the versus setup). P2 can also be a CPU (choose L1–10).\n- **Kachinuki (local 3–4 players):** a queue in join order. White (queue[0]) fights red (queue[1]) in one-round bouts. The winner stays, the loser goes to the back, and the others kneel in seiza at the mat edge. A streak counter is shown, and the first to 5 bout wins is champion. The same `KC.Kachinuki` class runs online (§12.4).\n\n### 12.2 AI Hub session protocol v1 (read `lib/hub/README.md` whole before M10)\n- **Mode:** `shared-world`. **Seats:** `[2, 4]`. The hub's own test runs shared-world games with 4 players, latency, jitter and loss, a host-leave run, and a 2-player server run, so 4 seats are required.\n- **Head:** the AI Hub client `session.js` (1.4.0) is pasted **unmodified as the first script, right after `<meta charset>`**. Then:\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"2-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Win bouts: first to 2 points. Winner stays on.\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move: left stick/WASD · Strike: right stick, face buttons or IJKL\">\n```\n- **Session flow:**\n  1. `AIHub.active` is true from the first line, so boot straight into `sessionCard` (no title, menus or attract).\n  2. Call `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [2, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, run the normal game.\n  3. Build the stadium, then call **`AIHub.ready()` with no key press**.\n- **The game's own card** (`card: true`) shows:\n  - the stadium, with the players kneeling in seiza in their hub colours (`#e8443a #3b82f6 #22c55e #f5b700`);\n  - names (drawn with the pixel font, uppercased, unsupported characters mapped to `?`, 10 characters max; never as HTML);\n  - ready marks from the `card` event's `ready`;\n  - how to win and this player's controls (device-specific icons);\n  - the countdown `left`.\n  - **Ready** is ✕, Enter or a tap/click on READY: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **More time** is △, T or a click: `AIHub.answer('more')`.\n  - The card uses no `Math.random` (use `AIHub.rng('card')` if needed).\n- **Start:** the `start` event. Show a 3-2-1 countdown with drum hits while `AIHub.time() < 0`; \"BEGIN!\" at 0.\n\n### 12.3 Roles\n- **Host** (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the authoritative `KC.Kachinuki` and `KC.Bout` at 120 Hz. If the host is one of the two fighters, its local input drives that fighter **through a fairness delay**: its inputs are delayed by the current opponent's one-way latency, an EMA of `AIHub.time()` at receipt minus the message's `a`, clamped 0–150 ms.\n- **Server** (`AIHub.server`, the host with no player): the same host code with no local input and no camera control. It still renders.\n- **Guests** send their own input and render the world from snapshots. Spectators (queued players) send heartbeats so traffic flows both ways, which the harness checks.\n\n### 12.4 Kachinuki rules online\n- The queue is the seat order. Bouts are one round to 2 points with a 30 s clock and hantei at time-out. Winner stays; the loser goes to the back of the queue.\n- **The round ends** when a player reaches **5 bout wins** (`TUNE.sessionTarget`) or the hub calls time.\n- **Standings:** by bout wins (descending), then points scored (descending), then points conceded (ascending); ties are grouped together. `scores = { id: boutWins }`.\n- `stats = { id: { 'Bouts won', 'Full points', 'Half points', 'Best streak' } }`.\n- `awards`: IPPON HUNTER (most full points), IRON WALL (most blocks), KACHINUKI (best streak ≥ 3).\n\n### 12.5 Messages (JSON, compact; ≤ 16 KB, ≤ 20 per second per copy)\n\n| t | Direction | Reliable | Content | Rate |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `in` | guest → host | yes | `{ t, s: seq, a: AIHub.time(), p: posture 0–4 (n,u,d,l,r), h: tech held 0–4, e: [[techDir, aMs], …] }`: technique press edges since the last message | on change, ≤ 15/s; coalesce edges |\n| `hb` | every non-host → host | no | `{ t, a }` | 2/s |\n| `s` | host → all | **no** | `{ t, a, k: step, ph: phase, c: clock, sc: [w, r], rw: [w, r], q: [ids], f: [[id, x, z, facing, state, tech, phase, u×1000, flags], …], ref: [state, u×1000] }` | 12/s (every 83 ms of **real** time) |\n| `ev` | host → all | yes | `{ t, list: [{ k: 'call'|'point'|'block'|'bout'|'boutEnd'|'stand'|'over', … }, …] }`: all of one step's events batched into one message | on event (typically ≤ 3/s) |\n\nUse real time (`performance.now()` or `AIHub.time()`) for network cadence, never game time. Slow motion and hit-stop must not change it. Budget: a guest sends ≤ 17/s (`in` + `hb`) and the host ≤ 15/s, under the client's 20/s limit with headroom for its 40-message burst.\n\n### 12.6 Netcode details\n- **Host:** applies a guest's `in` on arrival. Press edges are applied in order; a late edge still fires as soon as it arrives, never dropped. The host takes the posture from the latest message.\n- **Guests:** keep a snapshot buffer and render at `now − 100 ms`, interpolating positions. Each fighter is a **puppet** `Karateka`: `applySnapshot()` sets the serializable state; `u` advances by `dt` between snapshots; then `pose()` runs.\n- **Events** play calls, sounds and popups locally. The guest's own technique gets **cosmetic prediction**: on a local technique press while its puppet is free, start that technique's wind-up locally at once (whoosh plus kiai). If a snapshot confirms the same technique within 250 ms, blend to the authoritative phase over 60 ms; otherwise blend back to the snapshot state.\n- **Leave:**\n  - a fighter in the current bout leaves → the other wins by forfeit (\"KIKEN\"), and the next challenger enters;\n  - fewer than 2 players remain → finish at once;\n  - the host leaves → the hub voids the round; on `end` (`host-left`), show \"HOST LEFT\" and stop.\n- **Time-up:** the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` at once (well inside `graceMs`). A bout in progress counts only if a side leads on points.\n- **End:** take no input, send nothing, and stop music, voices and sfx (`audio.music(null)`, mute the voice bus). Show \"ROUND OVER\" with the hub's standings.\n- **Live pause (EC4):** in a session the world never pauses. Options (when Create is not held) opens the standard menu over the running game, and the local fighter's input is neutral while the menu is open. Set `autoPause: false`; scenes are not `pausable`.\n- **No other network.** No WebSocket, WebRTC or fetch; no `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open` or navigation; storage is the client's memory storage. Never insert names as HTML.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Attract mode (`KC.Attract`)\n- **Trigger:** **10 s with no input** on the title, the main menu or any sub-menu screen (not in gameplay). Every key, button, stick past the dead zone, mouse move or click, or tap counts as input. **Any input exits** to the title and resets the timer.\n- **Loop** (a \"DEMO — PRESS START\" banner throughout; music at half volume):\n  1. **Exhibition bout**, about 45 s: two random roster fighters at a random stage, both CPUs in **showcase mode** (cheating allowed: perfect reads, forced techniques). The director makes the bout show a half point, a full point, an aiuchi, a block, a sweep, a somersault cross-up and a **time-out hantei** (it sets the clock to 6 s for the last exchange).\n  2. **Bonus reel**, about 35 s: an auto-played PERFECT board break, 15 s of Pots Ahoy! with perfect reads (including a bowl catch), and one Stop the Bull charge.\n  3. **How to fight**, about 30 s: Senpai demonstrates techniques while the Move List's stick icons light up.\n  4. **High scores** (6 s), then the **title** (5 s), then repeat.\n- **Touch-only devices:** it runs as usual under the \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\" panel (§3.5).\n- Deterministic per loop index (seeded), so tests can assert the cycle.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Dev mode (`KC.Dev`)\n- **Access:** the pause menu's **Dev mode** item (required). After the first use, F2, Backquote or L3+R3 toggles the panel. `?dev=1` in the URL also enables it. Hide the item in AI Hub sessions, where it would break fairness.\n- **Panel:** a canvas pixel UI, 10-foot readable, 8 rows per page, tabs switched with L1/R1, Q/E or a click.\n  - D-pad or stick up/down selects. Left/right adjusts (hold to accelerate). △ or R resets the focused value. Mouse drag on a slider adjusts it, and a click toggles.\n  - **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits at the top of the Tuning tab.\n  - **Live sliders:** systems read `KC.TUNE` every step, never cached, so changes apply at once.\n- **TUNING** (`KC.TUNE_DEFAULTS`):\n\n| key | range | default |\n|---|---|---|\n| `gameSpeed` (`game.timeScale`) | 0.25–2 | 1 |\n| `techSpeed` (scales all technique frames) | 0.5–1.5 | 1 |\n| `walk` | 40–120 | 72 |\n| `jump` | 250–450 | 360 |\n| `gravity` | 700–1400 | 1100 |\n| `hitstop` | 0–0.3 s | 0.10 |\n| `fullAt` | 300–1000 | 600 |\n| `counterMul` | 1–3 | 2 |\n| `reachScale` | 0.7–1.4 | 1 |\n| `blockWindow` (frames) | 6–30 | 14 |\n| `clock` (s) | 10–99 | 30 |\n| `pointsToWin` | 1–5 (step .5) | 2 |\n| `roundsToWin` | 1–3 | 2 |\n| `cpuLevel` (0 = per opponent) | 0–10 | 0 |\n| `cpuReact` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n| `cpuAggression` (−1 = per opponent) | −1–1 | −1 |\n| `refPace` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n| `potSpeed` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n| `bullSpeed` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n| `kiSpeed` (×) | 0.5–2 | 1 |\n| `sessionTarget` (bout wins) | 1–10 | 5 |\n\n- **TOGGLES:** hit, hurt and push boxes; frame data (startup, active, recovery, advantage of the last technique); input display; AI thoughts (state, chosen technique, read result); **P1 invincible** (the CPU cannot score); P2 control (CPU, pad 2, dummy: stand, crouch, jump, block, attack); infinite time; freeze CPU; slow motion 0.25×; **frame step** (while frozen, ▸ advances one step); FPS and stats; mute voices; skip cutscenes; unlock all.\n- **WARP:** any ladder node, the Technique Lab, any stage (versus quick bout), any bonus, any lesson, the ending, credits and the attract mode; set the rank; +10,000 score; win or lose the current round or match; fire any referee call; reset the save.\n- **TECHNIQUE LAB** (scene `lab`, deep link `#lab/<techId>`): the hero vs a standing dummy. Cycle techniques (L1/R1), play (✕), slow motion (□), boxes (△), step. It shows the frame data and the **measured contact gap**, with live sliders for that technique's frames and reach, and \"COPY JSON\" puts the technique entry on the clipboard. It doubles as the filmstrip target.\n- **INFO:** version, build, seed, scene, bout phase, FPS, AI state, the last hit's quality breakdown (timingQ, spacingQ, counter).\n- **Persistence:** stored under `E.store('kc:dev')`. While any tuning value is non-default, the HUD shows **DEV** and **no high scores are recorded**. \"Copy tuning JSON\" exports `KC.TUNE`, so designers tune live and paste the result into data.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Architecture and modules\n","numLines":450,"startLine":400,"totalLines":1258}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014P79Crabix8GzamdjQj1NZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh33HkrVPFdyGvKNfJ9b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XxDNe9WpYm5M1hGT4HaJb4","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/work/PLAN.md","offset":849,"limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17229,"cache_read_input_tokens":41990,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17229},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92d1ba00-1a0f-4edd-bbd5-53c74c3ffc62","uuid":"0eb3bfd9-ba33-40c7-a9c9-12eaaa056e69","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:40:25.430Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh33HWxZyLWcDg5KN2Qm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XxDNe9WpYm5M1hGT4HaJb4":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/work/PLAN.md","offset":849,"limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XxDNe9WpYm5M1hGT4HaJb4","type":"tool_result","content":"849\t\n850\t### 15.1 Folder layout (all dev files inside the dev folder; only the final HTML and `exit-interview.json` sit beside PLAN.md)\n851\t```\n852\tKarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743/\n853\t  engine/            sparse copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (my-3d2dge.js, modules/, tools/, kits/, docs/, package.json) + CHANGES.md (every engine edit and why)\n854\t  vendor/aihub/      session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only)\n855\t  assets/            kc-set.js (cut store set) + kc-shapes.js (our E.SHAPES) + kc-sprites.js\n856\t  src/               game source fragments, concatenated in name order into one IIFE (§15.2)\n857\t  data/about.json    the About-screen text = the exit interview (§19)\n858\t  dev.html           dev page: session.js first in <head>, engine, assets, build/game.js\n859\t  build/game.js      generated\n860\t  tools/build.mjs    src → build/game.js (one IIFE, `node --check`), then bundle → ../KarateChampon-…html and ../exit-interview.json\n861\t  tests/run.mjs      THE test command (§18)\n862\t  tests/lib/*.mjs    loadGame (vm), fakeHub, autopilot helpers\n863\t  tests/*.test.mjs   suites\n864\t  tests/out/         screenshots, filmstrips (reviewed by eye only)\n865\t```\n866\t\n867\t### 15.2 Source files (each a fragment of one shared IIFE that starts `const E = My3D2dge`, `'use strict'`; the namespace is `const KC = {}`, exposed as `window.KC` for tests)\n868\t\n869\t| File | Responsibility |\n870\t|---|---|\n871\t| `00-header.js` | core-loop comment, version, `KC`, `TUNE_DEFAULTS`, live `TUNE` |\n872\t| `01-util.js` | rng streams, 4-way quantizer with hysteresis, easing, text sanitizer, number formatting |\n873\t| `02-data-techniques.js` | `KC.DATA.techniques` (§4.2) |\n874\t| `03-data-roster.js` | `KC.DATA.roster` (§7.1, §6.2), tips, voices, quotes, unlocks |\n875\t| `04-data-stages.js` | `KC.DATA.stages` (§7.2) |\n876\t| `05-data-ladder.js` | `KC.DATA.ladder`, `KC.DATA.lessons`, `KC.DATA.bonus` |\n877\t| `06-data-text.js` | every string (Appendix A), the call table in EN and JP, the hint pool |\n878\t| `07-validate.js` | `KC.validateData()`: ids resolve, numbers in range, every technique reachable in the matrix; runs at boot in dev and in tests |\n879\t| `10-input.js` | `KC.readControl(input, foeSide)`, the press-edge and upgrade window, P1/P2 key maps, `promptFor`, `KC.glyph` icon renderer, device tracking, touch-only gate |\n880\t| `11-poses.js` | karate poses, spins, flips, kime and the referee signals as rig post-processing or EC7 helpers |\n881\t| `20-voice.js` | `KC.Voice` synth, profiles, `speak(line, who) → seconds`, kiai, crowd |\n882\t| `21-music.js` | all songs and stings |\n883\t| `22-sfx.js` | `audio.define(...)` for every sfx in §11.2 |\n884\t| `30-karateka.js` | `KC.Karateka` (body, state machine, techniques, boxes, control, snapshot/apply, pose, draw) |\n885\t| `31-referee.js` | `KC.Referee` (actors, signals, calls queue, subtitles, judges, hantei votes) |\n886\t| `32-bout.js` | `KC.Bout` (§5.2 state machine, scoring, events, its own hit-stop, aiuchi window, stats) |\n887\t| `33-ai.js` | `KC.KarateAI` (perception, decisions, levels, personalities, showcase, habit tracker) |\n888\t| `34-hud.js` | fight HUD, popups, hints, input display, prompt bar |\n889\t| `35-stage.js` | stage painters (backdrop, floor, props, crowd, lights, weather, ambience), side and 2.5D |\n890\t| `36-crowd.js` | crowd silhouettes and front-row rigs, reactions, Mei cameo |\n891\t| `40-bonus-boards.js`, `41-bonus-pots.js`, `42-bonus-bull.js` | the three bonus stages (`KC.Bull` creature in 42) |\n892\t| `43-drill.js` | `KC.Drill` (Senpai's practice) and Free Spar |\n893\t| `50-scene-title.js` | title, main menu, touch gate, idle timer |\n894\t| `51-scene-attract.js` | `KC.Attract` director |\n895\t| `52-scene-road.js` | Champon Road screen, VS card, promotion, continue, game over, ending, credits, cutscene player |\n896\t| `53-scene-fight.js` | the `fight` scene for all modes (`data.mode`: story, versus, kachinuki, attract, freeSpar) |\n897\t| `54-scene-versus.js` | versus setup, `KC.Select`, stage select, results, local Kachinuki |\n898\t| `55-scene-misc.js` | dojo menu, bonus menu, records, about, move list, the `bonus` and `practice` scene wrappers |\n899\t| `60-online.js` | `KC.Net` (AIHub roles, card, messages, puppets, prediction, fairness delay) and `KC.Kachinuki` |\n900\t| `70-dev.js` | `KC.Dev` panel, overlays, Technique Lab scene, warp |\n901\t| `80-debug.js` | `game.debug` (§15.6), `game.stateHash` |\n902\t| `90-start.js` | `new E.Game(...)`, scene registry, boot steps, deep links, `game.start` |\n903\t\n904\t### 15.3 Data schemas (designers extend the game by editing these; systems never hard-code content)\n905\t- **technique:** `{ id, name, jp, input: [L, R], height: 'high'|'mid'|'low'|'none', f: [startup, active, recovery], travel: { x, phase }, air: { vz, vx } | null, reach: [min, max], pts: [lo, hi], unblockable, knockdown, closeAlt, closeGap, spin: 0 | .5 | 1, move: rigSpec, limb, hitR, kiai, bull, potHeights }`\n906\t- **fighter:** `{ id, name, title, rig, stats: { walk, reach, speed }, signature, ai: { level, aggression, patience, jump, block, gap, crouch, weights, habit }, voice: { f0, formant, rate, wave, rough, vib }, quotes: { intro, win, lose }, tip, unlock }`\n907\t- **stage:** `{ id, name, music, backdrop, floor: { mat, colors }, props: [[kind, id, x, y, z, opts]], lights: { ambient, list }, crowd: { rows, palette, front: [ids], mei }, weather, particles, ambience, arena: { x0: 0, x1: 400, marks: [150, 250] } }`\n908\t- **ladder node:** `{ kind: 'cutscene'|'practice'|'match'|'bonus'|'ending'|'credits', id, stage, opponent, lesson, bonus, promote, hints, script }`\n909\t- **lesson:** `{ id, title, stage, drills: [techId] }`. **bonus:** `{ id, kind: 'boards'|'pots'|'bull', stage, params }`.\n910\t- Adding an opponent, stage, lesson or bonus variant is a data edit plus `validateData()`. The Dev Warp tab lists any new nodes automatically.\n911\t\n912\t### 15.4 Engine changes (keep a record in `engine/CHANGES.md`; rerun `node kits/test.mjs fighting arcade` after each one)\n913\t- **EC1, right-stick codes.** `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` are pressed past 0.5 (on the dominant axis only, a 4-way gate) and released below 0.35. Their labels are 'R stick ↑' and so on, and they go in `FIXED_CODES`, like the left stick's `PadAxis*`. (Today the right stick exists only as `input.padAim` and cannot be bound or labelled; checked in `Input.tick`.)\n914\t- **EC2, standard-setting defaults.** `new E.Game({ defaults: { glyphs: 'playstation' } })` overrides a standard setting's default, and Restore defaults returns to it. (`STANDARD_SETTINGS` is internal and its glyphs default is `'auto'`.)\n915\t- **EC3, font glyphs.** Add ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 font (`G57`).\n916\t- **EC4, live pause.** With `game.live = true`, `_step` keeps running timers and the scene's `update` while paused or while a menu screen is open. `game.paused` is still set, so the scene neutralizes local input. Needed for shared-world sessions, where a guest's menu must not stop the world.\n917\t- **EC5, voice bus.** `audio.bus('sfx'|'music'|'voice')` returns the GainNode. Add a standard **Voices** volume slider in Sound, honoured by mute.\n918\t- **EC6, Backdrop custom layers.** `{ kind: 'custom', parallax, draw(g, r, scrollX, layer) }`.\n919\t- **EC7, karate poses and moves.** Add `bow`, `seiza`, `kamae`, `yoi` and the block poses to the Humanoid's pose table, and the `kc_*` move specs to `E.MOVES`. If a change inside the rig proves risky, do it game-side by wrapping `rig.solvePose` (allowed and documented).\n920\t- **No change is needed for:** `menu.items` as a function (already supported); `E.Arcade`'s idle time (pass `idle: 10` if it is used; our own `KC.Attract` owns the 10 s rule); `E.FightStage` and `E.Platformer` (reused as they are). **Do not use `E.Match`, `E.Fighter`, `E.FightAI` or `game.freeze`:** they are built around life bars (SF2 rules), and `freeze` stalls `update`.\n921\t\n922\t### 15.5 Game configuration\n923\t```js\n924\tconst HUB = typeof AIHub !== 'undefined' ? AIHub : null, SESSION = !!(HUB && HUB.active);\n925\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d', id: 'karate-champon',\n926\t  defaults: { glyphs: 'playstation' },                          // EC2\n927\t  autoPause: !SESSION,                                          // sessions never pause (EC4: game.live = SESSION)\n928\t  menu: { items: () => [...], settings: [...§8.10], controls: { tUp: 'Technique ↑ high', tDown: 'Technique ↓ low', tLeft: 'Technique ←', tRight: 'Technique →' }, quit: SESSION ? false : 'title' } });\n929\t```\n930\t- `AIHub` exists on the page because the session client defines it even outside sessions, but not in Node tests. That is why `HUB` is read through a `typeof` guard; tests inject a fake `AIHub` into the vm context before loading the game.\n931\t- Scenes set `view: 'side'`, `views: ['side', 'brawler']` (fight scenes only; the `camera` setting picks one) and `input: KC_KEYS`. They are `pausable: true` in gameplay (not in sessions) and set `camera: { zoom: false }` and `touch: []`.\n932\t- **Boot steps** (`game.start({ boot: { title: 'KARATE CHAMPON', steps } })`):\n933\t  1. \"Sweeping the dojo floor\" (`validateData`);\n934\t  2. \"Folding the gi\" (build the rigs);\n935\t  3. \"Simmering the broth\" (sfx and music);\n936\t  4. \"Tuning the referee's whistle\" (voice profiles);\n937\t  5. \"Raking the gravel\" (stage caches).\n938\t- **Deep links:** `#title`, `#road/<node>`, `#fight/<stage>/<opponent>`, `#versus`, `#kachinuki/<n>`, `#bonus/<id>`, `#practice/<lesson>`, `#lab/<techId>`, `#attract`, `#about`, `#ending`. These are for `tools/check.mjs` and `filmstrip.mjs`.\n939\t\n940\t### 15.6 `game.debug` (for tests and the engine's tools)\n941\t- `snapshot()` returns:\n942\t```js\n943\t{ scene, mode, node, rank, score, settings: { difficulty, calls },\n944\t  bout: { phase, clock, points, roundWins, round, lastCall, lastPoint, fighters: [{ id, x, z, state, tech, phase, facing }] },\n945\t  bonus: { kind, state, points, objectsLeft }, drill: { lesson, index, result },\n946\t  attract: { step, t }, online: { role, me, queue, standings }, dev: { on, tuneChanged } }\n947\t```\n948\t- Helpers: `warp(nodeOrScene)`, `setTune(k, v)`, `resetTune()`, `control(side, ctrl)` (inject an intent), `autopilot(side, level | false)`, `exportTuning()`, `forceCall(id)`.\n949\t- `game.stateHash = () => [...fighters' x, z, state, tech, the bout phase, clock, points]` for the engine's replay tool.\n950\t\n951\t### 15.7 Build and bundle\n952\t- `node tools/build.mjs`:\n953\t  1. Concatenate `src/*.js` into one IIFE in `build/game.js` and check it with `node --check`.\n954\t  2. Write `../KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743.html`:\n955\t     - `<meta charset>`;\n956\t     - `session.js` inline and **byte-identical** (the test compares its hash with `vendor/aihub/session.js`);\n957\t     - viewport, `<title>Karate Champon</title>`, the description and the hub metas;\n958\t     - `<style>`;\n959\t     - the `E.bootSplash('KARATE CHAMPON')` markup (generate it by loading the engine in a Node vm, or copy `game/index.html`'s `#e3-boot` block with the title changed);\n960\t     - `<canvas id=\"screen\">`;\n961\t     - then inline `<script>`s: the engine, the assets, the game.\n962\t  3. Escape `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n963\t  4. Write `../exit-interview.json` from `data/about.json` and validate it with `JSON.parse`.\n964\t- The final page makes **no external requests**. `tools/check.mjs` reports `external`, and the test asserts it is empty.\n965\t\n966\t---\n967\t\n968\t## 16. Build order and milestones\n969\tEach milestone ends with its tests green (`node tests/run.mjs --only <suites>`) and a playable build. Do them in order. Each depends on the ones before it, and the riskiest feel-defining work comes first.\n970\t\n971\t| M | Deliverable | Exit criteria |\n972\t|---|---|---|\n973\t| **M0 Setup** | dev folder; sparse engine copy (`git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` then `git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff`, ≈16 MB); `curl` the 4 hub files (never clone ai-hub: it is 5.3 GB); `npm install` and `npx playwright install chromium` in `engine/`; `build.mjs`; the `run.mjs` skeleton; `dev.html` with an empty title | `node kits/test.mjs fighting arcade` passes; `run.mjs` prints `0 failed` |\n974\t| **M1 Input and Karateka core** | EC1, EC3; `KC.readControl`, glyph icons; Karateka with walk, jump, crouch and the 4 standing techniques; joint boxes; the Technique Lab scene | `input.test` passes; the 4 techniques land in the Lab |\n975\t| **M2 Full moveset** | all 15 techniques plus block, flips, roll and pivot; EC7 poses; spins and flips; filmstrips reviewed | `technique.test` passes (reach ±4 u, height rules, block, counter, aiuchi) |\n976\t| **M3 Bout, referee and HUD** | `KC.Bout`, `KC.Referee` (text calls first), judges and hantei, HUD; the `fight` scene vs a dummy | `bout.test` passes; a quick bout plays start to finish in the browser |\n977\t| **M4 AI** | `KC.KarateAI`, personalities, levels, showcase, Autopilot | `ai.test` passes (completes, deterministic, level ordering, coverage) |\n978\t| **M5 Stages and art** | EC6; the 8 stages and 3 bonus locations; crowds; custom shapes and sprites; lights; the 2.5D view | screenshot review of every stage in both views; `tools/check.mjs` look notes addressed |\n979\t| **M6 Bonus and drills** | Boards, Pots, Bull (with the creature), `KC.Drill`, Free Spar | `bonus.test`, `drill.test` pass |\n980\t| **M7 Story and presentation** | title, main menu, Champon Road, VS cards, cutscenes, promotions, continue, game over, ending, credits, `E.Arcade` tables and initials, saves, unlocks, options, the About screen reading `data/about.json` (Source Information filled in now; the feedback answers are finalized at M12, and the shipped build has no placeholder text) | `story.test` (scripted full playthrough) passes |\n981\t| **M8 Audio** | EC5; `KC.Voice`; all music and sfx; crowd; wire them into the bus events | `audio.test` passes; listen pass in the browser |\n982\t| **M9 Local multiplayer** | versus setup, select, stage select, results, P2 keyboard/pad maps, local Kachinuki | `versus.test` passes |\n983\t| **M10 Online** | EC4; `KC.Net` with all roles; the card; puppets; prediction; fairness delay; Kachinuki; leave, time-up, end | `online.test` (fake hub) passes, then **the real harness passes** (2p; 4p with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; host-leave; server) |\n984\t| **M11 Attract, touch gate and dev** | `KC.Attract`, the touch-only gate, the full dev panel and warps | `attract.test`, `dev.test`, `ui.test` pass |\n985\t| **M12 Polish and ship** | juice and balance passes (Autopilot win-rate table), comfort, a final look review, About text plus `exit-interview.json`, the bundle, **a full manual playthrough**, a no-TODO scan | the full `node tests/run.mjs` prints `0 failed`; the bundle opens from `file://` with no errors; `grep -nE \"TODO|FIXME|XXX|placeholder\"` over `src/` finds nothing |\n986\t\n987\t---\n988\t\n989\t## 17. Riskiest parts and how to de-risk them\n990\t\n991\t| Risk | Why it is risky | De-risking (when) |\n992\t|---|---|---|\n993\t| **Karate animations** (spins, back kicks, somersaults, blocks) | `E.MOVES` has no karate-specific techniques; rig spins and airborne flips are unproven | Build the **Technique Lab in M1**, before any content. Prototype the Back Kick and the Front Somersault first. Spin by animating the 3D rig's facing angle. Fallbacks, in order: joint post-processing around the hips (Emberdeep), then a cut mocap clip. Review filmstrips for every technique |\n994\t| **Fair, readable hit detection** | Joint-derived boxes make outcomes depend on animation tuning | The explicit height rules (§4.3) on top of joint boxes; the Lab's **measured gap** vs the table (±4 u, asserted); dev box overlays; quality computed from data, not pixels |\n995\t| **Twin-stick on keyboard feels awkward** | Two-hand chords and keyboard rollover | The WASD + IJKL layout; the 4-frame posture upgrade window; the 0.15 s buffer; face buttons as an alternative; drills that teach it; input display. Test with real keyboards in M1–M3 |\n996\t| **Netplay feel under latency** | Host authority, the 20 msg/s cap, 150 ms latency in the hub test | Deliberate, slow techniques (the source's pace) forgive latency. Add the fairness delay and guest wind-up prediction. **Build the fake hub with latency, jitter and loss at the start of M10** and measure input-to-response time (target ≤ 250 ms at 150 ms one-way). Fallback: a uniform input delay for both sides |\n997\t| **AI quality and exploits** | The source's CPU was exploitable (a TAS found fixed reactions) | Seeded CPU-vs-CPU and Autopilot balance tables in `ai.test`; habit trackers at L8+; mistakes and reaction curves; live dev sliders |\n998\t| **Scope** | 9 matches, 11 locations, 3 bonus games, 3 lessons, story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract, dev, voices | Data-driven content (§15.3). One `Bout` and one `Karateka` power every mode (story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract, drills). A vertical slice is playable at M3 |\n999\t| **Hub harness conformance** | The card answers, server mode, time-up grace, traffic both ways, host-leave void | Follow `examples/arena.html`'s structure. Run the real harness headless with Playwright in the test command (§18.3) as soon as M10 starts, with a stub world |\n1000\t| **The engine's pause and hit-stop stop `update`** | That stalls networking and makes slow motion change send rates | EC4 live pause; the Bout's own hit-stop; real-time network cadence |\n1001\t| **Single-file bundling** | Inline scripts can contain `</script`; the session client must stay unmodified | The build escapes `</script` and a test compares the session client's bytes; the test loads the bundle from `file://` and checks errors |\n1002\t| **Environment** | `/tmp` here is a nearly full shared tmpfs (a clone failed with \"No space left on device\"); ports and processes are shared | Keep downloads and caches inside the dev folder; never clone ai-hub; serve with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from `vendor/aihub` + the bundle, parse the printed port, fetch the game URL and check for `<title>Karate Champon` before trusting a result, and kill only that PID |\n1003\t\n1004\t---\n1005\t\n1006\t## 18. Testing plan\n1007\t\n1008\t### 18.1 The one command\n1009\t`node tests/run.mjs` (from the dev folder) builds first, then runs every suite. It **prints only failing checks** (name and detail) and one final line:\n1010\t`KC TESTS: 412 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (38.2 s)`\n1011\t- `--only a,b` runs the named suites; `--no-browser` skips the Playwright suites.\n1012\t- **Rerun only the suites that cover code you changed**, then run the full command at each milestone's end and before shipping.\n1013\t- Each suite is `export default async (t) => { t.check(name, ok, detail) }`.\n1014\t- Headless suites load the engine, the assets and `build/game.js` in a Node `vm` context with stand-ins for `window`, `addEventListener` and `location` (as `kits/test.mjs` does). They drive the game with `game.advance(seconds)`, `input.tap` / `input.hold` / `input.press(code)` / `input.feed(...)`, and read state through `game.debug`. Fixed seeds everywhere.\n1015\t- Use screenshots and filmstrips **only for what a script cannot judge** (art, animation). Scripts check everything else.\n1016\t- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n1017\t\n1018\t### 18.2 Headless suites (what each checks)\n1019\t1. **`data`**: `validateData()` passes; every matrix cell maps to a defined technique or action; every stage, opponent, lesson, music and sfx id referenced exists.\n1020\t2. **`input`**:\n1021\t   - the 4-way quantizer (dominant axis, hysteresis, keyboard priority);\n1022\t   - all 20 matrix cells × both foe sides produce the expected technique id;\n1023\t   - the press edge (holding does not repeat);\n1024\t   - the 4-frame posture upgrade (R→ then L→ two frames later gives `midLunge`);\n1025\t   - the 0.15 s buffer;\n1026\t   - face buttons match the right stick; P1 and P2 split maps;\n1027\t   - `promptFor` labels: keyboard keycaps; pad with `glyphs=playstation` (✕ ○ □ △, R-stick icons); with `glyphs=xbox` (A B X Y);\n1028\t   - with EC1, the right stick past 0.5 presses `tUp` and so on, fed through `input.press('PadRAxisUp')`.\n1029\t3. **`technique`**:\n1030\t   - every technique runs from idle with the frame totals of §4.2 (in 120 Hz steps × `techSpeed`);\n1031\t   - travel distances; close substitutions (gap < 12, < 18);\n1032\t   - **measured contact gap within ±4 u of the table**, against a standing dummy, for every technique;\n1033\t   - height rules (high vs crouch misses, low vs air misses, `squatPunch` vs crouch hits, `upperPunch` vs air hits);\n1034\t   - block rules (high and mid blocked, low not; attacker recoil +10 frames; defender advantage 6);\n1035\t   - quality to points (golden cases: Round Kick at q = 1 gives 600 FULL, at q = 0.5 gives 500 HALF, at q = 0.15 gives 300 HALF; a clean Front Kick counter gives 400 HALF; a clean Back Kick counter gives 800 FULL; an Upper Lunge Punch at q = 0.15 still gives 600 FULL);\n1036\t   - aiuchi within 6 steps gives no point; spins end facing the foe; flips cross over at gap ≤ 70.\n1037\t4. **`bout`**:\n1038\t   - the full phase sequence with timings × `refPace`;\n1039\t   - the clock runs only in `fight`;\n1040\t   - points accumulate (½ and 1) up to `pointsToWin`; best of 3;\n1041\t   - time-out with a leader; time-out tie leads to hantei: 3 votes, a majority, deterministic per seed;\n1042\t   - time bonus; perfect bonus; the `reset` skip; the soft-lock guard; event bus order.\n1043\t5. **`ai`** (CPU vs CPU, seeded):\n1044\t   - every level and personality completes a match in under 6 simulated minutes;\n1045\t   - the same seed twice gives the same hash;\n1046\t   - the §6.3 win-rate and coverage targets over a seeded batch;\n1047\t   - reactions (at L8, a sweep thrown at it is answered by a jump in at least 50% of trials);\n1048\t   - showcase mode uses all 15 techniques within one exhibition.\n1049\t6. **`bonus`**:\n1050\t   - Boards: power → boards mapping, PERFECT at ≥ 95, overcharge;\n1051\t   - Pots: a deterministic sequence per visit; the right height breaks, the wrong one gets hit and ends; a boulder must be dodged; bowl catch +1,000 and break −500; PERFECT;\n1052\t   - Bull: each valid technique in the window stops it; an invalid one or early/late timing tramples; 3 charges; perfect window.\n1053\t7. **`drill`**: every drill accepts its technique and rejects others; SHARP within 50 frames; hints after 3 misses; skip; lesson totals.\n1054\t8. **`story`**, the **scripted playthrough of the whole game**:\n1055\t   - from the title, navigate the menus with real inputs into CHAMPON ROAD;\n1056\t   - with dev toggles (Autopilot L10 on P1, `cpuLevel` 1, `pointsToWin` 1), play **every ladder node in order**: prologue, lesson I, matches 1–2, boards, harbor, lesson II, bamboo, pots, log, lesson III, ship, bull, market, fuji, stadium, ending, credits, initials (three confirms), title;\n1057\t   - assert the rank and belt colour after each match, autosaves, unlock flags and score growth;\n1058\t   - force one loss to check CONTINUE (yes: a retry with the score reset) and a second loss followed by GAME OVER and initials;\n1059\t   - an **honest run** (no cheats, Autopilot L7 vs the normal ladder on Normal) must finish without soft-locks. Each node has a 10-simulated-minute cap; report the win rates.\n1060\t9. **`versus`**: P2 joins (pad and keyboard half); select (locked fighters cannot be picked; CPU level); stage select; rules applied; results menu; local Kachinuki with 4 inputs (rotation order, winner stays, loser to the back, first to 5).\n1061\t10. **`online`** (a fake hub: an in-process bus with a shared `AIHub.time()`, configurable latency, jitter, loss and the 20/s limiter; 2–4 game copies in separate `vm` contexts advanced in lockstep):\n1062\t    - connect → card → `ready()` with no key; Enter answers ready;\n1063\t    - start at time 0; inputs reach the host; snapshots reach guests;\n1064\t    - every copy agrees on bout winners and standings;\n1065\t    - Kachinuki rotation; a fighter leaving mid-bout means forfeit; under 2 players means finish;\n1066\t    - time-up → `finish()` within grace with valid standings;\n1067\t    - server role (no local player) runs and sends;\n1068\t    - after `end`, no sends and the music stops;\n1069\t    - message size ≤ 16 KB and rate ≤ 20/s per copy;\n1070\t    - the guest's predicted wind-up is confirmed or rolled back within 250 ms;\n1071\t    - with `AIHub.active` false, `connect` returns null and the title appears.\n1072\t11. **`attract`**: 10 s idle on the title, the main menu and a sub-menu each starts the attract (and 9.9 s does not); key, pad, mouse move and click exit to the title; the loop visits bout, bonus reel, how-to, scores and title in order; the exhibition shows the required calls (half, full, aiuchi, hantei).\n1073\t12. **`dev`**: the pause menu has Dev mode; every slider changes its live variable and affects play (for example, `hitstop` changes the freeze steps, `fullAt` changes the half/full outcome); RESET ALL restores defaults; the toggles work (P1 invincible blocks CPU points, boxes overlay flags); warps reach every node; high scores are not recorded while tuned.\n1074\t13. **`ui`**:\n1075\t    - menus by keyboard and pad, and by mouse hover and click via `input.feed`;\n1076\t    - the prompt bar follows `lastDevice`; PlayStation is the default glyph family and Xbox works;\n1077\t    - Restore defaults goes back to PlayStation;\n1078\t    - the **touch-only gate**: with stubbed `matchMedia` and `maxTouchPoints`, the panel shows, touch controls are off, the attract runs, and a key press lifts the gate;\n1079\t    - the About pages hold every TASK field with no empty values.\n1080\t14. **`audio`**: every song parses with no engine warnings (capture `console.warn`); every sfx id is defined; voice plans are deterministic per line; durations drive dialog timing; call syllable patterns are fixed.\n1081\t15. **`smoke`**: every scene and stage (including both views and every bonus) runs for 5 simulated seconds with `game.errors` empty.\n1082\t\n1083\t### 18.3 Browser suites (Playwright, the engine's pinned 1.63.0)\n1084\t1. **`page`**: `node engine/tools/check.mjs <bundle>.html` over `file://`. No errors, the external request list is empty, the snapshot is printed, and look notes are saved to `tests/out` for review.\n1085\t2. **`scenes`**: screenshots of every deep link (each stage in both views, each bonus, the Lab, the title, the card) saved to `tests/out/` **for eye review only**. The script fails only on errors or a blank frame (filled < 0.3).\n1086\t3. **`filmstrips`**: `tools/filmstrip.mjs <bundle>.html#lab/<id> --seed 1` for each technique, block, flip, the referee signals and the bull, into `tests/out/strips/`. Review them by eye at M2, M5 and M12.\n1087\t4. **`hub`**: start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` in a temp dir inside the dev folder holding `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and the bundle. Read the port, check that the game URL serves `Karate Champon`, then open:\n1088\t   - `harness.html?game=<bundle>&players=2&auto=1`\n1089\t   - `…&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n1090\t   - the same with `&scenario=host-leave`\n1091\t   - `…&players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`\n1092\t\n1093\t   Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and require `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, printing the failing checks' names and details. Kill the server by its PID.\n1094\t5. **`session-bytes`**: the inline session client is byte-identical to `vendor/aihub/session.js` and is the first `<script>` after `<meta charset>`.\n1095\t\n1096\t### 18.4 What only a person can judge (do it by eye, with screenshots and filmstrips)\n1097\tAnimation quality, stage art quality (compare with the Look target), how readable the referee calls are, glyph legibility at TV distance (view the screenshots at 25% scale), and voice pleasantness.\n1098\t\n1099\t---\n1100\t\n1101\t## 19. Hand-off checklist (the build's final step)\n1102\t\n1103\t### 19.1 Definition of done (from TASK.md)\n1104\t- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features (grep `src/` and the bundle).\n1105\t- [ ] The single file `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743.html` sits beside PLAN.md and opens from `file://`. The dev folder holds everything else.\n1106\t- [ ] Every requirement in the traceability table (§19.4) is implemented and its test passes.\n1107\t- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs` prints `0 failed`. The hub harness passes all four runs.\n1108\t- [ ] The About screen, the chat reply and `exit-interview.json` contain the same Source Information and feedback answers.\n1109\t\n1110\t### 19.2 About screen, chat reply and exit interview (one source: `data/about.json`)\n1111\t- **Source Information** (the builder fills this in honestly; write \"unknown\" for anything unconfirmed):\n1112\t\n1113\t| Field | Value |\n1114\t|---|---|\n1115\t| Date | 2026-11-03 17:47:43 |\n1116\t| Source | *Karate Champ* (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as *Karate Dō*) |\n1117\t| Plan | `PLAN.md` |\n1118\t| Genre and Perspective | 1v1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages; 2D side view (optional 2.5D tatami view) |\n1119\t| Tech | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: list the ECs), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node with Playwright 1.63.0 |\n1120\t| Art Direction | the engine's HD rigs as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240 |\n1121\t| Animation | the engine's procedural Humanoid rig plus new karate techniques, poses and the bull rig |\n1122\t| Audio | code-generated chip music and sfx, plus the gibberish voice synth |\n1123\t| Online | AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, 2–4 players) plus local versus |\n1124\t| Testing | headless `game.advance` suites, a scripted full playthrough, Playwright checks and the hub harness |\n1125\t| Research | the sources in §1.1, plus the builder's own |\n1126\t| Environment | Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown |\n1127\t| Model | the builder's own model and effort, as TASK.md lists them |\n1128\t| Usage | estimates only if available, else \"unknown\" |\n1129\t\n1130\t- **Testing, Engine and Prompt feedback:** answer every question in TASK.md, in order, from real experience. The engine answers should name the ECs and why each was needed.\n1131\t- **`exit-interview.json`:** exactly TASK.md's shape. Check it with `JSON.parse`. The suggested `aiHub` block:\n1132\t```json\n1133\t{ \"name\": \"Karate Champon\", \"tagline\": \"Two sticks. One clean strike.\",\n1134\t  \"description\": \"A point-sparring karate tournament inspired by Karate Champ: land one clean technique and let the referee call it. Climb from white belt to Champ, break boards, smash flying pots and stop a charging bull.\",\n1135\t  \"type\": \"Game\", \"genre\": \"Fighting\", \"source\": \"Karate Champ (1984)\", \"series\": \"\", \"players\": \"1–4\",\n1136\t  \"controls\": \"Left stick/WASD: move, jump, crouch · Right stick, face buttons or IJKL: techniques · Options/Esc: pause\",\n1137\t  \"tags\": [\"2d\", \"arcade\", \"versus\", \"retro\", \"pixel art\", \"comedy\"] }\n1138\t```\n1139\t\n1140\t### 19.3 Operational rules (from TASK.md; repeated here because they are easy to miss)\n1141\t- Read only what you need. Never read other runs' folders in the parent directories.\n1142\t- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. When a server is needed (the hub harness), bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, confirm the page is ours, and stop only the PIDs you started. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n1143\t- Performance is out of scope. Add detail, lights and effects freely.\n1144\t\n1145\t### 19.4 Requirements traceability\n1146\t\n1147\t| TASK.md requirement | Plan section | Proven by |\n1148\t|---|---|---|\n1149\t| Genre and perspective picked | §0, §2 | `smoke`, screenshots |\n1150\t| Title riff (50 brainstormed, RNG pick) | header | — (done) |\n1151\t| Research | §1.1 | About screen |\n1152\t| Single HTML file, dev subfolder, bundle | §15.1, §15.7 | `page`, `session-bytes` |\n1153\t| Built on the engine, changes allowed | §15.4 | `kits/test.mjs` after each EC |\n1154\t| Art direction (native style plus new assets) | §9 | scene screenshots |\n1155\t| Animation (native rig plus new animations) | §10 | `technique`, filmstrips |\n1156\t| Audio in code; gibberish voices | §11 | `audio` |\n1157\t| Online via AI Hub, plus multiplayer on its own | §12 | `online`, `hub`, `versus` |\n1158\t| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic | §3–§8 | `story` (full playthrough) |\n1159\t| Distinctive source traits → mechanics and UX | §1.2 | `technique`, `bout`, `bonus`, `drill` |\n1160\t| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | §14 | `dev` |\n1161\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | §13 | `attract` |\n1162\t| Narrative tone like the source | §1.3, Appendix A | review |\n1163\t| 10-foot, controller-first UX; native keyboard and mouse | §3, §8 | `ui`, `input` |\n1164\t| Prompts follow the last device; PS glyphs default; Xbox option | §3.3 | `ui`, `input` |\n1165\t| No dense web-style screens | §3.4, §8.6 | review |\n1166\t| Touch-only: gate message, settings button, attract runs | §3.5 | `ui`, `attract` |\n1167\t| Testing: headless, full playthrough, one command, failures only | §18 | `run.mjs` output |\n1168\t| Source Information and feedback in the reply and the About screen | §19.2 | `ui` (About fields) |\n1169\t| `exit-interview.json`, valid JSON | §19.2 | build step plus `JSON.parse` |\n1170\t| No TODOs or placeholders | §19.1 | grep check in M12 |\n1171\t\n1172\t---\n1173\t\n1174\t## Appendix A: Text (terse, ALL CAPS; voices are gibberish, these are the subtitles)\n1175\t\n1176\t**Prologue**\n1177\t1. Card: \"NAGASAKI HARBOR. SPRING 1984.\"\n1178\t2. Card: \"THE CHAMPON CUP RETURNS.\"\n1179\t3. Card: \"THE PRIZE: THE GOLDEN BOWL.\"\n1180\t4. TARO: \"I AM TARO MOYASHI. WHITE BELT.\"\n1181\t5. TARO: \"I WILL BE CHAMP!\"\n1182\t6. NEGI: \"FIRST, YOU LEARN TO KICK.\"\n1183\t\n1184\t**Lessons**\n1185\t- NEGI: \"WATCH. THEN DO.\" Drill prompts: \"WATCH.\" / \"NOW YOU.\" / \"GOOD!\" / \"SHARP!\" / \"NO. WATCH AGAIN.\"\n1186\t- End: \"LESSON COMPLETE.\" NEGI: \"YOU ARE READY. FIGHT KAMABOKO.\"\n1187\t- Lesson II: NEGI: \"STEP IN. STRIKE THROUGH.\"\n1188\t- Lesson III: NEGI: \"TURN YOUR BACK. NEVER YOUR EYES.\"\n1189\t\n1190\t**Mei**\n1191\t- Harbor:\n1192\t  - MEI: \"YOU ARE THE NEW ONE? YOU LOOK LIKE A BEAN SPROUT.\"\n1193\t  - TARO: \"…I AM A BEAN SPROUT.\"\n1194\t  - MEI: \"EAT. THEN FIGHT.\"\n1195\t- After the harbor: MEI: \"NOT BAD, MOYASHI.\"\n1196\t- Pots intro: MEI: \"DON'T BREAK MY BOWLS! BLOCK TO CATCH!\"\n1197\t- Market: MEI: \"THE FINAL IS TOMORROW. DON'T BE LATE.\"\n1198\t\n1199\t**Opponent taunts:** in §7.1. **Wins:** TARO: \"OSU!\" **Losses:** TARO: \"AGAIN…\"\n1200\t\n1201\t**Final entrance**\n1202\t1. GENKOTSU: \"THE FINAL. THE CHAMPION ENTERS.\"\n1203\t2. MEI: \"SURPRISE, MOYASHI. THE GOLDEN BOWL IS MINE.\"\n1204\t3. TARO: \"…YOU?\"\n1205\t4. MEI: \"WHO DO YOU THINK MAKES THE BEST CHAMPON?\"\n1206\t\n1207\t**Ending**\n1208\t1. GENKOTSU: \"WHITE WINS! A NEW CHAMP!\"\n1209\t2. MEI: \"A BEAN SPROUT BECAME A CHAMP.\"\n1210\t3. MEI: \"NEXT YEAR I TAKE IT BACK.\"\n1211\t4. TARO: \"OSU!\"\n1212\t5. Card: \"YOU ARE THE KARATE CHAMPON.\"\n1213\t\n1214\t**Bonus stages**\n1215\t- \"BONUS STAGE! BREAK THE BOARDS!\" / \"BONUS STAGE! POTS AHOY!\" / \"BONUS STAGE! STOP THE BULL!\"\n1216\t- Results: \"PERFECT!\" / \"WEAK!\" / \"NICE CATCH!\" / \"MOTTAINAI!\" / \"TRAMPLED!\"\n1217\t\n1218\t**Promotion:** \"PROMOTED! YELLOW BELT\" (and green, blue, brown; \"BLACK BELT 1ST DAN\", \"3RD DAN\", \"5TH DAN\", \"8TH DAN\", \"CHAMP\")\n1219\t\n1220\t**Continue and game over:** \"CONTINUE?\"; MEI: \"GET UP, MOYASHI!\"; \"GAME OVER\"; GENKOTSU: \"TRAIN. RETURN.\"\n1221\t\n1222\t**Hints** (story matches 1–3)\n1223\t- \"THE REFEREE STOPS THE FIGHT AFTER EVERY CLEAN HIT.\"\n1224\t- \"FIRST TO 2 POINTS WINS THE ROUND.\"\n1225\t- \"CLEAN, FULL-REACH STRIKES SCORE FULL POINTS.\"\n1226\t- \"HIT THEM DURING THEIR TECHNIQUE: DOUBLE POINTS!\"\n1227\t- \"LOW KICKS AND SWEEPS CAN'T BE BLOCKED. JUMP THEM.\"\n1228\t- \"CROUCH UNDER HIGH KICKS.\"\n1229\t\n1230\t**Tips** (after 2 lost rounds)\n1231\t- EBI: \"EBI JUMPS A LOT. UPPER PUNCH CATCHES JUMPERS.\"\n1232\t- KIKURAGE: \"KIKURAGE BLOCKS MIDS. GO LOW.\"\n1233\t- IKA: \"IKA FLIPS OVER YOU. TURN AND STRIKE.\"\n1234\t- BUTA: \"BUTA LUNGES FROM FAR. BLOCK, THEN COUNTER.\"\n1235\t- ASARI: \"ASARI CROUCHES. JUMPING KICKS HIT HER.\"\n1236\t- KYABETSU: \"KYABETSU PUNISHES MISSES. BE PATIENT.\"\n1237\t- MEI: \"MEI WATCHES YOUR HABITS. MIX HIGH AND LOW.\"\n1238\t\n1239\t**Online card**\n1240\t- \"KACHINUKI! WINNER STAYS ON.\"\n1241\t- \"FIRST TO 2 POINTS WINS A BOUT. MOST BOUTS WINS.\"\n1242\t- \"READY\" / \"MORE TIME\"\n1243\t- \"NEXT: YOU\" / \"HOST LEFT\" / \"ROUND OVER\"\n1244\t\n1245\t**Touch gate:** \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"\n1246\t\n1247\t## Appendix B: Default input preset (`KC_KEYS`, single player)\n1248\t```js"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92d1ba00-1a0f-4edd-bbd5-53c74c3ffc62","uuid":"55b59f8f-b4ea-4ff0-a45c-e9329adcc83c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:40:25.567Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n### 15.1 Folder layout (all dev files inside the dev folder; only the final HTML and `exit-interview.json` sit beside PLAN.md)\n```\nKarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743/\n  engine/            sparse copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (my-3d2dge.js, modules/, tools/, kits/, docs/, package.json) + CHANGES.md (every engine edit and why)\n  vendor/aihub/      session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only)\n  assets/            kc-set.js (cut store set) + kc-shapes.js (our E.SHAPES) + kc-sprites.js\n  src/               game source fragments, concatenated in name order into one IIFE (§15.2)\n  data/about.json    the About-screen text = the exit interview (§19)\n  dev.html           dev page: session.js first in <head>, engine, assets, build/game.js\n  build/game.js      generated\n  tools/build.mjs    src → build/game.js (one IIFE, `node --check`), then bundle → ../KarateChampon-…html and ../exit-interview.json\n  tests/run.mjs      THE test command (§18)\n  tests/lib/*.mjs    loadGame (vm), fakeHub, autopilot helpers\n  tests/*.test.mjs   suites\n  tests/out/         screenshots, filmstrips (reviewed by eye only)\n```\n\n### 15.2 Source files (each a fragment of one shared IIFE that starts `const E = My3D2dge`, `'use strict'`; the namespace is `const KC = {}`, exposed as `window.KC` for tests)\n\n| File | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `00-header.js` | core-loop comment, version, `KC`, `TUNE_DEFAULTS`, live `TUNE` |\n| `01-util.js` | rng streams, 4-way quantizer with hysteresis, easing, text sanitizer, number formatting |\n| `02-data-techniques.js` | `KC.DATA.techniques` (§4.2) |\n| `03-data-roster.js` | `KC.DATA.roster` (§7.1, §6.2), tips, voices, quotes, unlocks |\n| `04-data-stages.js` | `KC.DATA.stages` (§7.2) |\n| `05-data-ladder.js` | `KC.DATA.ladder`, `KC.DATA.lessons`, `KC.DATA.bonus` |\n| `06-data-text.js` | every string (Appendix A), the call table in EN and JP, the hint pool |\n| `07-validate.js` | `KC.validateData()`: ids resolve, numbers in range, every technique reachable in the matrix; runs at boot in dev and in tests |\n| `10-input.js` | `KC.readControl(input, foeSide)`, the press-edge and upgrade window, P1/P2 key maps, `promptFor`, `KC.glyph` icon renderer, device tracking, touch-only gate |\n| `11-poses.js` | karate poses, spins, flips, kime and the referee signals as rig post-processing or EC7 helpers |\n| `20-voice.js` | `KC.Voice` synth, profiles, `speak(line, who) → seconds`, kiai, crowd |\n| `21-music.js` | all songs and stings |\n| `22-sfx.js` | `audio.define(...)` for every sfx in §11.2 |\n| `30-karateka.js` | `KC.Karateka` (body, state machine, techniques, boxes, control, snapshot/apply, pose, draw) |\n| `31-referee.js` | `KC.Referee` (actors, signals, calls queue, subtitles, judges, hantei votes) |\n| `32-bout.js` | `KC.Bout` (§5.2 state machine, scoring, events, its own hit-stop, aiuchi window, stats) |\n| `33-ai.js` | `KC.KarateAI` (perception, decisions, levels, personalities, showcase, habit tracker) |\n| `34-hud.js` | fight HUD, popups, hints, input display, prompt bar |\n| `35-stage.js` | stage painters (backdrop, floor, props, crowd, lights, weather, ambience), side and 2.5D |\n| `36-crowd.js` | crowd silhouettes and front-row rigs, reactions, Mei cameo |\n| `40-bonus-boards.js`, `41-bonus-pots.js`, `42-bonus-bull.js` | the three bonus stages (`KC.Bull` creature in 42) |\n| `43-drill.js` | `KC.Drill` (Senpai's practice) and Free Spar |\n| `50-scene-title.js` | title, main menu, touch gate, idle timer |\n| `51-scene-attract.js` | `KC.Attract` director |\n| `52-scene-road.js` | Champon Road screen, VS card, promotion, continue, game over, ending, credits, cutscene player |\n| `53-scene-fight.js` | the `fight` scene for all modes (`data.mode`: story, versus, kachinuki, attract, freeSpar) |\n| `54-scene-versus.js` | versus setup, `KC.Select`, stage select, results, local Kachinuki |\n| `55-scene-misc.js` | dojo menu, bonus menu, records, about, move list, the `bonus` and `practice` scene wrappers |\n| `60-online.js` | `KC.Net` (AIHub roles, card, messages, puppets, prediction, fairness delay) and `KC.Kachinuki` |\n| `70-dev.js` | `KC.Dev` panel, overlays, Technique Lab scene, warp |\n| `80-debug.js` | `game.debug` (§15.6), `game.stateHash` |\n| `90-start.js` | `new E.Game(...)`, scene registry, boot steps, deep links, `game.start` |\n\n### 15.3 Data schemas (designers extend the game by editing these; systems never hard-code content)\n- **technique:** `{ id, name, jp, input: [L, R], height: 'high'|'mid'|'low'|'none', f: [startup, active, recovery], travel: { x, phase }, air: { vz, vx } | null, reach: [min, max], pts: [lo, hi], unblockable, knockdown, closeAlt, closeGap, spin: 0 | .5 | 1, move: rigSpec, limb, hitR, kiai, bull, potHeights }`\n- **fighter:** `{ id, name, title, rig, stats: { walk, reach, speed }, signature, ai: { level, aggression, patience, jump, block, gap, crouch, weights, habit }, voice: { f0, formant, rate, wave, rough, vib }, quotes: { intro, win, lose }, tip, unlock }`\n- **stage:** `{ id, name, music, backdrop, floor: { mat, colors }, props: [[kind, id, x, y, z, opts]], lights: { ambient, list }, crowd: { rows, palette, front: [ids], mei }, weather, particles, ambience, arena: { x0: 0, x1: 400, marks: [150, 250] } }`\n- **ladder node:** `{ kind: 'cutscene'|'practice'|'match'|'bonus'|'ending'|'credits', id, stage, opponent, lesson, bonus, promote, hints, script }`\n- **lesson:** `{ id, title, stage, drills: [techId] }`. **bonus:** `{ id, kind: 'boards'|'pots'|'bull', stage, params }`.\n- Adding an opponent, stage, lesson or bonus variant is a data edit plus `validateData()`. The Dev Warp tab lists any new nodes automatically.\n\n### 15.4 Engine changes (keep a record in `engine/CHANGES.md`; rerun `node kits/test.mjs fighting arcade` after each one)\n- **EC1, right-stick codes.** `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` are pressed past 0.5 (on the dominant axis only, a 4-way gate) and released below 0.35. Their labels are 'R stick ↑' and so on, and they go in `FIXED_CODES`, like the left stick's `PadAxis*`. (Today the right stick exists only as `input.padAim` and cannot be bound or labelled; checked in `Input.tick`.)\n- **EC2, standard-setting defaults.** `new E.Game({ defaults: { glyphs: 'playstation' } })` overrides a standard setting's default, and Restore defaults returns to it. (`STANDARD_SETTINGS` is internal and its glyphs default is `'auto'`.)\n- **EC3, font glyphs.** Add ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 font (`G57`).\n- **EC4, live pause.** With `game.live = true`, `_step` keeps running timers and the scene's `update` while paused or while a menu screen is open. `game.paused` is still set, so the scene neutralizes local input. Needed for shared-world sessions, where a guest's menu must not stop the world.\n- **EC5, voice bus.** `audio.bus('sfx'|'music'|'voice')` returns the GainNode. Add a standard **Voices** volume slider in Sound, honoured by mute.\n- **EC6, Backdrop custom layers.** `{ kind: 'custom', parallax, draw(g, r, scrollX, layer) }`.\n- **EC7, karate poses and moves.** Add `bow`, `seiza`, `kamae`, `yoi` and the block poses to the Humanoid's pose table, and the `kc_*` move specs to `E.MOVES`. If a change inside the rig proves risky, do it game-side by wrapping `rig.solvePose` (allowed and documented).\n- **No change is needed for:** `menu.items` as a function (already supported); `E.Arcade`'s idle time (pass `idle: 10` if it is used; our own `KC.Attract` owns the 10 s rule); `E.FightStage` and `E.Platformer` (reused as they are). **Do not use `E.Match`, `E.Fighter`, `E.FightAI` or `game.freeze`:** they are built around life bars (SF2 rules), and `freeze` stalls `update`.\n\n### 15.5 Game configuration\n```js\nconst HUB = typeof AIHub !== 'undefined' ? AIHub : null, SESSION = !!(HUB && HUB.active);\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d', id: 'karate-champon',\n  defaults: { glyphs: 'playstation' },                          // EC2\n  autoPause: !SESSION,                                          // sessions never pause (EC4: game.live = SESSION)\n  menu: { items: () => [...], settings: [...§8.10], controls: { tUp: 'Technique ↑ high', tDown: 'Technique ↓ low', tLeft: 'Technique ←', tRight: 'Technique →' }, quit: SESSION ? false : 'title' } });\n```\n- `AIHub` exists on the page because the session client defines it even outside sessions, but not in Node tests. That is why `HUB` is read through a `typeof` guard; tests inject a fake `AIHub` into the vm context before loading the game.\n- Scenes set `view: 'side'`, `views: ['side', 'brawler']` (fight scenes only; the `camera` setting picks one) and `input: KC_KEYS`. They are `pausable: true` in gameplay (not in sessions) and set `camera: { zoom: false }` and `touch: []`.\n- **Boot steps** (`game.start({ boot: { title: 'KARATE CHAMPON', steps } })`):\n  1. \"Sweeping the dojo floor\" (`validateData`);\n  2. \"Folding the gi\" (build the rigs);\n  3. \"Simmering the broth\" (sfx and music);\n  4. \"Tuning the referee's whistle\" (voice profiles);\n  5. \"Raking the gravel\" (stage caches).\n- **Deep links:** `#title`, `#road/<node>`, `#fight/<stage>/<opponent>`, `#versus`, `#kachinuki/<n>`, `#bonus/<id>`, `#practice/<lesson>`, `#lab/<techId>`, `#attract`, `#about`, `#ending`. These are for `tools/check.mjs` and `filmstrip.mjs`.\n\n### 15.6 `game.debug` (for tests and the engine's tools)\n- `snapshot()` returns:\n```js\n{ scene, mode, node, rank, score, settings: { difficulty, calls },\n  bout: { phase, clock, points, roundWins, round, lastCall, lastPoint, fighters: [{ id, x, z, state, tech, phase, facing }] },\n  bonus: { kind, state, points, objectsLeft }, drill: { lesson, index, result },\n  attract: { step, t }, online: { role, me, queue, standings }, dev: { on, tuneChanged } }\n```\n- Helpers: `warp(nodeOrScene)`, `setTune(k, v)`, `resetTune()`, `control(side, ctrl)` (inject an intent), `autopilot(side, level | false)`, `exportTuning()`, `forceCall(id)`.\n- `game.stateHash = () => [...fighters' x, z, state, tech, the bout phase, clock, points]` for the engine's replay tool.\n\n### 15.7 Build and bundle\n- `node tools/build.mjs`:\n  1. Concatenate `src/*.js` into one IIFE in `build/game.js` and check it with `node --check`.\n  2. Write `../KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743.html`:\n     - `<meta charset>`;\n     - `session.js` inline and **byte-identical** (the test compares its hash with `vendor/aihub/session.js`);\n     - viewport, `<title>Karate Champon</title>`, the description and the hub metas;\n     - `<style>`;\n     - the `E.bootSplash('KARATE CHAMPON')` markup (generate it by loading the engine in a Node vm, or copy `game/index.html`'s `#e3-boot` block with the title changed);\n     - `<canvas id=\"screen\">`;\n     - then inline `<script>`s: the engine, the assets, the game.\n  3. Escape `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n  4. Write `../exit-interview.json` from `data/about.json` and validate it with `JSON.parse`.\n- The final page makes **no external requests**. `tools/check.mjs` reports `external`, and the test asserts it is empty.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Build order and milestones\nEach milestone ends with its tests green (`node tests/run.mjs --only <suites>`) and a playable build. Do them in order. Each depends on the ones before it, and the riskiest feel-defining work comes first.\n\n| M | Deliverable | Exit criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| **M0 Setup** | dev folder; sparse engine copy (`git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` then `git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff`, ≈16 MB); `curl` the 4 hub files (never clone ai-hub: it is 5.3 GB); `npm install` and `npx playwright install chromium` in `engine/`; `build.mjs`; the `run.mjs` skeleton; `dev.html` with an empty title | `node kits/test.mjs fighting arcade` passes; `run.mjs` prints `0 failed` |\n| **M1 Input and Karateka core** | EC1, EC3; `KC.readControl`, glyph icons; Karateka with walk, jump, crouch and the 4 standing techniques; joint boxes; the Technique Lab scene | `input.test` passes; the 4 techniques land in the Lab |\n| **M2 Full moveset** | all 15 techniques plus block, flips, roll and pivot; EC7 poses; spins and flips; filmstrips reviewed | `technique.test` passes (reach ±4 u, height rules, block, counter, aiuchi) |\n| **M3 Bout, referee and HUD** | `KC.Bout`, `KC.Referee` (text calls first), judges and hantei, HUD; the `fight` scene vs a dummy | `bout.test` passes; a quick bout plays start to finish in the browser |\n| **M4 AI** | `KC.KarateAI`, personalities, levels, showcase, Autopilot | `ai.test` passes (completes, deterministic, level ordering, coverage) |\n| **M5 Stages and art** | EC6; the 8 stages and 3 bonus locations; crowds; custom shapes and sprites; lights; the 2.5D view | screenshot review of every stage in both views; `tools/check.mjs` look notes addressed |\n| **M6 Bonus and drills** | Boards, Pots, Bull (with the creature), `KC.Drill`, Free Spar | `bonus.test`, `drill.test` pass |\n| **M7 Story and presentation** | title, main menu, Champon Road, VS cards, cutscenes, promotions, continue, game over, ending, credits, `E.Arcade` tables and initials, saves, unlocks, options, the About screen reading `data/about.json` (Source Information filled in now; the feedback answers are finalized at M12, and the shipped build has no placeholder text) | `story.test` (scripted full playthrough) passes |\n| **M8 Audio** | EC5; `KC.Voice`; all music and sfx; crowd; wire them into the bus events | `audio.test` passes; listen pass in the browser |\n| **M9 Local multiplayer** | versus setup, select, stage select, results, P2 keyboard/pad maps, local Kachinuki | `versus.test` passes |\n| **M10 Online** | EC4; `KC.Net` with all roles; the card; puppets; prediction; fairness delay; Kachinuki; leave, time-up, end | `online.test` (fake hub) passes, then **the real harness passes** (2p; 4p with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; host-leave; server) |\n| **M11 Attract, touch gate and dev** | `KC.Attract`, the touch-only gate, the full dev panel and warps | `attract.test`, `dev.test`, `ui.test` pass |\n| **M12 Polish and ship** | juice and balance passes (Autopilot win-rate table), comfort, a final look review, About text plus `exit-interview.json`, the bundle, **a full manual playthrough**, a no-TODO scan | the full `node tests/run.mjs` prints `0 failed`; the bundle opens from `file://` with no errors; `grep -nE \"TODO|FIXME|XXX|placeholder\"` over `src/` finds nothing |\n\n---\n\n## 17. Riskiest parts and how to de-risk them\n\n| Risk | Why it is risky | De-risking (when) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Karate animations** (spins, back kicks, somersaults, blocks) | `E.MOVES` has no karate-specific techniques; rig spins and airborne flips are unproven | Build the **Technique Lab in M1**, before any content. Prototype the Back Kick and the Front Somersault first. Spin by animating the 3D rig's facing angle. Fallbacks, in order: joint post-processing around the hips (Emberdeep), then a cut mocap clip. Review filmstrips for every technique |\n| **Fair, readable hit detection** | Joint-derived boxes make outcomes depend on animation tuning | The explicit height rules (§4.3) on top of joint boxes; the Lab's **measured gap** vs the table (±4 u, asserted); dev box overlays; quality computed from data, not pixels |\n| **Twin-stick on keyboard feels awkward** | Two-hand chords and keyboard rollover | The WASD + IJKL layout; the 4-frame posture upgrade window; the 0.15 s buffer; face buttons as an alternative; drills that teach it; input display. Test with real keyboards in M1–M3 |\n| **Netplay feel under latency** | Host authority, the 20 msg/s cap, 150 ms latency in the hub test | Deliberate, slow techniques (the source's pace) forgive latency. Add the fairness delay and guest wind-up prediction. **Build the fake hub with latency, jitter and loss at the start of M10** and measure input-to-response time (target ≤ 250 ms at 150 ms one-way). Fallback: a uniform input delay for both sides |\n| **AI quality and exploits** | The source's CPU was exploitable (a TAS found fixed reactions) | Seeded CPU-vs-CPU and Autopilot balance tables in `ai.test`; habit trackers at L8+; mistakes and reaction curves; live dev sliders |\n| **Scope** | 9 matches, 11 locations, 3 bonus games, 3 lessons, story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract, dev, voices | Data-driven content (§15.3). One `Bout` and one `Karateka` power every mode (story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract, drills). A vertical slice is playable at M3 |\n| **Hub harness conformance** | The card answers, server mode, time-up grace, traffic both ways, host-leave void | Follow `examples/arena.html`'s structure. Run the real harness headless with Playwright in the test command (§18.3) as soon as M10 starts, with a stub world |\n| **The engine's pause and hit-stop stop `update`** | That stalls networking and makes slow motion change send rates | EC4 live pause; the Bout's own hit-stop; real-time network cadence |\n| **Single-file bundling** | Inline scripts can contain `</script`; the session client must stay unmodified | The build escapes `</script` and a test compares the session client's bytes; the test loads the bundle from `file://` and checks errors |\n| **Environment** | `/tmp` here is a nearly full shared tmpfs (a clone failed with \"No space left on device\"); ports and processes are shared | Keep downloads and caches inside the dev folder; never clone ai-hub; serve with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from `vendor/aihub` + the bundle, parse the printed port, fetch the game URL and check for `<title>Karate Champon` before trusting a result, and kill only that PID |\n\n---\n\n## 18. Testing plan\n\n### 18.1 The one command\n`node tests/run.mjs` (from the dev folder) builds first, then runs every suite. It **prints only failing checks** (name and detail) and one final line:\n`KC TESTS: 412 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (38.2 s)`\n- `--only a,b` runs the named suites; `--no-browser` skips the Playwright suites.\n- **Rerun only the suites that cover code you changed**, then run the full command at each milestone's end and before shipping.\n- Each suite is `export default async (t) => { t.check(name, ok, detail) }`.\n- Headless suites load the engine, the assets and `build/game.js` in a Node `vm` context with stand-ins for `window`, `addEventListener` and `location` (as `kits/test.mjs` does). They drive the game with `game.advance(seconds)`, `input.tap` / `input.hold` / `input.press(code)` / `input.feed(...)`, and read state through `game.debug`. Fixed seeds everywhere.\n- Use screenshots and filmstrips **only for what a script cannot judge** (art, animation). Scripts check everything else.\n- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\n### 18.2 Headless suites (what each checks)\n1. **`data`**: `validateData()` passes; every matrix cell maps to a defined technique or action; every stage, opponent, lesson, music and sfx id referenced exists.\n2. **`input`**:\n   - the 4-way quantizer (dominant axis, hysteresis, keyboard priority);\n   - all 20 matrix cells × both foe sides produce the expected technique id;\n   - the press edge (holding does not repeat);\n   - the 4-frame posture upgrade (R→ then L→ two frames later gives `midLunge`);\n   - the 0.15 s buffer;\n   - face buttons match the right stick; P1 and P2 split maps;\n   - `promptFor` labels: keyboard keycaps; pad with `glyphs=playstation` (✕ ○ □ △, R-stick icons); with `glyphs=xbox` (A B X Y);\n   - with EC1, the right stick past 0.5 presses `tUp` and so on, fed through `input.press('PadRAxisUp')`.\n3. **`technique`**:\n   - every technique runs from idle with the frame totals of §4.2 (in 120 Hz steps × `techSpeed`);\n   - travel distances; close substitutions (gap < 12, < 18);\n   - **measured contact gap within ±4 u of the table**, against a standing dummy, for every technique;\n   - height rules (high vs crouch misses, low vs air misses, `squatPunch` vs crouch hits, `upperPunch` vs air hits);\n   - block rules (high and mid blocked, low not; attacker recoil +10 frames; defender advantage 6);\n   - quality to points (golden cases: Round Kick at q = 1 gives 600 FULL, at q = 0.5 gives 500 HALF, at q = 0.15 gives 300 HALF; a clean Front Kick counter gives 400 HALF; a clean Back Kick counter gives 800 FULL; an Upper Lunge Punch at q = 0.15 still gives 600 FULL);\n   - aiuchi within 6 steps gives no point; spins end facing the foe; flips cross over at gap ≤ 70.\n4. **`bout`**:\n   - the full phase sequence with timings × `refPace`;\n   - the clock runs only in `fight`;\n   - points accumulate (½ and 1) up to `pointsToWin`; best of 3;\n   - time-out with a leader; time-out tie leads to hantei: 3 votes, a majority, deterministic per seed;\n   - time bonus; perfect bonus; the `reset` skip; the soft-lock guard; event bus order.\n5. **`ai`** (CPU vs CPU, seeded):\n   - every level and personality completes a match in under 6 simulated minutes;\n   - the same seed twice gives the same hash;\n   - the §6.3 win-rate and coverage targets over a seeded batch;\n   - reactions (at L8, a sweep thrown at it is answered by a jump in at least 50% of trials);\n   - showcase mode uses all 15 techniques within one exhibition.\n6. **`bonus`**:\n   - Boards: power → boards mapping, PERFECT at ≥ 95, overcharge;\n   - Pots: a deterministic sequence per visit; the right height breaks, the wrong one gets hit and ends; a boulder must be dodged; bowl catch +1,000 and break −500; PERFECT;\n   - Bull: each valid technique in the window stops it; an invalid one or early/late timing tramples; 3 charges; perfect window.\n7. **`drill`**: every drill accepts its technique and rejects others; SHARP within 50 frames; hints after 3 misses; skip; lesson totals.\n8. **`story`**, the **scripted playthrough of the whole game**:\n   - from the title, navigate the menus with real inputs into CHAMPON ROAD;\n   - with dev toggles (Autopilot L10 on P1, `cpuLevel` 1, `pointsToWin` 1), play **every ladder node in order**: prologue, lesson I, matches 1–2, boards, harbor, lesson II, bamboo, pots, log, lesson III, ship, bull, market, fuji, stadium, ending, credits, initials (three confirms), title;\n   - assert the rank and belt colour after each match, autosaves, unlock flags and score growth;\n   - force one loss to check CONTINUE (yes: a retry with the score reset) and a second loss followed by GAME OVER and initials;\n   - an **honest run** (no cheats, Autopilot L7 vs the normal ladder on Normal) must finish without soft-locks. Each node has a 10-simulated-minute cap; report the win rates.\n9. **`versus`**: P2 joins (pad and keyboard half); select (locked fighters cannot be picked; CPU level); stage select; rules applied; results menu; local Kachinuki with 4 inputs (rotation order, winner stays, loser to the back, first to 5).\n10. **`online`** (a fake hub: an in-process bus with a shared `AIHub.time()`, configurable latency, jitter, loss and the 20/s limiter; 2–4 game copies in separate `vm` contexts advanced in lockstep):\n    - connect → card → `ready()` with no key; Enter answers ready;\n    - start at time 0; inputs reach the host; snapshots reach guests;\n    - every copy agrees on bout winners and standings;\n    - Kachinuki rotation; a fighter leaving mid-bout means forfeit; under 2 players means finish;\n    - time-up → `finish()` within grace with valid standings;\n    - server role (no local player) runs and sends;\n    - after `end`, no sends and the music stops;\n    - message size ≤ 16 KB and rate ≤ 20/s per copy;\n    - the guest's predicted wind-up is confirmed or rolled back within 250 ms;\n    - with `AIHub.active` false, `connect` returns null and the title appears.\n11. **`attract`**: 10 s idle on the title, the main menu and a sub-menu each starts the attract (and 9.9 s does not); key, pad, mouse move and click exit to the title; the loop visits bout, bonus reel, how-to, scores and title in order; the exhibition shows the required calls (half, full, aiuchi, hantei).\n12. **`dev`**: the pause menu has Dev mode; every slider changes its live variable and affects play (for example, `hitstop` changes the freeze steps, `fullAt` changes the half/full outcome); RESET ALL restores defaults; the toggles work (P1 invincible blocks CPU points, boxes overlay flags); warps reach every node; high scores are not recorded while tuned.\n13. **`ui`**:\n    - menus by keyboard and pad, and by mouse hover and click via `input.feed`;\n    - the prompt bar follows `lastDevice`; PlayStation is the default glyph family and Xbox works;\n    - Restore defaults goes back to PlayStation;\n    - the **touch-only gate**: with stubbed `matchMedia` and `maxTouchPoints`, the panel shows, touch controls are off, the attract runs, and a key press lifts the gate;\n    - the About pages hold every TASK field with no empty values.\n14. **`audio`**: every song parses with no engine warnings (capture `console.warn`); every sfx id is defined; voice plans are deterministic per line; durations drive dialog timing; call syllable patterns are fixed.\n15. **`smoke`**: every scene and stage (including both views and every bonus) runs for 5 simulated seconds with `game.errors` empty.\n\n### 18.3 Browser suites (Playwright, the engine's pinned 1.63.0)\n1. **`page`**: `node engine/tools/check.mjs <bundle>.html` over `file://`. No errors, the external request list is empty, the snapshot is printed, and look notes are saved to `tests/out` for review.\n2. **`scenes`**: screenshots of every deep link (each stage in both views, each bonus, the Lab, the title, the card) saved to `tests/out/` **for eye review only**. The script fails only on errors or a blank frame (filled < 0.3).\n3. **`filmstrips`**: `tools/filmstrip.mjs <bundle>.html#lab/<id> --seed 1` for each technique, block, flip, the referee signals and the bull, into `tests/out/strips/`. Review them by eye at M2, M5 and M12.\n4. **`hub`**: start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` in a temp dir inside the dev folder holding `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and the bundle. Read the port, check that the game URL serves `Karate Champon`, then open:\n   - `harness.html?game=<bundle>&players=2&auto=1`\n   - `…&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n   - the same with `&scenario=host-leave`\n   - `…&players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`\n\n   Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and require `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, printing the failing checks' names and details. Kill the server by its PID.\n5. **`session-bytes`**: the inline session client is byte-identical to `vendor/aihub/session.js` and is the first `<script>` after `<meta charset>`.\n\n### 18.4 What only a person can judge (do it by eye, with screenshots and filmstrips)\nAnimation quality, stage art quality (compare with the Look target), how readable the referee calls are, glyph legibility at TV distance (view the screenshots at 25% scale), and voice pleasantness.\n\n---\n\n## 19. Hand-off checklist (the build's final step)\n\n### 19.1 Definition of done (from TASK.md)\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features (grep `src/` and the bundle).\n- [ ] The single file `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174743.html` sits beside PLAN.md and opens from `file://`. The dev folder holds everything else.\n- [ ] Every requirement in the traceability table (§19.4) is implemented and its test passes.\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs` prints `0 failed`. The hub harness passes all four runs.\n- [ ] The About screen, the chat reply and `exit-interview.json` contain the same Source Information and feedback answers.\n\n### 19.2 About screen, chat reply and exit interview (one source: `data/about.json`)\n- **Source Information** (the builder fills this in honestly; write \"unknown\" for anything unconfirmed):\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Date | 2026-11-03 17:47:43 |\n| Source | *Karate Champ* (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as *Karate Dō*) |\n| Plan | `PLAN.md` |\n| Genre and Perspective | 1v1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages; 2D side view (optional 2.5D tatami view) |\n| Tech | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: list the ECs), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node with Playwright 1.63.0 |\n| Art Direction | the engine's HD rigs as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240 |\n| Animation | the engine's procedural Humanoid rig plus new karate techniques, poses and the bull rig |\n| Audio | code-generated chip music and sfx, plus the gibberish voice synth |\n| Online | AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, 2–4 players) plus local versus |\n| Testing | headless `game.advance` suites, a scripted full playthrough, Playwright checks and the hub harness |\n| Research | the sources in §1.1, plus the builder's own |\n| Environment | Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown |\n| Model | the builder's own model and effort, as TASK.md lists them |\n| Usage | estimates only if available, else \"unknown\" |\n\n- **Testing, Engine and Prompt feedback:** answer every question in TASK.md, in order, from real experience. The engine answers should name the ECs and why each was needed.\n- **`exit-interview.json`:** exactly TASK.md's shape. Check it with `JSON.parse`. The suggested `aiHub` block:\n```json\n{ \"name\": \"Karate Champon\", \"tagline\": \"Two sticks. One clean strike.\",\n  \"description\": \"A point-sparring karate tournament inspired by Karate Champ: land one clean technique and let the referee call it. Climb from white belt to Champ, break boards, smash flying pots and stop a charging bull.\",\n  \"type\": \"Game\", \"genre\": \"Fighting\", \"source\": \"Karate Champ (1984)\", \"series\": \"\", \"players\": \"1–4\",\n  \"controls\": \"Left stick/WASD: move, jump, crouch · Right stick, face buttons or IJKL: techniques · Options/Esc: pause\",\n  \"tags\": [\"2d\", \"arcade\", \"versus\", \"retro\", \"pixel art\", \"comedy\"] }\n```\n\n### 19.3 Operational rules (from TASK.md; repeated here because they are easy to miss)\n- Read only what you need. Never read other runs' folders in the parent directories.\n- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. When a server is needed (the hub harness), bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, confirm the page is ours, and stop only the PIDs you started. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n- Performance is out of scope. Add detail, lights and effects freely.\n\n### 19.4 Requirements traceability\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Plan section | Proven by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective picked | §0, §2 | `smoke`, screenshots |\n| Title riff (50 brainstormed, RNG pick) | header | — (done) |\n| Research | §1.1 | About screen |\n| Single HTML file, dev subfolder, bundle | §15.1, §15.7 | `page`, `session-bytes` |\n| Built on the engine, changes allowed | §15.4 | `kits/test.mjs` after each EC |\n| Art direction (native style plus new assets) | §9 | scene screenshots |\n| Animation (native rig plus new animations) | §10 | `technique`, filmstrips |\n| Audio in code; gibberish voices | §11 | `audio` |\n| Online via AI Hub, plus multiplayer on its own | §12 | `online`, `hub`, `versus` |\n| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic | §3–§8 | `story` (full playthrough) |\n| Distinctive source traits → mechanics and UX | §1.2 | `technique`, `bout`, `bonus`, `drill` |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | §14 | `dev` |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | §13 | `attract` |\n| Narrative tone like the source | §1.3, Appendix A | review |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UX; native keyboard and mouse | §3, §8 | `ui`, `input` |\n| Prompts follow the last device; PS glyphs default; Xbox option | §3.3 | `ui`, `input` |\n| No dense web-style screens | §3.4, §8.6 | review |\n| Touch-only: gate message, settings button, attract runs | §3.5 | `ui`, `attract` |\n| Testing: headless, full playthrough, one command, failures only | §18 | `run.mjs` output |\n| Source Information and feedback in the reply and the About screen | §19.2 | `ui` (About fields) |\n| `exit-interview.json`, valid JSON | §19.2 | build step plus `JSON.parse` |\n| No TODOs or placeholders | §19.1 | grep check in M12 |\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A: Text (terse, ALL CAPS; voices are gibberish, these are the subtitles)\n\n**Prologue**\n1. Card: \"NAGASAKI HARBOR. SPRING 1984.\"\n2. Card: \"THE CHAMPON CUP RETURNS.\"\n3. Card: \"THE PRIZE: THE GOLDEN BOWL.\"\n4. TARO: \"I AM TARO MOYASHI. WHITE BELT.\"\n5. TARO: \"I WILL BE CHAMP!\"\n6. NEGI: \"FIRST, YOU LEARN TO KICK.\"\n\n**Lessons**\n- NEGI: \"WATCH. THEN DO.\" Drill prompts: \"WATCH.\" / \"NOW YOU.\" / \"GOOD!\" / \"SHARP!\" / \"NO. WATCH AGAIN.\"\n- End: \"LESSON COMPLETE.\" NEGI: \"YOU ARE READY. FIGHT KAMABOKO.\"\n- Lesson II: NEGI: \"STEP IN. STRIKE THROUGH.\"\n- Lesson III: NEGI: \"TURN YOUR BACK. NEVER YOUR EYES.\"\n\n**Mei**\n- Harbor:\n  - MEI: \"YOU ARE THE NEW ONE? YOU LOOK LIKE A BEAN SPROUT.\"\n  - TARO: \"…I AM A BEAN SPROUT.\"\n  - MEI: \"EAT. THEN FIGHT.\"\n- After the harbor: MEI: \"NOT BAD, MOYASHI.\"\n- Pots intro: MEI: \"DON'T BREAK MY BOWLS! BLOCK TO CATCH!\"\n- Market: MEI: \"THE FINAL IS TOMORROW. DON'T BE LATE.\"\n\n**Opponent taunts:** in §7.1. **Wins:** TARO: \"OSU!\" **Losses:** TARO: \"AGAIN…\"\n\n**Final entrance**\n1. GENKOTSU: \"THE FINAL. THE CHAMPION ENTERS.\"\n2. MEI: \"SURPRISE, MOYASHI. THE GOLDEN BOWL IS MINE.\"\n3. TARO: \"…YOU?\"\n4. MEI: \"WHO DO YOU THINK MAKES THE BEST CHAMPON?\"\n\n**Ending**\n1. GENKOTSU: \"WHITE WINS! A NEW CHAMP!\"\n2. MEI: \"A BEAN SPROUT BECAME A CHAMP.\"\n3. MEI: \"NEXT YEAR I TAKE IT BACK.\"\n4. TARO: \"OSU!\"\n5. Card: \"YOU ARE THE KARATE CHAMPON.\"\n\n**Bonus stages**\n- \"BONUS STAGE! BREAK THE BOARDS!\" / \"BONUS STAGE! POTS AHOY!\" / \"BONUS STAGE! STOP THE BULL!\"\n- Results: \"PERFECT!\" / \"WEAK!\" / \"NICE CATCH!\" / \"MOTTAINAI!\" / \"TRAMPLED!\"\n\n**Promotion:** \"PROMOTED! YELLOW BELT\" (and green, blue, brown; \"BLACK BELT 1ST DAN\", \"3RD DAN\", \"5TH DAN\", \"8TH DAN\", \"CHAMP\")\n\n**Continue and game over:** \"CONTINUE?\"; MEI: \"GET UP, MOYASHI!\"; \"GAME OVER\"; GENKOTSU: \"TRAIN. RETURN.\"\n\n**Hints** (story matches 1–3)\n- \"THE REFEREE STOPS THE FIGHT AFTER EVERY CLEAN HIT.\"\n- \"FIRST TO 2 POINTS WINS THE ROUND.\"\n- \"CLEAN, FULL-REACH STRIKES SCORE FULL POINTS.\"\n- \"HIT THEM DURING THEIR TECHNIQUE: DOUBLE POINTS!\"\n- \"LOW KICKS AND SWEEPS CAN'T BE BLOCKED. JUMP THEM.\"\n- \"CROUCH UNDER HIGH KICKS.\"\n\n**Tips** (after 2 lost rounds)\n- EBI: \"EBI JUMPS A LOT. UPPER PUNCH CATCHES JUMPERS.\"\n- KIKURAGE: \"KIKURAGE BLOCKS MIDS. GO LOW.\"\n- IKA: \"IKA FLIPS OVER YOU. TURN AND STRIKE.\"\n- BUTA: \"BUTA LUNGES FROM FAR. BLOCK, THEN COUNTER.\"\n- ASARI: \"ASARI CROUCHES. JUMPING KICKS HIT HER.\"\n- KYABETSU: \"KYABETSU PUNISHES MISSES. BE PATIENT.\"\n- MEI: \"MEI WATCHES YOUR HABITS. MIX HIGH AND LOW.\"\n\n**Online card**\n- \"KACHINUKI! WINNER STAYS ON.\"\n- \"FIRST TO 2 POINTS WINS A BOUT. MOST BOUTS WINS.\"\n- \"READY\" / \"MORE TIME\"\n- \"NEXT: YOU\" / \"HOST LEFT\" / \"ROUND OVER\"\n\n**Touch gate:** \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"\n\n## Appendix B: Default input preset (`KC_KEYS`, single player)\n```js","numLines":400,"startLine":849,"totalLines":1258}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XxDNe9WpYm5M1hGT4HaJb4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
